      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A DUCK of a man generally makes a goose of a husband. It's a cold day when a henpecked man is not in hot water. .</p>
        <p>SordE men will take more time to name a dog 'than others would spend in building a house. A GRAM OFFENCE--selling 140 eggs for twelve dozen. A grocer offence—putting sand in sugar. WHY do men always speak of their wives as their "better halves ? " Simply because they " half " to.</p>
        <p>A WRITER says "poetry is unvoiced music." That substantiates our belief that the tomcat sings in prose. THE surest of governing, both in prirate family and kingdom, is for a husband or prince sometimes to drop their prerogatives. THEY are making the new styles of collars so high that before long young men will ha ,e to stand on tiptoe to see anything at all. DOAN like de man what passes me in de road widout speo.kin'. De dog do.t won't neither growl nur wag his tail ain't no a'rthly er count. QUEER, isn't it? A man who will swallow any kind of a dish with an imposing French name will be scared to death if he catches a cold with aGreek or Latin title.</p>
        <p>FRANK showed the picture on his slate. "It's awful bad," said teasing Kate; "just like the small-pox." "Why ?" asked he. "Because it's sketching, don't you see ?" CusromF,R—" Have you some good imported cigars ?" New shopman—" Not just now; but we will have in about an hour. The printer around the corner is at work on the labels now."</p>
        <p>"1 FEAI:," said Dr. Jalap, " that you have got some forci:zn substance in this prescription." .Possibly," replied the apothecary; "bat look at your prescription. It is foreign all through." rich, it is easy enough to conceal our wealth ; but if poor, it is not quite so easy to conceal poverty. We shall find that it is less difficult to hide a thousand guineas than one hole in our coat. POLICE SEILGEANT (surprising his subordinate, who should be on duty)—" Here, here; Oi'll have none o' that." Subordinate (devouring the last znorsel)—" Be Jabers, Oi don't think yez will !" A LADY of about thirty years of age married a wealthy old bachelor of Dallas, Texas. A female friend asked her.:—" But tell me. Why didn't you marry your husband ten years ago ?" " Well, you see, ten years ago he was too old for me." WilY should people never be frightened at the appearance of a ghost? BeCause, if it is a ghost, its appearance must, in the nature of things, be immaterial.</p>
        <p>An DEALER—" Yes, that was painted by one of the masters. But, I beg your pardon, sir, you must not touch it with your umbrella." Flake Whyte—" What's the matter? Ain't it dry yet ?" To tell what a man says, pay attention to the tongue. To ascertain what he means, pay attention to the eye. To talk in opposition to the heart is one of the easiest things in the world ' • to look this opposition is more difficult than algebra. I DON'T wonder that people talk of the good old times, said the president of the gas company. At one time it was dark for three days and three nights in the land of Egypt. What a big thing it would be for the gas companies if we could have something like that in these days. "HERE, waiter, bring me a plate of corned beef bash." "No co'n'd-beef hash to-night, sah." "But it's on the bill of fare." " Yaas, sah, we mos' allus has co'n'd-beef hash, but, ye see, cab, -all our kiinnah customalis was specially bongry to-day, sah." "What's that got to do with it?' "Weal, ye see, sab, dey done ate up all de co'n'd-beef on their plates.—Chicago News. - -</p>
        <p>A GENTLEMAN who has recently returned from a 'Emir in the United States says :—" I was amused by an idea given me by an American in office. I asked how much his situation was worth ; he -answered six hundred dollars besides stea/ings. This was frank and honest. In England the word would have softened down to perquisites. I after- wards found that it was a common expression in the States to say a place was worth so much .besides cheatage." A NI ARSZILLES MERCHANT, lately deceased, who .started in business with a thousand pounds, ho- Caine a millionaire. Ile left his property to a friend, with the condition that he should Le buried with the sum of one thousand pounds placed in his coffin. The executor bewa.Ved this reckless waste of money, and was at his end how to defeat the whimsical clause in the will. At length happy thought come. "I will put a cheque," he said, " for a thousand into the coffin."</p>
        <p>A MI NT-ciTETZ of the name of Spas, whose pas- toi.;ate lay in the North, was thef idler o 2 13 chil- &amp;cu. At the baptism of the thirteenth, an aged brother divine, di,sirous to choose what seer:ie.-1 to him an appropriate description of the life of man, called on the congregation to join in singing the fifth. paraphrase, beginning with the line, "As sparks in close succession rise." So unconscious vas he that be could not understand the people!s titter until, when he descended from the pulpit, his puL was explained to him. " RErECCA, you shall nod spheak mit dot Moses 'Levi vonce more." " Oh, fadder, you preak mine heardt. Ve vos almost engaged. Vy shall I not spheak of him ?" "He haf sheated me. He haf -sheated me. He haf sold me a paste diamond for shenuine shtone.." " Oh, fadder, dot shouldt recommendt him to you as a son-in-law. If he can fool a wise man like you, see vat a fortune he half in de t chewelry piziness." "Ve Rebecca, you vas schmarder as I thought. Get Ifidsried ven you like. lam ankschious to go into bartnership with mine son-in-law !" -</p>
        <p>.--.AN Austin lady, who had been to Galveston for several weeks, returned one day last week. She ,heard from the neighbours that her coloured cook lad been entertaining the Reverend Whangdoodle Baxter during the I,,dy's absence. "Matilda," aid the lady very sternly, "I have told you repeatedly that you must not have auy man visiting the house during my absence. That most not occur again ; do you understand? " "Ob course, hit shan't occur no moah. I tlidn't know dat you wanted de gemmans ter visit me while you was at tome. You never told me (lilt lx:foah."</p>
        <p>A RIM priest had hoarded a fine collection of jewels, to which he was constantly adding, and of which he was inordinately proud. Upon showing them to a friend, the latter feasted his eyes for some time, and on taking his leave thanked his host for the jewels. "How 1" cried the priest ; 6' I have not given them to you ! Why do you thank me " Well," rejoined his friend, " I have at least had as much pleasure from seeing them as you can have, and the only difference between us that I can discover is that you have the trouble of watching them."—Translated from the Chinese.</p>
        <p>Ati old lady read a paragraph in .one of the Jpaper9 the other day, describing how a grindstone in a sawmill and killed four men. She hap- pened to remember that there was a small grind- stone down in her cellar, leaning against the wall; so she went out and got an accident insurance policy, and then, summoning her servant, and holding a pie-board in front of her face, so that if the thing exploded her face would not be injured, tin.d the stone taken out into the road, where 24 pails of water was thrown over it, and a stick was stuck in a Hole bearing a placard marked "Dan- gerous." She says it is a mercy the whole house %vas not blown to pieces by the thing before this.— Wood and Iron. LAWYER: "You say you make an examination of the premises. What did you find ?" Witness: "Oh, nothing of consequence—a 'beggarly account of empty boxes,' as Shakespeare says !" Lawyer: "Never mind what Shakespeare said. He will be summoned and testify for himself, if he knows any- thing about the case. THE bear and the wolf had been warm friends for many years when the wolf one da7 asked for e. loan to help him out of a tight squeeze. Lo his titter astonishment the favour was promptly refused. "Haven't we been friends " asked the 'wolf. V Truly we have." "And don't you wish to be in the future ?" "It is for that reason," itnswered the bear, "that I refuse the loan. 11 you ha,ve no trouble in repaying me you will depead upon me in all future emergencies. If you fail to repay, you will become my enemy for iife." Moral—Go to your friends for sympathy— to your pawnbroker for loans. PitorEson—" A.ll, Miss Reform, you are quite right ! But the truth is, I am sorry to say, that you Are 20 years ahead of your age." Miss Reform hastily—" Dear me, professor, do you mean to say that I look as if I were 60 ?"</p>
