      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>EMPLOYERS' LIABILITY.</p>
        <p>ITS EVOLUTION.</p>
        <p>Brave, noble, and resolute has, writes" John Ball," in the "Sun," been the workers' fight for more than twenty years to scotch the great industrial " Juggernaut." Prior to 1872, without let or hindrance, it claimed its annual army of helpless victims. No one raised a voice against the employers' free license to slaughter their toilers with dangerous machinery, employment of boy labour and careless, incompetent foremen. It was all accepted as so much of the inevitable " waste" in the great industrial race. Then a still small voice was heard saying," Why should not the workers receive the same legal protection as the public ? " The next three years saw a great growth of opinion, especially among railway men, for whose special benefit the Bill was being chiefly promoted. At the Glasgow Trades Congress in 1875, the delegates in the full blush of pleasure on the concession of the "Labour Laws," expressed their determination to place the law of em- ployers' liability in the very forefront of their demands. A new Bill was framed and placed in the hands of Alexander Macdonaid, the "friend of the Miners." A year of hard ,earnest agitation up and down the country followed. The result was immediate. The second read- ing of Mr. Macdonald's Bill was reached in May of 1876, and Mr.(now Viscount Cross),the Tory Home Secretary, was compelled to admit the unsatisfactory state of the law, and at once granted the appointment of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>SELECT COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>A SELECT COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>to deal with the Bill. Mr. A. J. Mundella and Mr. J. Shaw-Lefevre were appointed so the committee, which was overwhelmingly representative of employers. A great deal of interesting evidence was taken, and though the committee did not finish their inquiry by the end of the year, they early discovered their strong em- ployer bias in the examination of witnesses. This was remarked upon at the Trades Union Congress of that year, when Mr. John Burnett (now Labour Correspon- dent of the Board of Traae), just through with his leadership of the nine hours movement, made a great speech urging the Parliamentary Committee to remain firmly by Mr. Macdonald's 13111. Then came from one of the delegates a significant note of warning, as with almost prophetic instinct. He expressed the opinion that the Select Committee intended to recommend a permis- sive instead of a compulsory Bill. He moved a resolution protesting against such a prospective measure. The idea struck the Congress as being so " absurd and farcical," and so unlikely to happen, that they thought such a resolution unnecessary. Even such experienced leaders as Mr. Burnett, Mr. George Shipton and Mr. H. Broad- hurst all urged its utter needlessness. The committee reported, and sure enough the Government brought in</p>
        <p>A PERMISSIVE BILL, which was in every way unsatisfactory. Mr. Macdonald again introduced his Bill which,besides preventing "con- tracting out," sought to completely abolish the iniquit- ous doctrine of common employment. Lord Brassey introduced a sort of half way measure abolishing the plea of "common employment" in the case of persons exer- cising any power of control or superintendence. But Lord Brass.ey ultimately withdrew this Bill and agreed to support that of Mr. Macdonald. The Government measure was introduced in 1879,and, miserable, halting measure though it was, raiscd a perfect storm of indig- nation among employers. Indeed,so great was the pres- sure, that the Bill was withdrawn in the following August. It may be interesting to many to kni-w that one ai the drier instruments in bringing about this end was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PRESENT LORD SHAND,</head>
        <p>THE PRESENT LORD SHAND,</p>
        <p>who has just been appointed chairman of the C 151 Con- ciliation Board. At that timP a judge, he had just been raised from the position of an advocate, where he !sad had a large practice from the Scotch miners. His action was so pronounced that serious complaints were made against him at the time. Though a judge he attended the empl.yers meetings,and it was alle,ged "wirepolled" day alter day at the House of Commons to get the Bill withdrawn. In 1880 the Government reintroduced their Bill, this time into the House of Lords, who pro- posed to refer the matter to a select committee. When this decision became known, the Parliamentary Cqm- mittee of the Trades Union Congress petitioned the Gov- ernment</p>
        <p>TO APPOINT LORD ROEBEItY, as a sympathetic lord, on the .committee, so that the labour interests might be vc-,tched. But before the com- mittee was formed, Disraeli was defeated, and the disso- ;ution came with a return of the Liberals to power.. Mr. Gladstono at once introduced a Bill limiting the doctrine of common employment to workmen actually engaged in toil alongside each other, and allowing the recovery of three years wages for loss of life or total disablement. The Lords lent their whole energies to the mutilaaion of this measure; arid returned it to the Commons in a seri- ously maimed condition. The Government, however, remained I)erfectly firm, and returned the Bill in its original form to the " Gilded Chamber," through which it passed the following night. The workmen at once made it perfectly clear that they accepted the Bill merely as ;in instalment, and would not rest satisfied until " contracting out" was rigidly prohibited. Resolutions on these lines were passed throughout the United King- dom. Availing themselves of the permissive character ot the melsure, scores of firms at once c.ritracted their workmen out of he law. Insurance funds sprung up</p>
        <p>like mushrooms in a night. Builders introduced hun- dreds of men of straw as " sub-contractors," to prevent workmen coming upon them for damages, and ail the tricks and subterfuges of the law were resorted to to make the Act inoperative. Mr. Thomas Burt, M.P., was charged by the Trades Union Congress with the duty of bringing in a Bill TO ABOLISII " CONTRACTING OUT,"</p>
        <p>and to othe-r-wis—e-;;;;dy--the Act. Six Ciousand peti- tions were presented to the House in its favour. But the election of 1885 was reached without anything being done. In March of 1386, another Select Committee was appointed to deal with the subject. The vital point upon which the fight now hung was that of " contract- ing out." Employers moved heaven and earth t') escape liability under the Act. The late Sir George Findley, the L. and N. W. manager, examined as a witness. frankly adu.itted that the company only contributed to t heir insurance society to tie th, hands of their employes. ,SAid</p>
        <p>'Ol course the large contribution which the cotnpany haq made has been to secure the men in perfect accord with the directors, and to prevent anything like disrup- tion or disturbance or litigation between the men an d themselves. They have been willing voluntarily to pay a large sum to get that important object attained." In flite of these damaging admi,sions,aud the preponderat- ing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WORKING-CLASS OPINION</head>
        <p>WORKING-CLASS OPINION</p>
        <p>against the principle of " contracting out," the Com- mittee report(d in favour of it, very large:y, I think, on the strength of the late Mr. Bradlaugh's representations. Then came the 1886 election and the accession of the Tories to power. Mr. Henry Matthews brought in a measure not only sanctioning "contracting out," but also introducing an objectionable system of insurance besides. After a couple oi years' fight, and in conse- quence of the pressure from the unions, the Government were compelled to abandon their Bill, and nothing further was done to stop the progress of the industrial Juggernaut until Mr. Asquith's excellent measure and the Lords' wicked mutilation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>STRANGE DISEASE AT ASITTON-IN</head>
        <p>A STRANGE DISEASE AT ASITTON-IN</p>
        <p>MAKERFIELD.</p>
        <p>IZEN The Medical Officer of the Local Government Board has just issued an interesting and valuable report nvlde by Dr. S. W. Wheaton as the result of an inquiry con- ducted by him into the origin of an epidemic of "fever" that has recently occurred in the district of Ashton-in- Makerfield, near Wigan. It seems that the number of cases of "fever," certified in most cases to be typhoid, has been growing with alarming rapidity during the past two years, and an inquiry was consequently ordered. The district is described as one in which there is not a single empty house, and in which the over- crowding of the population goes on unchecked. Dr. Wheaton found that the disease described as " fever " and frequently AS " typhoid " was in reality a type not readily classified, for it partook of the characteristics of influenza, of typhoid, and of typhus. The problems he had to solve concerned its real nature and its origin. The former he pronounced to be mainly typhoid but sometimes typhus, and he attributes its spread to the custom prevalent in the locality " for people to crowd into a house in which sickness is present,and to sit there in the presence of the patient. Be says that " the dis- ease had selected the most insanitary portions of the dis- trict and the places where there was most overcrowding and where the habits of the people were most dirty." The report establishes two things very clearly—namely, that a district in which the sanitary authority neglects to enforce the requirements of the law is a menace to the health of the community around it, and that under conditions such as those found to prevail at the place in question a disease of the serious character of typhus may he generated in an alarming degree.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
