      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>flfl EXAMINER. SATURDAY, MARCH 24, 1894.</head>
        <p>flfl EXAMINER. SATURDAY, MARCH 24, 1894.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MY "Dzmurs: Nigusi,.</p>
        <p>With the earlier mid brighter days of spring the first change from winter garments is usually made in the outdoor wrap, which becomes oppressive when the sun shines, and a jacket of smart cub and light weight is altogether more comfortable and effective. Already spring jackets are shown, minus the heavy frills and bulky collars and pelerinew which were fashionable last season, and where there is either the one or the other, it is closely shaped at the neck and elopes out full and wide at the edge. In all millinery arrangements black flowers are the chief idea of he moment. A pretty lace and jet bonnet of the long narrow shape now fashion- able was almost entirely covered with mauve. coloured cyclamen, trimmed with jet and ivy berries, a jet butterfly in the front and black satin at the back. Huge bows are introduced on many of the bonnets. A light green straw, for example, bad a black one at the back and in front; while a jet plateau was trimmed under the brim with deep pink geraniums, an aigrette, and a bow of tulle.</p>
        <p>A pretty toqt. lack satin, jet pine, and black jtsttecl quills, is very quiet in style; or the crown and quills may be black, and the bows and border in petunia, or green, or in a deep aolfetino velvet. This toque is very easily made on the lit- tle buckram shapes which are now sold by all fancy millinery houses. Braiding will be largely used on cloth and serge gowns this season; and where this Is not actually worked on the oloth, appliqué trimmings of b in one or two widths may be used With exoellent effect. A useful model has a coat-shaped basque out in one. It forms an excellent spring costume without an outer wrap.</p>
        <p>A pretty afternoon promenade costume is in ruce•coloured corded silk, enhanced with rows of ; embroidered galon. Two side folds hang on the i skirt from the waist to the feet, passing over the fancy bands. Capote in maroon velvet, wrought with gold and ruby, and adorned with feather aigrette and bow in velvet. White, I believe, is to be very much worn dur- ing the season. It will appear isi conjunction with black and grey and heliotrope in gowns, it will he used alone for summer garb, and it will take a prominent place in millinery.. Cloth coats are wonderfully freshened if cleaned by the chemical process, which removes all the dust and superficial stains, and raises the flap. All creases are smoothed away, and the cloth hangs better, and the seams look neat and close. If the sleeves are small at the top, an epaulette or a double frill gives a wider appearance, or, if no more of the cloth is obtainable, one single epaulette of plush or velvet, cub in circular form, so as to fall wide at the edge and avoid bulk at the top, makes a very stylish finish.</p>
