      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>THE DOUBLE LIFE;</p>
        <p>OR, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY. By the Author of " DARKWOOD."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER V. THE SECRET HOME. Let me live among high thoughts, and smiles As beautiful as love; with grasping hands, And a heart that flutters with diviner life Whene'er my step is heard.</p>
        <p>PROCTOR. The Lady Beatrice moved towardit a door oppo- site to that by which she had entered the dressing- room, passed through a bed-chamber, and con- fronted a third door. She pushed it open softly, and looked into the room upon which it gave. It was a pretty, square room with an oriel win- dow, glowing lights, and a bright fire. It was evi- dently a study. Book-cases were lot into the walls, and their glazed doors stood ajar. An easel, with brushes, was in one corner. A couch, draped with a half-finished picture, pallet and trailing leopard skins, was drawn up before the cheerful. hearth.</p>
        <p>The Latly Beatrice was about to enter the study when a step was heard in the hall without, and a gentleman entered the room abruptly, coming for- ward and flinging himself in a careful attitude upon</p>
        <p>the leop.tril-sTi.in—eouch. He was, without. doubt, the Spanish count to whom the cabman referred.</p>
        <p>He looked the :Spanish grandee. Tall and state- ly, intemely dark, with raven hair, he was at once commanding and majestic. singularly in con- trast to his hair and complexion, his eyes were of the purest, deepest blue, Hianianiter seemed habitually haughty and melancholy. He had the look of en exile—that look of sorrowful yearning</p>
        <p>cen in those banished from home and country,</p>
        <p>and who on.; for freedom and their rights again. He was known as the Count Arevalo. Ile had bought The Evergreens, as the pretty, secluded place was called, many years previous to the open- ing date of our story, and had since led a hermit or student-like existence between its walls, making no acquaintances, receiving, no visitors. As she looked Out upon the Spanish count, the face of the Lady Beatrice was absolutely trans- figured. As the frost aud ice break up and melt before the warm sun, so the frost and ice of her manner and looks melted as she regarded him.</p>
        <p>The hot colour leaped to her cheeks:. Her eyes glowed. Her red mouth broke into a joyous smile. Unconscious of her proximity, the count closed his eyes wearily, murmuring : "It is lonely to-night. It is almost a week since she was here."</p>
        <p>The earl's daughter. stole from her concealment, crossed the floor noiselessly, came up behind the recumbent figure, put her hands over the gentle- man's eyes, and cried, gayly: "Guess who it is, Count Arevalo ? " The count started with a cry of joy, caught her bands in both his own, and, leaping to his feet, he ejaculated: " Beatrice ! My wife! You have come to me upon such a night as thiz ? " "Yes, my husband!" answered the Lady Bea- trice, with a radiant look. "Did you think the storm could keep me from you? The greater the gloom without, the more need have I of light and pleasure within." The count gathered the Lady Beatrice to his bosom, lavishing upon her a very storm of kisses and caresses, and again and again calling her by that sweetest of all names, wife. This, then, was the secret of the Lady Beatrice Hampton ! This the cause of her mystelious ab- sence which had so puzzled and distressed her father and Lord A.dlowe ! This the reason of her repellant haughtiness to suitors in her fashionable world.</p>
        <p>She did, indeed, lead a double life. In the one she was the icy and unapproachable belle and queen of society. In the other, she was a wife, the idol of her husband, the sunlight of his home. It was a strange mystery, that in which the Lady Beatrice lived and moved—a mystery which will be made apparent in the course of our truthful record.</p>
        <p>_ "My angel !" exclaimed the count, with a fervor that showed the fond • epithet to come from his heart. " Sit down beside me, darling. You did not walk from the station ? If I had thought your coming possible, I should have sent the puny-car- riage to meet you." " Do not reproach yourself, my husband," said the 'Lady Beatrice, permitting herself to be drawn to a seat upon the couch. " I came home in a cab, and let myself in at the garden dour. I wanted to surprise you—" " And you succeeded completely," replied the count. " I came up here but now, my darling, to think of you and to fancy you were with me, little dreaming that you were indeed here. How shall I repay your love, your devotion, your self-sa,cri- lice ? "</p>
        <p>_ He took up her slender, white hands, with bat one ring visible now, and that a plain gold wedding ring she had put on with her present attire, and kissed them s ith lover-like rapture. "You are making me out very generous and kind to you," said the Lady Beatrice, with a light, care-free laugh. " Instead, I was kind to myself principally. It was so dull at Hampton House, and I was out of sorts—gloomy, in fact, and so I came to you to be cheered up." " You gloomy, Beatrice " said the count, incred- ulously. "You, my sunlight, my household angel, my bright-faced queen ! Why, I have never seen you gloomy since the day you united your for- tune to mine! It is as if you had said the sun was gloomy ! " "The sun has its shadows, you know, my hus- band ! " "True, my darling, and you must have some gloomy hours. I have been inclined to forget this, for my own moods are so sombre and gloomy, and you have always come to me like the warm sun- shine, dissipating the shadows. I have relied upon I you, depended upon your strength and wisdom, never thinking in my selfishness that your heart must be sometimes weak—" "My heart is never weak," interposed the Lady Beatrice, gravely, yet with a sweet supporting tenderness that was inexpressibly dear to her hear- er. "Your leaning upon it gives it strength. But I wish we could own our marriage, my husband t For your sake, I wish it I " . "I am content, Beatrice. To know that you are mine, that your thoughts turn to me from your crowded ball-rooms and your gay companions, is bliss enough. To read in the court papers that the haughty and unapproachable Lady Beatrice Hamp- ton' was the star at some gay assembly or court drawing-room is happiness to me. I have seen allusions to your coldness, to your many admirers, and to your persistent supposed celibacy, and my heart has thrilled with rapture at the thought that this haughty lady was my sweet and gentle wife, this proud court beauty my secret home angel, and the mother of my children !" With tears of genuine feeling he .clasped her closer, and pressed a kiss of holy tenderness upon her brow. "Why were your thoughts so gloomy to-night, precious wife?" asked the count, after a brief and blissful silence. "Has anything gone wrong with you at Hampton House? Does any one suspect our secret?" "No, no; I think not, my husband; although my father to-night remarked upon my coldness, and protested his desire to see me married before he dies. He says he wants to see his grandchildren about his knees before he goes hence. He is good and noble, but very stern and unforgiving when his will is crossed. Besides, such a confession would ruin you, my husband. While one person lives your existence must remain a profound secret." " You mean LOIN' Trevelyan?" " Yes dearest. Lord Trevelyan is dreadfully bitter aLrainst you. He cannot live much longer and until he dies, no one must dream that Geoffrey Trevelyan lives and is in England under the name of the Count Arevola ! Lord Trevelyan would nok scruple to send you to prison—to penal sery tude even—if he dreamed that you still lived. He has proofs which he is malignant enough to use should the chance arise." "I know it," groaned the Spanish count, other- wise Geoffrey Trevelyan, who had been supposed dead so many years, and of whose continued existence no one save the Lady Beatrice even dreamed, whatever Lord Adlowe might have idly suspected. "He has proofs, that would destroy me for a crime I never committed. I never robbed my uncle. I never assaulted him, with intent to murder him. I have often explained to you how it was, Beatrice. It was to my cousin Ormond, Lord Adlowe, I owe all my trouble. Had I never been born, Ormond would have inherited the title and estates of the Marquis Trevelyan. I was, however, the son of Lord Trevelyan's second brother, and by law my, claims were of course prior to those of Ormond, who was the son of Lord Trevelyan's sister. Ormond never forgave me for having come into the world to interfere with his claims. My uncle, Lora Trey elyau, to whose</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>-guarcuanslup I was left at an early age, as was also my cousin, took a malicious pleasure in' reminding Ormond of the change my coming had made in his prospects. How often have I heard my uncle say to Ormond, as he laid his hula on my cousin's head 'You would have been the richest man in .England, toy, boy II it had out Leon fur your ouud,di Geoffrey 1 All the grand Trevelyan estates would have gone to you, but for him.' Ormond's hatred for me was thus early fostered into a consuming passion. It was he who turned my uncle against me, and blackened my name. It is be, Beatrice, to whom I owe all my troubles ! " "My poor husband!" said the Lady Beatrice, in soft, caressing tones, fondling the thin, nervous hand that clasped her shoulder. " Ormond's hatred for me culminated in the hour when he discovered my love for you, Beatrice," said the count, as we shall continue to call him.</p>
        <p>"lb seems he loved you even then, my queen, my beautiful darling. Yet he skilfully disguised his hatred and feigned friendship. My uncle, Lord Trevelyan, was very penurious. He is now miserly. Although his inalienable heir, he being without children, he yet kept me upon a bare pittance by way of allowance. I was but one-and- twenty then, a gay, joyous youth, full of health and spirits, very unlike the quiet being I am now." "How well I remember you as you were then, my husband !" said the Lady Beatrice, carefully avoiding to call him Geoffrey—a name she had scarcely allowed herself to utter for many years, lest it might sometimes escape her lips unawares. "You were a bright, laughter-loving boy, innocent of harm as a babe! I like better the grave and thoughtful man into which you have matured," she added, gently, as if fearing that he regretted his lost joyousness and spirits. "Life has a tendency to sober us all."</p>
        <p>"Yes, Beatrice, but I am happier thar I was then," returned her husband, tenderly. "My grave mien hides a heart more joyous than I carried in my urtried. youth. Ormond was older than I. He knew of my pecuniary trials, and lost no oppor- tunity of inciting my mind against my uncle, even while he poisoned my uncle's mind against me. One evening—we were at Trevelyan Park then— he led the conversation against Lord Trevelyan, as usual. He told me that his lordship had a desk filled with gold and bank-notes. He plied me with drink. It was the first time and the last, Beatrice, that I was overcome with liquor. He tempted me to eat opium with him, declaring that the practice made his life delightful. I yielded, for an experi- ment. I was always ready for foolish experiments then," he added, with a deep-drawn sigh. "Well ? " said the Lady Beatrice, excitedly, al- though she had heard the story many times be- fore.</p>
        <p>"The liquor or the drug, or both, fired me to frenzy. I was in a wild deliriam. The next hour was a blank. I awoke from that lost period to find myself in Lord Trevelyan's chamber, struggling with his lordship, a knife in my hands ! It was an awful moment ! I stood paralysed 1. I heard in uncle heaping reproaches and curses upon. me heard Ormond's cry of horror as he entered to rescue our relative. I heard the shrieks of the ser- vants, and their cries of astonishment. Then it all rushed upon soul in keen and agonised realisation. The money bags on the floor, the wound on my uncle's shoulder, the drawn dagger ! Oh, God ! and I would have died before harming one grey hair on Lord Trevelyan's head ! " . . _</p>
        <p>The Count's voice was broken with sobs, and his face was convulsed with a terrible anguish. "Do not grieve so, my husband," said the Lady Beatrice, in tender sympathy. "You were not to blame. You did not know what you were doing. It was Lord Adlowe who was guilty of the at- tempted crime. He would have had Lord Trevel- ya,n killed, and you hanged for the murder. Then he would have inherited he titles and fortune he so carved."</p>
        <p>"You are right, Beatrice," replied her husband, calming himself by a resolute effort. "I know that my cousin, Lord Adlowe, took advantage of my delirium, and incited me to the fearful crime which was so happily frustrated. It is in his power to clear my name, if he chooses. Until he does, or until Lord Trevelyan dies, I must stay in hiding. My uncle can never forgive me the as- sault I made upon him. It is too much to expect that he should. All I desire is to escape his venge- ance and pursuing hatred. If he thought me still alive, he would leave no stone unturned to effect what he deems my proper punishment.' "He is pitiless and implacable ! " sighed the Lady Beatrice. "And you were innocent."</p>
        <p>"I was innocent of all desire for his death," said her husband, solemnly and earnestly. "I loved him Beatrice, despite his faults. He was the only father I remember. If the truth ever comes to light, my wife—if Ormond should ever confess the exact truth—we may find that it was Ormond's hand, not mine, that dealt the blow that roused the old man from his slumbers. Sometimes I have a faint and struggling memory that Ormond led me into the bed-chamber ; that he dealt the blow ' • that he thrust the blood-stained knife into my hands, and fled so quickly that he was not noticed. Certain it was that he was the first to come at my uncle's summons."</p>
        <p>"You are right !" cried the Lady Beatrice with the force of a thorough conviction. "That explan- ation is the true one. Lord Adlowe led you into the room—struck the blow—thrust the knife into your holds—and fled the room. I know this is so, my husband. Oh, if we could but prove it !" _</p>
        <p>"That we never can !" returned the count, sor- rowfully. "I stood there stupefied, Beatrice, bending under a storm of reproaches, not knowing what to say in my own defence. Ormond raised his voice against me! Then Lord Trevelyan flung at me one of his bags of gold, and bade me take it and be gone before he should have me arrested I He turned me from his house ! I went to London, to you ! The story of that night's doings followed me. My uncle repented having let me go. He set detectives on my track, that he might deliver me up to justice. I sold my diamond•pin and ring, and with the proceeds fled the country. I went to. Brazil. I sent home carefully prepared proofs of my death in that country. I returned home a few months later—gained an interview with you—and learned that you not only believed in my innocence, but loved me, and clung to me !" " Of course I did !" said the Lady Beatrice, brightly. "We had been betrothed before that sad affair, and I would not give you up. I insisted on the right to cheer you up, my broken-hearted darling. We were married secretly nearly seven- teen years ago. You are thirty-nine years old now, darling, and there is not a gray hair in your head." "But you are as youthful as ever, my bonny bride!" said her lover-husband, fondly. "My sweet-eyed Beatrice, our hidden life here at The I Evergreens has been a rare poem. Here we have I loved and lived. Here were our children born,</p>
        <p>when you were supposed to be absent on the Continent, or in the country. Hcz.e I have studied and written two or three anonymous works,. which have yielded me a certain income, and better still, a reputation which I can claim if ever the stain is cleared from my name. Despite our sorrows, my Beatrice, we are very happy." His glad eyes looked into her radiant ones with joyous tenderness. "I forgot to tell you, my husband," said the Lady Beatrice, after a delicious silence, "that Lord Adlowe has returned from the East. He dined with us ti is evening at Hampton House." "Adlowe back again? At your house ? " cried the count, startled. " Yes, darling, he is the same treacherous, evil- minded man as of old ; but he has caught, during his wanderings, something of romance and a love of the marvellous. He fancies himself a Corsair, I think 7" 'Did he speak to you again of love, Beatrice 7" and the count's brow darkened with agitation, and he folded his wife closer, as if she were a lamb to be protecLed from the ravening wolf. "Of course, dear husband,' answered the Lady Beatrice, lightly. "He would not be Ormond Adlowe if he did not. . He has talked to me of love these many years since I wore short dresses. You can trust me, my husband. Leave me to manage him."</p>
        <p>Her husband pressed a reverent kiss on her pure brows. "I do trust you, my wife," he said, simply, but with his heart and soul in his tones. "I leave him to you; but beware of him, my darling. His very breath is poisonous." The Lady Beatrice forebore to narrate the par- , ticulars of her interview with Lord Adlowe, fear- ing to distress her husband unnecessarily. I "Oh, God if I cc:mid only claim you before the world 1" cried her husband, his voice tremulous with passion. "The time nul,t come for that recognition, my precious wife, my Beatrice ! " "It will come !" declared the Lady Beatrice, in calm assurance, her eyes shining lovingly upon the handsome face bending over her. " Let us wait patiently a little longer. I t will all come out right. And now, my husband," she added, "take 'me too see our treasures. They will assist me in cheering your sad heart. Conte 1" The count arose, his sadness vanishing under her ' sunny regard, gave her his arm, and they quitted the study t,J6etber.</p>
        <p>iTo be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>THREE WAYS OF LOOKING AT IT.</head>
        <p>THREE WAYS OF LOOKING AT IT.</p>
        <p>"Hurrah! it snows," shouted a warmly clad, well-fed boy as he glanced through the windows of the breakfast room one morning early in December; " I'll get my sledge ready, for there's going to be fine sport on the long hill."</p>
        <p>On the same morning, within a mile of the same spot, another boy of about the same age opened the door of the miserable, ramshackle cabin he called home. His feet was bare, and on his thin body hung the fragments of what had once been a suit of clothes. If he had en- joyed a hearty breakfast his appearance did not show it. He was one of the millions of boys to whom poverty has taught the hard reality of things. When he saw the feathery flakes dancing in the air on the biting wind, he hugged himself, shivering, and mut- tered, "My God !it snows." So you see there are at least two ways of looking at</p>
        <p>anything. "I dreaded the approach of winter," says a woman in a recent letter.</p>
        <p>This makes a third way of looking at one thing ; as the woman's reason was different from that of either et the boys. In her case it was a fear of taking cold. She says she took cold almost for nothing. Indeed, she had been through enough to make her sensitive and timid.</p>
        <p>The story as she tells it is this : " From early child- hood," she says, " I suffered from a hacking cough, which was believed to be the'early stages of consump- tion. Up to twelve years of age I was strong and healthy. At first I began to complain of feeling tired, weak, and languid. My appetite was bad, my tongue coated ,and I was continually spitting up a thick phlegm. Presently a hacking cough fastened upon me, and I got no rest day or night.</p>
        <p>"For weeks together I have been confined to my bed, and my breathing was so bad I had to be propped up with pillows. I coughed so much I thought my head would split open, and I had to have it bandaged. I had a constant irritation and tickling in the throat. he straining was so great I feared I should burst a blood- vessel.</p>
        <p>'I As time went on I got emaciated and ttin, and my parents were sure I was in a decline, and were very anxious about me. After trying cough mixtures and other medicines I went to the Birmingham Infirmary (we were then living at ,Birmingham). The doctors asked me many questions and gave me medicines and cod-liver oil, but none of these things helped me, and I wasted away. In the summer I took cold at apparently nothing and I dreaded the approach of 'winter. Owing to the fits of coughing'l scarcely ever slept at night, and when I awoke in the morning the phlegm would break and chip off .quite hard, leaving my chest very tender and sore. In 1&amp;74 we removed to Stourbridge, where I had fresh medical treatment, but the cough and weaknesi became worse and worse.</p>
        <p>"I was now almost a skeleton, and could scarcely stand. In 1880 we came to London, where I had further medical advice, but I got no better. In this condition I remained, slowly sinking, until November, 1881, when we came upon a little book describing Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. I read the book, which contained cases like my own, only it said the disease was not con- sumption, but indigestion and dyspepsia, showing the same feelings and symptoms. "I began taking the Syrop, and felt relief after the first bottle.. Gradually as this medicine acted upon my system my stomach improved, and I could eat better and digest my food; and by-and-by the cough left me and I gained strength, for which I deeply thanked Mother Seigel.</p>
        <p>"Since then I have been in the best of health. All my friends wondered at my recovery, and I told them what had done it after the doctors had failed. I will gladly answer enquiries. Yours truly, (eif.med) Mrs. Jana Braithvvaite, 5, Walnut Tree Walk, Lambeth, London, July 16th, 1892."</p>
        <p>We call attentioß once more to the dreadful mistakes which arise from the close resemblance between the_ signs of true consumption and those of indigestion and dyspepsia. Thousands die of the latter disease because the doctors seem unable to distinguish the one from the other. In the latter there is no organic trouble with the lungs whatever, and such cases as compared with real consumption are as a hundred to one. If you are affl;cted as this lady was, first try Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and the chances are you will soon recover.</p>
        <p>Mrs. B. no lon2er dreads the winter, because the cold has no longer an ally within her own body.</p>
        <p>"Is this true, old man, that your wife hill la you?" "I am afraid it is, old fellow." "Why, what's the trouble—or is it something you don't want to talk about ?" "Oh, no; it's no secret. She said she was afraid to sleep in the house alone all night." STRUCK A BONANZA.—" HOW has young Fosdick succeeded in the crockery business ?" "Splen- didly 1 Amassed a large fortune in three years." "Miraculous "Not at aIL You see, he secured a contract to supply dishes to an insane asylum." HIS NATURAL ENEMY.—Mrs. Elderleigh : "Do you love your teacher, Johnny ?" Johnny "Yes, ma'am." Mrs. Elderleigh : "Why do you love her ?" Johnny "Cause the Bible says we're to love our enemies." MUTUAL RESENTBIENT.—HOjaCk "I iwonder what made Tillinghast and Dimling such mortal enemies ?" Tomdik : "Some one told. them there was a strong. resemblance." A BROAD HINT. —Mistress : Now, "Bridget, about that letter to your sweetheart—what do you wish me to write ?" Bridget: " Och, shure, ma'am, an' iv yer leddyship would just wroite that iv it's more wages the misthress will be givin me, it's Patrick an' messel', shure, will be married when it the nixt year 1"</p>
      </div>
