      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The declaration of Lord Rosebery that time is to be found for the Miners' Eight Hours Bill has given immense satisfaction in Labour circles. It is now regarded as certain that the bill will pass through the Commons in the course of the present session.</p>
        <p>In his Budget speech Sir William Harcourt will probably have some interesting remarks on the weather in respect to its influence upon the national income. In the hot weather which pre- vailed during last summer people took to drinking 'eer in preference to spirits, with marked effect Upon the revenue from excise.</p>
        <p>I hear that the minority report of the Labour Commission is now in type,and will be presented to the Commissioners on the first meeting after the Duke of Devonshire's return, it is understood to be a every thoroughgoing document. It is signed by Mr. Mawdsley, Air. Tom Mann, and Mr. Michael Austin, M.A.,and possibly other Commissioners may loin them.</p>
        <p>Colonel Cody met Mr. Fred May of New York in a Washington café the other day and promptly gave him a severe punch in the jaw. May is the man 'who horsewhipped James Gordon Bennett in earlier years. and the " an Francisco Chronicle" adds that Buffalo Bill and he quarralled six years ago in London over a young actress who is presently ap•- pearing in the Broadway Theatre at New York.</p>
        <p>According to a return just presented to the House of Commow, the estimated population of England AID to the middle of last year (1893) was 29,731,100. The number nf TP-rsonn married during the year so ending was 436.502; the births were 914,189,and the deaths were 569 923. Of the births the number of males was 465,517, and of females 448,672; of the deaths the number of males was 291,948, and of males 277,975.</p>
        <p>Friends of General Booth propose to raise by July next a thanksgiving fund of £,,0,000 in celebration of the jubilee of his ministry. Toe General, who intends to add to the £50,000 a further sum of £20,000 bequeathed to him by a lady, is desirous of Marking the jubilee by the establishment of a home for lost childreti, . new shelter for the homeless and starving in bondon,and anotherin Glasgow,and the selection of the site for the oversea colony.</p>
        <p>--- In the current issue of the " Speaker " there is a curious story told by a writer who went to the House of Lords to hear Lord Rosebery deliver his irst speech as Premier. Prior to tha scene in the gilded chamber Lord Rosebery passed into the House of Commons leading his little daughter by the hand, and just as he was entering his foot slipped and he nearly fell, but recovered himself in time. Students of signs and portents should be interested in this story.</p>
        <p>The circulation of Professor Drummond's little books is something startling. " Toe Greatest Thing in the World " has reached three hundred and 'fifteen thousand ; " Pax Vobiseum," one hundred and twenty-two thousand ; "The Changed Life." eighty-five thousand ; "The Programme of Chris- tianity," sixty-three thousand ; •"The City Without a Church,," fifty-nine thonsand. These are all, absolutely and professedly ,religious books.</p>
        <p>"The Song of the Shirt is still being sung, but the dirge is gradually dying and in its place is com- ing a brighter melody. Anyhow, to quit metaphor, the Early Closing kssociation may pat itself on the back. A hundred and seventy-seven firms in the wholesale Manchester, Bradford, hardware and mis- ,eellaneous trades, twenty-six West-end firms, including several of the principal drapery and furn- i&amp;hing, glass and china houses, will close their Premises from the Thursday evening before Good Friday to the following Tuesday morning, are Inanimous.</p>
        <p>The Canadian pig, like the Canadian hen, is look- ing up, says the "Colonies and India." Samples of the pea-fed bacon from the Dominion have been sent to Australia by the Huddart liners, and are said to have sold well. But let us hope that it will be described as Canadian. It isA:imite good enough to stand on its own merits, and, if cut and cured to ellit the markets to which it is sent, a good sale may always be predicted for it, There is a growing demand for it in I ondon, Bristol, Liverpool, and Glasgow. Pa price it is rapidly creeping up, and before long there will not be much margin between it and the best Wiltshire.</p>
        <p>Mr. Devereux, the co-respondent in the pathetic divorce case of Romer versus Romer and Deverenx. 'reported on 'Saturday last, is the husband of the popular actress known to the public as Miss Annie Hughes. Mrs. Devereux is, the "'Daily 'News" understands, about to take proceedings against her husband for divorce. Mrs. Devereux has only been 'married a few years, and has only appeared on the stage since at somewhat lengthy intervals. Miss Hughes" made her name "as the soubrette in Mr. (Gillette's war play," Held by the Enemy," and since then has held an almost unrivalled position as an 'lngenue,. She can be pathetic, too, when there is need of it, as those who have watched her career Can tell you. What a pity if there 'is to be !pathos 111 her drama of life.</p>
        <p>Opinion among the Irish members is not quite unanimous as to the effect of Lord Rosebery's speech. Some of them are thoroughly satisfied. Some of them think that the explanation leaves tatters almost as they were. It emphasises the inability of the present Parliament to pass Home Rule, and also, apparently, asserts the inability of any other Parliament to do so without .an increased majority. What, they ask, is to happen if the Government is again supported by a majority of, Say, fifty'? Some other Irish members, while not dissatisfied with the explanation, think Lord Rose- berry might have seized the opportunity to make a specific declaration that the Home Rule he is in favour of is Home Rule on the lines of the bill of 1893. It may be taken that the general effect of the Premier's speeches has been to leave the Irish members not so much satisfied as quiescent.</p>
        <p>I hear a rumour that the new Registration Bill will make provision for two registers in each year, One in January and the other in J uly. If this is the ease, it will be a very important departure in our registration methods ; and it also suggests interest- lpg reflections in connection with the existing poli- tical situation. It reav be taken for granted that if the bill is carried within a reasonable time the Government will not—if they can avoid it—dis- solve on the present register. They will wait until the July register is in force -; and this in all proba- bility means that they will wait until the autumn. The idea that there will be an autumn election, which was at one time generally scoured,has in the last few days been gaining ground. It is the most Obvious way in which the Government can hope to redeem their promises in regard to the legislation of t:he session, while at the same time averting the Indefinite "banging up" of Dome Rule.</p>
        <p>The Irish members are quite satisfied that the present session should be given up to British legis- lation, but it is doubtful whether they would be Willing to wait longer. On the other hand, it is certain that the Government cannot hope seriously to take in hand the Welsh Disestablishtnent ;.Bill or the Evicted Tenants Bill before harvest time. If they contemplate a long session lasting into Sep- tember and followed by a general election in October or November, there is no reason why they Should not make an effort to deal with most of the leading measures mentioned in the Queen's Speech, 60 that if they have to go to the country with small results the blame will be seen to lie elsewhere. Of Course it is impossible to say what development tents may take in the present state of parties; ut,taking all things into consideration, it certainly 6eems that an autumn election would fit in very Well with the exigencies of the case.</p>
        <p>In Pall. Winter, and Spring we need the warmth furnished bY healthy flesh. We also need the latent force therein to draw from. We can have both by taking Scott's Emulsion Scott's Emulsion supplies solid flesh and plenty of extra ,trength—two safeguards against disease. Physicians, the world over, endorse it. Chemists sell it. 2s. O. and</p>
        <p>While in America Mr. Chamberlain heard of the lam- -9S transatlantic professor, who one morning addressed nis Class in words to this effect : "Gentleman, this rllorning you will be required to write me an essay. The me allowed you is five minutes, and the subject is The Immortality of the Soul.'"</p>
        <p>.Thi. !loyal Laundresses say Critchley's "STARCH GLOS • 1 ! nuequalled for imparting a beautifnl finish to starche nen. &amp;c.. either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn. a all. says My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs. Bradford : "Dear sir._ "33 Pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called tsrch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It Should be used in every house. Bold at id., 3d., and fid..„by most starch sellers. If not in stock will proenre it. T. CiarTcuLs7„ Bkdakburn. Pieprietor.—Airt•</p>
