      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>LIBERAL MEETING AT</head>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETING AT</p>
        <p>WA RUIN GTON.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY 'MR A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Council of the Liberal Associa- tion was held at the Liberal Club on Monday even- ing for the purpose of passing resolutions relative to the present, political situation. Mr. Win. Pier- point, J.P. presided and among those present were: Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Councillor H. Roberts, J.P., Mr. J. 11. Fleming, J.P.,Mt. T. Birchall, J.P., r. T. Morris, Mr. J. Mounfield, Mr.D. Tinnion, Mr. J. strange, Mr. J. Gandy, and Mr. Clayton (agent.) Letters of apology for non-attendance were received from Messrs. C. J. Holmes and Councillor Shaw Green. The Cnatraterr said that he had to announce that Mr. John Rylands could not be there that night, as his medical adviser insisted upon his not taking part in any evening meetings. With the object of the meeting that night,everyone in the room and everyone connected with the Liberal party he was assured would have the heartiest wishes and sympathy. He was glad that they had such an able gentleman in the room as Mr. Arthur Crosfield. They were always pleased to have him among them. He did not wish to raise their hopes too much or to discourage Mr. Crosfield too much by expecting too much from him, but he was sure that v,ith the marked ability of Mr. Crosfield, he would say something well worth listening to.</p>
        <p>Mr.A. H. CROSFIELD, who was received with applause, proposed the adoption of the following resolution "That this meeting of the Warrington Liberal Association have learned with serious regret of the retirement of Mr. Mr. Gladstone, M.P., from the position which he occu- pied as her Majesty's Prime Minister, and it desires to place on record, its high appreciation of the in- numerable services rendered during a long public life, of his wisdom as a leader, his indomitable courage, his matchles eloquence and unswerving faith in the prin- ciples of justice and truth, which have earned for him and secured for him the heartfelt gratitude of his fellow- cduntrymen." The charactertic feature of Liberalism wa;, he said, its broad-mindedness. (Hear, hear and ap- plause.) The broad Liberal banner embraced different forward movements, but it would not be weakened be some diversity of opinion. Freedom of individual judg- ment was one of the essentials of their creed, and the party would not be weakened by a diversity of opinion, but perhaps it might rather lend colour and interest to it. It had been said that knowledge and perfect culture consisted in a thorough knowledge of something, and a small knowledge about everything else—(laughter and applause)—that they should know something of every- thing and everything of something. It was not a bad thing to have in the party,men who made a speciality of some particular line, and who were supported by and in their turn to support others who made some other thing their speciality, and it was not likely they would be so foolieh as to fight for their own special object to the ex- clusion—instead of in common with—the other objects *approved of. (Hear, hear.) Taking that view he thought they need .not look with any apprehension to the individual free-mindedness that they found in their party. Alluding to the cabinet, Mr. Crosfield said it had been remarked that they had not had a stronger cabinet since Sir Robert Peel gathered his friends around him in 1841. (Applause.) His recol- lection, unlike that of Mr. Gladstone did not go back to 1841, but he could well imagine that they never had a sounder and more competent set of men in office than they had on the Treasury Bench just now. (Applause.) He did not intend to criticize the band of men who were , now leadingthem, and had led them through other ses- sions, but take Sir William Harcourt. He thought he was an underrated man. (Hear, hear.) Apart from his [moral convictions, which were better than some people gave him credit for, who had there ever been who could better pull the party together in the 01,1 days, at two or three o'clock in the morning, when they had the all- night sittings? Who had faced the black situation with more confidence? He had eften been to the party with his spirited manner and activity as good as a tonic in I the House of Commons—as good a tonic indeed as was their chairman, Mr. William Pierpoint, to the Lib- eral party of Warrington. (Applause.) What was one' to say of Mr. John Morley? (Applause.) He was in -example to them. No self-seeking, no selfish motive hal induced him to stick to his post with regard to Irish matters. They all knew the difficulties he had had to contend with, but he had shown himself worthy of the traditions which descended upon his shoulders and which had been bequeathed to him by his master, John Stuart Mill. ( Applause.) With regard to the Premier,th ere were plaudits on all sides. 'Undoubtedly Lord Rasebery had acquitted himself with distinction, and he had shown great ability and competence in the different responsible positions that he had been called upon to fulfil. lie had riayPri his part with pluck and firmness on the County Council, and he had shown a touch of that pleasant humour that would suit equally the Irish, the Scotch, the 'English, and the Nif•lsh, and he thought they would find torethat if he did speak in the House of Lords much— and he hoped he would devote his attention chieflyto other audiences, they would also find that he was not wanting in that touch of irony which would enable him to pay their friend Lord Salisbury back, not, perhane, exactly in his own coin, but with keenness, without the touch of acidity they generally found in Lord Salisbury's re-- marks. (Applause.) But there was another branch in which they lociked for a great deal for Lord Rosebery. It was said that there was something of a rift within the lute between capital and labour now-a-days, and Hut there was a tendency an the part of leading members to separate themselves f rom the Liberal party. With regard Ito *the first assertion, there might be some foundation etor it, but with regard to the second he did not know whether he was more pleased or surprised at the man- ner in which the most influential and responsible of the labour members had borne themselves in the House of Commons. He did not know that there was a single section in the House that should commend itself to more genuine respect from them than such men as Mr. Fen- wick, Mr. Broadhurst, Mr. Burt, and last, but certainly not least, Mr. Burns. (Hear, hear.) He was now touching upon what was a very large, difficult, and interesting problem, but in reaping the golden harvest of irdustry, whatever might be the share that ought to go to labour and to capital, they should in the interests of both, consider well what they did. They should not kill the tree that bore the fruit. (Hear, hear.) It was an old phrme, but it was a good metaphor—a Platitude that would bear repitition. They must be-careful not to lop off the branches or nip the shoots in thebud in counter acting the selfishness of capital and the impulsiveness of labour.. (Applause..) In meeting the two Lord Rosehery had shown himself a man, and he should also be a good ser- vant of the country in the years that were before him. With regard to Mr. Gladstone—and he now came to the point of the resolution—of course their leader had for the greater part left them. The chief weight of the separation they had already had to face, and it seemed as if the keystone from the great political arch had been taken out. It would be difficult for Lord Rosehery to follow him—(hear, hear)—but time and nature, and those forces and the incredible power that made for progress and for wisdom, would give them men who would find a worthy ambition and, perhaps, too, a great and deeper source of consolation in devoting themselves to the furtherance of the good government of this country and its welfare. (Applause.) But, at the same time, they would look in vain for the meriad-minded genius and the boundless courage and in the case of men of his temperament that greatest virtue of ell, the heroic patience which in Mr. Gladetone had been for years the admiration not of England alone, but of the whole English-speaking world. (Loud ap- plause.) They might indeed find the genius, r courage, or patience, but where else would they find them. united in such a marvellous combination, with suela mental and physical powers at an age when the eyes of most men had grown dim to it all? (Applause.) It was said they frittered their time away a good deal over the press. He did not envy the man whose sole mental regime consisted in journalist criticism, but the press, which was invaluable to them, had done a good deal for all of them in so far that it hid eneliled them to follow the workings of his mind, the magnani- mity with which he had fought the most diffieult battle any statesmen had ever yet undertaken. It was said that he did not know what the difficulty, what the den ger was when he undertook it. Well, that was not the fact. In one breath people told them that, and in the other referred to him as " The wily old Parliamentary hand." He was that. (Laughter and applause.) But being that they did not think that he knew of the pre- judice that he had to face ? His (Mr. Crosfield's) father saw John Bright shortly after the terrible split in the Liberal party had taken place, and Mr. Bright showed him copies of the letters he had written to Mr. Gladstone expressing the sorrow he felt—there was no personal feeling there—at severing himself from Mr. Gladstone. In the face of that what was the use of pretending that Mr. Gladstone did not know of the difficulties he would have to face ? He did not know of them, and whereas Mr. Gladstone was represented as; a man who wag always simply fishing for votes,given the conviction—the moral conviction—that with him a certain course was right, nothing stood between him and the prosecution of that cause. (Applause.) Mr. Bright told his father—and he could remember him repeating the words when he returned home—" He is the greatest giant I have ever known ;" and he (the speaker) would not be exceeding the mark when he told Mrs. Gladstone.who was scarcely a less wonderful person than her husband, the other day at Biarritz, that to have known him was in every sense of the word to many of them a liberal education. (Applause.) In that beautiful place, surrounded by all the glories of nature he also saw Mr. Gladstone sitting in peace and quiet and enjoying the prospect before him. He did not intrude upon his privacy. but as he watched him there came back to his mind the lines of a man who was less read now than he deserved to he. As he thought of what rested on Mr. Gladstone's shoulders, he almost suggested the boundless energy and force of the kaantie as it railed in ;</p>
        <p>Between two worlds life hovers like a star "Twixt night and morn upon the horiz m's verge. How little do we know that which we are, flow less what we may be; the eternal surge Of time and tide rolls on, and bears afar Our bubbles. As the old burst new emerge, Lashed from the foam of ages, while the graves Of empires heave but like some passing waves. (Applause.) But the application of those lines was not perfect, far from it. As George Eliot for one had said in criticism of Mr. Buckle's opposite view : " the world of civilization is the better for the life of every good man and woman." If so, how much more for the energy and life work of such a statesman ? At a time like this one knew more of his personality than his partieular achieve- ments. (Hear, hear.) Indeed, while Mr. Gladstone was passing from their midst, they did not care to enter into considerations comparatively more sordid, but in so far as national well-being and mental happiness, and even ordinary everyday comfort for the general population of the kingdom and o.her kingdoms were concerned, there was no man living to whom they owed one-quarter, yea, one-tenth of what they owed to ' Mr. GI dstone for his commercial and his magnanimous policy. (Applause.) Bearing that in mind, when Mr. Gladstone asked the democracy of this country to apply to the sister democracy the same principles under which we had thriven so well, in place of the wretched policy which had been carried on for so many centuries, it seemed when they turned the cold shoulder on him that it was a sad and a piteous spectacle. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion, Mr. Crosfield said he thought they might go on with a good heart and support the men he had named stimulated by the example that many of them had shown, and by the record they had of others who had gene before them, still more stimulated by the old man who was ssill with them ; and, he woeld add, fortified tw the firm conviction that if they followed on those lines, and fought out their greet issues with courage, and with prudence, they would have the unalterable and priceless c insolation af knowing that they in so far as their moral capacities, be they small or en e, had enabled .them they had helped, to swell in the right direction that great tide on which the fortunes of our Empire and of the democracy, the leader of all the democracies in the world, depended. (Applause.) Mr. 13eneefeee said that be rose to second the resolu- tion with a certain mixture of feeling of pleasure and pain—of pleasure at seconding such a resolution as that, and to record their gratitude to the great statesman who had just retired from theg leadership of the party, and a feeling of pain that in the ordinary course of events that great career had come to an end. But that was not 'true. That great career would never come to an end. (Hear, hear.) As the poet said, " The good die not," and so when William Ewart Gladstone, had long been laid in his grave, his words would live. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Crostield alluded to the Liberal party and it; various statesmen. He (Mr. Birchall) must say that he never felt more proud of the Liberal party than he did at present. (Applause.) Never did it get so many kicks, and rubs, but never did it lift its head as proudly as it did at the present moment. With regard to the various statesmen who formed the Government, he did not know any of them personally, although he knew a little about Mr. Acland. Than Mr. Acland—froin what he had seen of him, and from what some of his friends had said of him—there was no nobler man in the cabinet —a man whose whole life had been centred in the well- being of the workingmen of the country. He had been connected with a movement with which he (Mr. Birchall) had been engaged for many years. Mr. Cros- field Alluded to the various abilities of Sir William Har- court. He thought he might have gone a little further. There was no man who had so severely scored the backs of his opponents, and rubbed the salt in, as Sir William Harcourt. (Laaghter.) When he arose, they knew what to expect. (Laughter.) With regard to the reso- lution,he might say that for nearly half a century he had taken deep interest in political affairs and worshipped at a distance the great men, and for nearly half a century lee had watched the career of William Ewart Gladstone. Two or three times he had had the pleasure of listening to him, and that noble voice would always ring in his heart. They knew some little of his family life. It was a model to the world. (Hear, hear.) The simplicity of childhood existed in that mind with the noble charac- teristics of the highest manhood, and he believed that the personal influence of Mr. Gladstone had been one of the greatest blessings to the world during the past half century. (Applause.) The motion was carried with much enthusiasm. Councillor ROBERTS moved :—" That, in the opinion of this meeting of the. Warrington Liberal Association, the power now possessed by the House of Lords over Bills that have secured the approval of the House of Commons is inconsistent with popular government, and subversive of the true interests of the people ; and declares that the lords and bishops should be deprived of the power of vetoing and mutilating measures iassed by the elected representatives of the people in Parliament. We also desire to express our entire confidence in Lord Rosebory, her Majesty's Prime Minister, and our hearty approval of the various measures to be introduced by her Majesty's Government during the present session, and trust that they may speedily become law.'' Allud- ing to certain recent correspondence in the local press, Mr. Roberts remarked that it would have been more to the interest of the Liberal party, more true to the instincts and professions of Liberalism, if those candid friends of theirs had ventured to bring their subjects of dispute before the proper authority constituted to con- sider and settle disputes. To venture in the public press he would not say with the object of exposing the weak- ness of the Liberal party,to expose the alleged weakness was hardly consistent with that loyalty to the party which they had to expect from the humblest of their members. If anything of the kind had to be done in the tuture, he hoped that the members of the party who could exercise'an influence,would see that it was not fair or wise that these controversies should be conducted outside the limits of their own home. (Applause.) Referring to Mr. Gladstone, he said he attributed his success largely not to his wonderful personality so much as to the conviction, the intense conviction of the fact that in all he did and said he was always conscious that a greater power than his own was helping him, and that a greater eve the n his own was watching over his con- duct. Dealing wit h the House of Lords, Mr. Roberts said it was an utter absurdity that the House should veto the will of the people. It was almost an insult to the intelligence of the peonle that it should be necessary to put upon record a resolu- e f the character he'had just read. The democracy d been enthroned by the Act of Parflament and made all powerful, and it did seem ridiculous that there should still be retained an element—giving almost a tyrannical power which was possessed by the House of Lords—hav- ing the uncontrolled power to thwart the expressed will of the people time after time. (Applause.) As to the action of the bishops with regard to the Parish Councils Bill, he said they were so determined to uphold the Lords temporal in the true Conservative spirit of the time that they were prepared to run the risk of damning the moral and social position of many of the ignorant , villagers by forcing them for the discussion of public I matters into the public-house rather than have them in the schools. They were false to the high mission which ' they professed to carry out. Fortunately, however, by the stalwarts in the lower House, headed by the Grand Old Man, the nefarious project was defeated, and they were determined that whatever form the reform of the House of Lord might take, the Bishops should come out of it. (Applause.) As to Lord Rosebery, he had never had the slightest hesitation in feeling assured that he was the right man in the right place, and the Liberal party under him to-day was every bit as strong as it was under Mr. Gladstone. (Hear, hear.) There need be no misgiving on the part of their Irish friends as to Lord Rosebery's position with regard to Home Rule. The Liberal party was pledged to the lips in Home Rule,and they dare not go beyond that pledge. To do so would be an act of treachery that could only end in a way such an act would deserve to end, namely. in utter disaster to the party guilty of it. (Applause.) Mr. BENJAMIN seconded the motion, which was unani- mously carried. A hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman was moved by Mr. BIRCHALL, seconded by Mr. JOHN GANDY, the latter of whom, alluding to some remarks by Mr. Benjamin concerning the House of Lords, expressed his belief that a House of Lords was unnecessary even if elected. He eskei whether it was not reasonable to think that if the House of Commons was elected by the people, it was thoroughly competent to do its work without the supervision of another chamber.—The vote of thanks was unanimously carried and the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>E EXAMINER, SAITII.DAf, MARCH 24, 1891.</head>
        <p>E EXAMINER, SAITII.DAf, MARCH 24, 1891.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The fortni2htly ineei ing of this Board was held on Wed nesdAy in the lliard room of the Workhouse. There were present Mr.G.Ratcliffe (chairman) Rev. G.F. Roberts, Messrs.R.Heaton,S.Davies,W. Hough- ton, A. J. Jolley, J. Hill, J. Muir, G. Pilling, Clarke, Mather, Turton, Fairclough, A. Jolley, J. Dobson, H. J. Longton, E. Aukland, W. Shaw, W. Balharry, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), Ward (master), and H. M. Mines (schools superinten- dent).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SCHOOL ITTENDA.ICCE OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ITTENDA.ICCE OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. HououToN moved the appointment of Messrs. Shearer and Wright as school attendance officers for the union for one year—Mr. E. Aukland seconded.—The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>FIRE ENGINE PRACTICE.—CONFLICTING OPINIONS. Mr. DAVIES moved the adoption ot the House Com- mittee's minutes which were of a routine character. Mr. Shaw in seconding the motion referred to the recent practice of the fire engine at the workhouse. He said that he believed that no matter how great a conflagra- tion there was in the workhouse, there was a sufficient water supply to meet the demand in the reservoir. At the same time the supply from the mains at the gate was quite insufficient.—Mr. W. Houghton said that he was not present at the time of the trial., but was it not a fact that most of the water used went back Into the pond again? In times of fire this would not be the case and there would perhaps not be sufficient water in that case. —Mr. Shaw was of opinion that there was twelve hours supply of water in the pond. (Mr. Muir "No, no.")—Mr. Hill did not believe that with full pressure, the water would last more than half an hour. There was an inede- quate supply of water for the purpose.—The Chairman, on the other hand, thought the result of the test was ' highly satisfactory. The superintenthnt of the fire brig- ade had told him that the workhouse was in a better position for water than any works in Warrington. The water in the pond would last five hours at least.—Mr. Davies also considered that they were in a satisfactory condition against fire.—Mr. Heaton thought that no public institution in Warrington was better provided against fire than the workhoyse, nor was there much liklihood of there being a fire with their stone steps &amp;c. 'Be also considered that there was ample supply in case ,of fire. The Town Council would in a short time give an efficient water supply of both Winwiek and Apple- ton water in that part of the town.—The discussion then dropped and the minutes were passed. PREPARING OR PARISH COUNCILS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said that a letter had been received from the Local Government Board wishing to be informed of the number of Guardians there were for each towliship in the Union. The required information had been sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DR. SPINICS' SUPERANNUATION.</head>
        <p>DR. SPINICS' SUPERANNUATION.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from the Local Government Board confirming the superanknation of Dr. Spinks on the occasion of his retirement from the post of medical officer for the workhouse and No 2 district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MEIDICAL APPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>MEIDICAL APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Davies,Dr.Peacocke was appoin- ted deputy to Dr. Braide for the House and District No 2 Mr. Muir : "Does this incur extra salary ?" (Laugh- ter). The Clerk.: "No." T. HALF YEARLY VIMIATES, The CLERIC stA)mitted the estimates for the half year ending Michaelmas oext and also a return of the ex- penditure at the workhouse and Padgate schools during the past three years. lie said that the preparation of estimates was to enable the board to fix upon the sums to be contributed by the 16 townships of the Union for each following half year, and until the first call was pay- able in the succeeding half year. Estimates did not affect the outlay. The expenditure was at present high, the primary cause being the growth ot county rates, the total of £5,199 in 1892-3 rising to £8,633 in 1893-4. Secondary and less material causes were the outcon-e of important improvements effec- ted at the workhouse and schools, and an excess of pauperism ireated by the coal strike. The annual poor rate return for 1892-3, showed the Board's expenditure in connection with relief of the poor to have been, for in-maintenance, £4,056 ; out- relief, £3,528; lunatics at asylums, £3,281 ; adminis- trative expenses, salaries, rations, £3,203 ; other expenses connected with relief, £750, total, £14.821. Add loan and interest on land and buildings, £1,127 ; total £15,948. Less exchequer contribution in aid of cost of lunatics at asylums, £1,269 ; out of probate duties on account of -the officers' salaries, rations, &amp;c., £3,081.; from relatives of paupers and corresponding sources, £B5l ; from other unions upon transfer of -chargeability of 15 non-settled lunatics, £192 ; and for relief of nousettled poor, £6O; total, £5,453, showing the cost of the relief of the poor, inclusive of land, buildings and administration, out of the local rates, to have been £10,495, or an actual " Poor Rate" for each half year of a fraction over 31. in 'the The sum collected by overseers as Poor Rates was £32,777. Of the difference between £10,495 and £32,777 County and Municipal Authorities received £14,294; highway board £2,767 ; school hoards, £535 ; registers of voters and jury lists cost £465 ; registration £306 ; and vac .nation , £452. The topographical position of theciUnion adversely affected the ratio of pauperism to population. A recent five years average furnished by Mr. Jenner Fust showed a variation between this and two adjacent unions as follows-: Barton-upon-Irwell :—Vagrants (upon cor- responding dates) 3; proportion of other paupers to population, 1 in 109 ; of lunatics to population. 1 in 890. Leigh : Vagrants 2 ; other paupers, 1 in 76 ; lunatics, 1 in 884. Warrington : Vagrants, 40 ; other paupers, lin 61 ; lunatics, lin 629. It may then be claimed that calls to meet the cost of relief and all Union outlay,vary- ing from 43-16 d. to 421 in the £ for each half of five consecutive years are evidence of careful and economi- cal admieistrati en and of executive diligence. The Boards outstanding liability on loan account did not ex- ceed sevenpence in the £ on the rateable value of the Union. The return of the expenditure at the Workhouse and at Padgate schools showed that during the three years ended September last, the total at the Wirkhouse was £10,963 16s. 11d., and at the schools £6,686 3s. 4d., those amounts including all expenditure except salaries rates, taxes, and insurance. The statement as to the county rates, showed that the calls had gone up from £1,820 10s. 91. for the March half year of 1888 to £4,477 Os. 21 in the March half year of 1894.</p>
        <p>TENDERS. The following tenders were accepted : Coal and slack, Messrs. R. Evans and Co., Ltd. ; beef and mutton, Mr. WM. sill; flour and meal, Mrs. Eckersley ; bread tfor schools), Mrs. Eckersloy ; potatoes end straw, Mr. J. W. Houghton cord and twill, Messrs. Iletcher and Lee ; clogs, Mr. Wm. Jones; firewood and stone, Warrington Slate Co.; glazing, Mr. J. Pritchard ; beer and porter. Mrs. Brundrit ; coffins. Mr. Lowden Wells; wines and spirits, Mr. R. Holland. The tenders for leather were left in the hands of the School Committee, and those for groceries, drapery,shoes,and brushes, to the House Com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WARRINGTON BANDS</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON BANDS</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Association was held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening, when the chair was taken by Mr. T. J. Down, the president. Mr. F. Monks, J.P., C.C., was also present. The Chairman expressed his pleasure at being present that evening. He pre- sumed the business of the meeting was to discuss the arrangements for the coming nrlsical season. He thought they might look at the last season in a cheerful frame of mind, for Mr. Taylor had told him that during last season, the attendances were larger and the receipts greater than in any former year. In looking back upon the past, they should be encouraged for the future. This year, they had a greater number of bands than ever. Provided they had fine weather they were looking for- ward to another successful seasen. He believed that the love of music, and the interest in music wag greater in Warrington than ever before. He was hopeful that the park would soon be filled with crowds of people as the football ground was filled at a great football match. —Mr. Joseph Taylor, the secretary, proposed an altera- tion in the minutes of the Association. The minute he referred to was that the representatives from the bands should consist of the conductor and one member from each band. It had been found that this did not work , sufficiently well, and he would propose that each band should send their representatives, the conductor not necessarily being one of them.—The motion was agreed to.—A proposal that a Committee skould be form(d to caect the money in the boxes in the park, and be paid at the same rate as the bandsmen, was deferred.—ln answer to Mr. Monks, Mr. Taylor said that including £25 received from the Cor- poration, the sum received during the past year had amounted to £59 63. 4d. The largest amount received in one afternoon was £2 17s. Bd. They had been able to pay their way,a thing they had not previously been able to do.—Mr. Taylor asked whether, in case the weather was unfavourable, and the Drill Hall could not be ob- tained, any alteration could be made in the programme. It was rather hard upon the band if after practising for some time they must lose their tarn.—Mr. Down said he did not see how this could be altered. If the band took the next week it would upset all the arrangements.— Mr. Armstrong did not think it would be of very great inconvenience, and thought it could be done.—lt was finally resolved that Mr. Taylor write to the committee of the volunteers to endeavour to get the use of the Drill Hall in case of wet weather on Sund.tys.—A dis- cussion next arose as to the raising of the bandsmen's fees. Mr. Taylor said that a balance of £4 was left last season.and it had been divided among the different bands to procure music, new instruments, &amp;c.—Mr. Down and Mr. Monks each thought this would be the best course to take again this year. Last year was the first time they had a surplus, and it would be wise to wait and see whether this state of things was likely to continue before making extra expense.—lt was finally decided to adopt this course.—This concluded the busi- ness of the meeting, and a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Down for presiding, on the motion of Mr.Gorton, seconded by Mr, Dell. " TOBACCONISTS commencing" Il ustratedgnide (post free TOBACCONISTS' OUTFITTING CO.--118. Huston-road, London</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MR. BROADHURST A. CANDIDATE.</head>
        <p>MR. BROADHURST A. CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Broadhurst was on Wednesday chosen as the Liberal candidate for Leicester, to replace Mr. J. A. Picton. who will retire owing to ill-health. Mr. Broad- hurst, in addressing the Liberal Association on Wednes- day evening,said that he loyally accepted the decision of the Government on the eight hours question. Mr. Rol- leston, President of the Leicestsr Conservative Associa- tion, was yesterday asked and accepted the invitation to become the Conservative candidate.</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK LOCAL BOARD. The Haydock Local Board have adopted a petition to the Lord Chancellor praying that Haydock be attached to the St. Helens County Court district insteall of Warrington district. The petition,drawn on the lines of that submitted to the board by Mr. John Green, solicitor, St. Helens,is signed by Mr.T. Grimk -Drayton,chairman, and Mr. John Matthews, clerk. It has been forwarded to the Hon.T.W.Legh, M.P.,for presentation to the Lord Chancellor.—At a special meeting of the Haydock Local Board Mr. Matthews submitted a letter from the County Coun- cil r3ferring to the Local Government Act, 1894, as to the division of Haydock into wards, and sub- mitted a plan showing three wards, namely: Black- , brook to be the West Ward ; from the toll bar to the railway crossing beyond Evans's offices to be the Central Ward ; and from the -railway to Haydock Coursing Ground to be the East Ward; three mem- bers to each. The scheme was approved, and will be submitted to the County Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON 1.11.C.A. CHORAL J RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY SP.CIETY. 1 AUIHORITY,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON 1.11.C.A. CHORAL J RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY SP.CIETY. 1 AUIHORITY,</p>
        <p>LECTURE AND PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>In connection with he above Society, a lecture entitled " An evening with Mendelssohn," was de- livered by the conductor, Rev. Edward Moore, on Monday evening. The lecture loom of the Y.M.C.A. was well filled with the members of the class and their friends, The musical illustrations were furnished by Messrs. R. H. Pearson (violin),H. Bond ('cello), and 3. B. Caldwell (pianoforte) ; the vocalists being Miss Stanley, and Messrs. J. M. and P. I. Bate. The chair was occupied by Dr. J. B. Naden, who, in introducing the lecturer, said they would probably find that the secret of Mendelssohn's success was to lie found in his love and enthusiasm for music. Mr. Moore, in his lecture, dealt with the chief events in the life of the composer, specially accen- tuating his thoroughness and care in all his work. The first musical item was the famous trio in D. miner. This exacting but beautiful work was carefully played by Messrs. Pearson, Boyd, and Caldwell, and was loudly applauded. Then Mr. Jos. M. Bate sang "If with all your hearts," with taste and care, the rendering being well received. After Mr. Moore had explained the origin of the "Songs without words," Mr. Caldwell_ played one of them, the " Duetto." Then came an un expected interruption in the programme. Dr. Naden; rising, said that he was called upon to discharge a plea- sant duty. It was always pleasing when good feeling existed between a teacher and his pupils. Evidently that was so between Mr. Moore and his class, for they had determined to show their appreciation and esteem for him in a tangible way. He then presented Mr. Moore on behalf of the class with a very handsome baton. It is made of ivory, richly mounted in silver. The centre band bears the inscription" Presented by the Warring- ton Y.M.C. A. Choral Society," and Mr. Moore's mono- gram is engraved on the base.—Mr. Moore, in reply,said they had quite taken him by surprise. However, he thanked them heartily for their kind feeling, and should highly prize their beautiful gift as long as he lived. (Loud cheers.) Mr. Moore then used the new baton to conduct the chorus " How lovely are the messengers," from St. Paul. Then came a selection from the " Hymn of Praise," by the gentlemen above named. This en- trancing work was splendidly played, and thoroughly enjoyed. Mr. Pearson then contributed in masterly style a couple of pieces from a violin " Suite." Elijah was briefly described, and was illustrated by " It is enough," truthfully rendered hy Mr. P. I. Bate, and the lovely aria, " 0 rest in the Lord," by Miss Stanley. The lecture was brought to a conclusion by the playing of a funeral march from the" Lieder," played at the com- poser's own funeral. Mr. Moore moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman and the friends who had assisted. Mr. Bayley, in seconding,wished to include the lecturer,ivho, he said, had given them one of the ablest lectures ever delivered in that room. Dr. Naden briefly responded and the proceedings closed.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority was held on Tuesday at the Workhouse, Dutton, Mr. J. Mclntyre presiding The remaining gentlemen present were the Revs. J. W. N. Tanner, and James Hanby, Messis. Jos. Knowles, C. W. Davenport, Enoch Jackson, James Whitlow, Thomas Baxter, 3. M. Williams, Thomas Lewis, Alfred Broome, H. E. Hey- wood, James Brandreth, William Ellison, George Priest ner, Thomas Ford, Charles Mounfield, Samuel Horton, J. L. Jones, Eaton Bretherton, Joseph Hall, W. Pickering, J. W. Bagu- ley, Wm. Whitlow, C. T. Whitley, J. E. Bate, Jno. Weir, Win. Turner, and Edward Aaron.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WESLEYAN METHODISM IN WARRINGTON AND THE NEIGHBOURHOOD.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN METHODISM IN WARRING- TON AND THE NEIGHBOURHOOD.</p>
        <p>The March quarterly meeting of the Warrington Wesleyen circuit was held on Monday afternoon at Bold-street chapel. A very large number of officers sat down to tea,at which Mrs. W. Bennett and Mrs. R. G. Henshall presided at the tables. Several other rladies were also present. The meeting was after- wards held in the band room ot the chapel. There , were present, Revs. J. Kirtlan, Owen Watkine, G. D. itlason, and J. Gunnell, Mr. W. Bennett and Mr. R. G. Ilenshall (circuit stewards) also many other cir- cuit officers. After the minutes of the last meeting were read, Mr. llenshall made the financial state- ment which showed a balance against the circuit as the result of special expenses incurred. Tke super- intendent was able to give a most gratifying report of the work of God as carried on during the year. The class members, and members on trial numbered ' 1355, an increase on the year of 85 following upon , an increase on the previous year of nearly 100. Never in the history ot the circuit had such num- bers been approached. This did not include com- municants who were not reckoned as class members The largest increases were at Bold-street, Padgate, Sanke3, Bridges, and Stockton Meath. The member- ship in Warrington was gaining upon the popula- tion, and the circuit enjayed great peace and con- tinuous prosperity. The temperance and school work would be reviewed in June. Mr. S. B. Stringer read the totals of the Bands of Hope which were encouraging, showing about 1100 members. The four ministers were cordially thanked for their ser- vices, and invited to remain another year, Rev. J. Kirtlan for a third year, Revs. Owen Watkins and G. D. Mason for a second year,and Rev. Gunnell for a fifth year. All accepted the invitations. The re- commendations of the circuit invitation committee, that the Rev. Owen Watkins should become super- intendent in 1895, and the Rev. G. D. Mason second minister was cordially approved. The circuit stewards and Messrs. R. Cook and W. Silcock were appointed to represent the circuit at the next dis- trict synod. Mr. 11. 0. Brigg of Lymm was unani- mously recommended as a candidate for the minis- try, and he thus took the first step towards what was hoped to be his destined career by the suffrages of the laymen, without which there is no entrance into the Wesleyan ministry, except for those which have been ministers in other churches. Mr. Brigg, if accepted by the conference, will be in training for seven years before he is fully received into the ministry. The meeting was brought to a close by the pronouncing of benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>"LOST HIS HEAD WHILST FIGHTING</head>
        <p>"LOST HIS HEAD WHILST FIGHTING</p>
        <p>FOR HER MAJESTY."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DESPERATE CHARACTER AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>A DESPERATE CHARACTER AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning at the Runcorn Police Court before Messrs. F. J. Norman and S. Taylor, Daniel Gilmore, of Mason-st.; Runcorn, was charged under a warrant with disobedience of a summons on Monday for being drunk and di.orderly in Bridge-st. at 830 p.m. on the 10th inst., and with assaulting P.C. Richardson, whilst in the execution of , his duty on Tuesday morning.—With reference to the ' first charge, P.C. Gregory stated that the prisoner was fighting with two men and was using very disgraceful language.—P.C. Richardson deposed tirit on the previ- ous morning at about nine o'clock he was on duty in the police office and heard Gilmore knocking in the cells. Witness went to him and inquired what he wanted to which the prisoner replied " a drink." The officer asked for his can whereupon the prisoner struck him in the mouth with his fist at the same time using a filthy ex- pression. Subsequently he tried to strike him with the can but was prevented by P.S. Gill. Another prisoner was in the cell at the time and he also c ime in tor con- siderable ill-usage at the hands of the prisoner. The other prisoner was brought up before Mr. Handley on Tuesday and charged with begging, but owing to the assault committed upon him by the prisoner whilst in the cell he was dismissed.—Corroborative evidence was given by P.S. Gill who added that the prisoner was ex- ceedingly rough.—Gilmore informed the magistrates that he had been in India, Egypt, and South Africa, and had had a sunstroke so that when he took drink he be- came mad. He pleaded fdr leniency saying that he had a big family to support.—lnspector Cooper stated that the prisoner had not been living long in Runcorn but there was a bad record against him. His appearances numbered 21.—Mr. Johnson, deputy magistrates' clerk, said the offences included assaults upon his wife and son, and that the prisoner had already undergone nine months' imprisonment for attempted murder.—The pri- soner said he had lost his head whilst fighting for Her Majesty, and if the Bench would only deal leniently with him he would sign the pledge.—The Chairman said he was a most desperate character and everyone had to suffer through his partaking of drink. They had taken a very lenient view of the case and decided to fine him 20s. and 153. 4d. costs on the first charge or a month's imprisonment, whilst for the assault on the constable he_ would be fined 40s. and 7s. costs or an alternative of an other month in gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LITTLE SISTERS OF THE POOR.</head>
        <p>THE LITTLE SISTERS OF THE POOR.</p>
        <p>The Little Sisters of the Tl'ojr have much pleasure te bearing their testimony as to the efficacy of Anemic's Porous Plasters. They have seen great benefit derived from them on account of the relief they so quickly give the sufferer. They find them a very soothing remedy for all sorts of pains.— T.M.T. Home for the Aged Poor, Parkfield-avenue, Birkenhead. The REV. MARK GUY PEARSE says :—"I find them a very .breast plate against colds and coughs." Mr. HENRY THORNE (evangelist) says : I have used them for pains in the back and side arising from rheumatic and other causes, always deriving benefit from their application. They are easily applied and very comforting." Over twenty millions of Allcock's Plasters have already been sold in Great Britain, the most telling testimony to this great external remedy. Allcock's. Plasters stop all aches and pains and sprains and strains, Lumbago. Sciatica, Rheumatism, Ruptures, Bronchitis, Asthma, all affections of the Throat and Lungs, actin; directly on the tissues, dilating the pores of the skin, giving instant relief.</p>
        <p>ALLCOCK'S PLASTERS are of great efficacy in INDIGESTION if worn over right side of the Pit of the Stomach. ALLCOCK'S—Of all Chemists, 111 k.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEES.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the minutes of the last meetings of Halton, Appleton, and Frodslaam Parochial Committees, which were adopted by the Authority. It was unani- mously resolved at a meeting of the Halton Parochial Committee, that £lO be paid to the Clerk for extra ser- vices in acting for the Committee, iy.cluding his attend- ances, writing up minutes, obtaining urban powers, and framing bye-laws in respect thereof, and for extra legal and other work in relation to sewer outfalls. Inasmuch as the work performed by the Committee saved the time of the central Authority the remuneration so paid should be out of the common fund.—The Appleton Parochial Committee at their meeting of the 16th inst. unani- mously resolved that £25 be paid to the Clerk for extra services in acting as Clerk to the Committee, attending the meetings, writing up the minutes, obtaining urban powers. and framing bye-laws in respect thereof, and for legal and other work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CLOSING OF NORLEY SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE CLOSING OF NORLEY SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrcte referring to the complaint of the Rev. C. R.Nunn as to the closing of the Norley schools, and enclosed copies of the reports of the Medical Officer of Health upon which the action of the Sanitary Authority in closing the school in question was based.—The Clerk said that he had also received a letter from the Rev. C. R. Nunn, who therein stated that he was quite aware that the above question had been re- ferred to the Education Department. The resolution might have been considered as an adequate reply to Mr. Woodhouse, who exercised his right as an ex-officio Guar- dian to be present and to discuss a matter which affected him as one of the managers of the Norley school, and whose statement was calculated to temper satisfaction with some little feeling of regret, and the risk of illness among scholars and on the School Board (if considered so undesirable) was not lessened by a compliance with the requirements of the Educational Department made known nearly a year ago, and by compulsion become necessary. The resolution was not, however, an answer to his official inquiry namely," Whether or no the pre- sent supply of driaking water—which was actually in existence when it was asserted there was no water, and notice was issued to provide it be accepted as sufficient and the notice still in force be withdrawn." This matter, he had been informed by the Department. rested with the Authority, and as ex-officio chairman and official correspondent of the managers,he asked to be in- formed of the Authority's decision on this point. He thanked the chairman for having corrected a mis-state- ment ef his, and did not for a moment, charge the Authority with any desire to harass schools. The thanks a the public were due to those who discharged onerous and not always pleasant duties. but when powers with which they were entrusted, were used to close any schools, more especially those maintained by the liberality of a few, to the saving of the ratepayers, and which had so many difficulties to contend with, and moreover to close them on the strength of reports of sick- ness which were distinctly and publicly challenged as not true to facts,then he submitted with all due respect,that the Authority and their medical officer owed it, not only to the managers but to themselves to clear up the matter thoroughly. This not having been done, he was com- pelled,as a public duty,to follow up the untrue and harass- ing report until it was cleared.—The Rev. J. Hanby understood that their officer had served a notice with regard to a water supply which would take two months to mature.—The Chairman said they claimed to have a water supply, and wished to know if that furnished by a filter was sufficient.—The Rev. J. Hanby said their officer contended that there was no water supply at all. The wattr carried in a bucket was not sufficient. When the Education Department received the report they de- sired, they would go on with the whol e business. He suggested that they acknowledge Mr. Nunn's communica- tion. If Mr. Nune's explanation with reference to the water supply was satisfactory to the Education Depart- ment,they as an authority would also be satisfied.—The motion was seconded by Mr. Brandreth and carried.</p>
        <p>THE AUTHORITY AND THE _MANCHESTER SMIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>In submitting the report of the committee on the above Bill, Mr. Brandreth said he thought they ought to try and prevent the company obtaining the Act which when it became law would upset the existing agreement, be- cause the authority's sewers were provided before the canal, and they ought to have been made to go under the channel. The committee had instructed their clerk to write to the Ship Canal Company to that effect. There was another resolution with respect Co the clause in the Company's Bill,in which they desired not to come under the same regulations as other canals and railway COM- panies in accordance with the Act of 1888. The Coin- mitten recommended that their clerk ascertain the par- ticulars of the Act in question. He moved that the re- port be confirmed. Mr. Stokes seconded and it was agreed to.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth presented the estimate for the next half. year which showed that the sum of £236 was required. The rateable value of the district, according to the pre- sent valuation lists wai £166.095 and a rare of ird of a ld.in the £ would realize £230. He therefore moved that a new rate *rd of a id. In the £ be levied.—The motion was seconded by Mr. Stokes and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>DR. ADAMS RE-ELECTED MEDICAL OFFICER.</head>
        <p>DR. ADAMS RE-ELECTED MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. Williams, Dr. Joseph Adams was re appointed medical officer to the Authority for the ensuing year at the salary of £l2O per annum. THE CLERK'S REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth moved and Mr. Weir secnnded that the Clerk should be paid the usual sum of £B5 for his ser- vices during the past year to the Authority.—The motion was unanimously agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ATTENDANCE OF MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>THE ATTENDANCE OF MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the following list of attendances of members up to date for the past year :—W. H. J. Jen- kins, had attended 5 Board and Committee meetings; Arthur Greenway, 8 ; J. W. N. Tanner, 24; C. W. Daven- port, 28; Joseph Knowles, 18; John Parkinson. 22; E. Jackson, 3; John Mclntyre, 22; Joseph Stokes, 20: Jas. SN'hitlow, 5; Alfred Broome, 10; Thomas Baxter, 18; TI. E. Heywood, 66 ; J. M. Williama, 76; Thomas Lewis,42 ; James Hanby, 28; James Parr. $ ; James Brand reth, 47; Samuel Woodward,2B ; Wm. Ellison, 4; Samuel Horton, 7 ; J. L. Jones, U; E. Bretherton, 14; Joseph Hall, 5; Wm. Pickering, 10; J. W. Bagulev, 3; W. Whitlow. 10; J. Priestner. 1; C. T. Whitley, 32; J. E. Bate, 1: John Weir, 17; W. Turner, 5 ; E. Aaron, 34; Thomas Ford, 17; C. Mounfield, 7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ANOTHER CONFESSION OF MURDER AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER CONFESSION OF MURDER AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Robert. Wynne, last from Liverpdol, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday,vith being drunk and disorderly in Irlam-street on Sun- day morning. P.C. Am son stateta that at 12 20 en Sunday morning, the prisoner came into the police- station and said he wanted to see the chief constable. He was drunk at the time and was ordered out and told that the chief constable was net there. Shortly after- wards the prisoner commenced making a noise in the streets, and he (Amsnn) went out to him hut he refused to go away. He said that he wanted to be locked up, as he was wanted at Wigan for murder. He had killed Mr. Blaylock, of the Crofters Arms Hotel at Wigan. He turiher said that he and Blaylock were going along Powell-street when Blaylock struck him. He "drew " Blaylock off, and hit him on the head. He afterwards washed his hands in a brook, and alOi the stains of blood ' from his trousers. He had since been in St. Helens and Earlestown. He was uneasy in his mind, and wished to ~ive himself no because he wanted to be hung. The orficer said that irrespective of this statement he would h ,ve locked the prisoner up. On Sunday morning IA m- ann and Inspector Bowen examined the prisoner, and asked him as to the statement he had made during the night. He then denied having committe3 the murder, and said that he had only made the statement to be locked up. At the time the Wigan murder was com- mitted he said that he was serving two months in gaol for smashinga window in St. Helens.—A Wigan con- stable applied to the magistrates tor the prisoner to be handed over to the Wigan police. He said that he knew the prisoner by sight, but did not know his name. He did not think that he had been previouslY locked up.— Alderman Harrison : "He does not seem to be able to murder anybody. (Laughter.)—The prisoner was handed over to the Wigan police. Wynne, who was conveyed from Warrington to Wigan CO Monday, was brought be- fore the Wigan bench on Tuesday, and denied that he made such ft statement. What, he contended, he had said was that he should like to find the guilty party and get the £lOO. He.was warned to be more careful, and discharged.</p>
        <p>The body of an ell zentleman of independent mew, who had been murdered, was found in a box in a bowel in Grafton-street, London, on Satitrdiy afternoon. It was, it is believed, about to he s)nt off to Germany. A French-woman has been arrested.</p>
        <p>RUPTUSE CITR.I) WITROUT OPEIA7 10,47.—5. J. Fherrnan, Hernia Sreniali,t, 64. ChAnoery-Lane, London ; 26. King - street, Mat elierer ; and 4. Burlingto New-rtnet. Birmincti, Ilo,)k on rupture and evil of trussts, post free.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
