      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SACRED CONCERT AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>SACRED CONCERT AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>No doubt owing to the advent of Holy week, and to the fact that we were in the midst of Lent, a sacred concert, suitable to the season, was given in the Town Hall, Newton, the proceeds to be devoted to St. Peter's Parochial fund. The idea evolvea from the ever fertile brain of Mrs. Royse, and it is a pity ' that her commendable efforts to enhance the funds of the parisleand to place before the public music rich in theme and execution were not better apprecaeed. The hall was only partly filled,but those who were present, thorvgb- lv appreciated the items submitted for their delecta- tion. There cannot be any doubt that a vast amount of time and trouble has been expended to make the affair a musical success, an important feature in the programme being the performances of the Bijou Orchestra which acquitted itself with remarkable cleverness and preci- sion. This is the outcome of arduous labour on the part of Mrs. Royce, and the manner in which the members of the band acquitted themselves, warrants that lady in hoping that it may become a permanency. The pro- gramme put forth was without doubt a ech one, and contained items to satisfy the most fastidious in musica! matters. Opening with the hymn " 0 sacred Head, surrounded," which was sung by the audience standing, the quartette "Since by man" from the Messiah, was spiritedly taken up by Miss Percival, Mrs. Royce, Mr. H Smith and Mr.:Levien, the chorus" By man came also," being followed by the quartette " For as in Adam," concluding with the chorus" Even so in Christ." A violin solo "My heart, ever faithful," by Miss Ellis was excellently played. Mr. Hindley Smith gave a nice rendering of the sour "There is a green hill far away," but he was heard to much better advant- age in the song "The good Shepherd," for which he received a recall, but did not respond. A couple of piano duets" Allegro and Adagio," and "Funeral March," were well executed by Mr. and Mrs. Potter. Mr. J. M. Levien, very feelingly interpreted the air " It is enough" from Elijah, and the trio " Lift thine eyes,' was tastefully sung by Miss Percival, Mrs. Fairclough, and Mrs_ Royse. The recitation " Arse Elijah," Mrs. Royse and Mr. Levien, and air" 0 rest in the Lord," Mrs. Royse were well received, the quartette for female voices " The Lord is my Shepherd," was well executed, and the selection from Handel played by the Bijou Orchestra,was very finely submitted,excellent time being preserved throughout. An encore was given, but no response was made to the call. The composition of the band is as follows :-Ist violins, Miss Airey, Miss Dickin- son, Miss May Ellis, Mr. J. Lewis, and Mr. H. Penning- ton. 2ad violins, Miss Airey, Miss J. Seton, Mr. J.. Newburn Levien, and Mr.W. Turner. Violoncellos,Mrs. Handyside, and Mr. J. Owen. Organ, Mr. J. Potter. Piano, Mrs. Royse. Trombone, Mr. P. Aspinall. The song, "Ave Maria," by Mrs. Royce with orgen and violin 'accompaniment, was applauded, and the duet arid chorus' I waited for the Lord," was fairly well sung by Miss Kean and Miss Percival. A violin solo from the " Hymn of Praise," by Miss Airey received an encore. For the song, " Les Rameaex," Mr. J. Newburn Levien received a recall, and responded by repeating the last verse. The anthem "As pants the hart," was well given by Mr. Kean and the choir. The fir al selection by the Orchestra, " War in.lreii of t' e Pi i ..sts," was well rendered and much applauded. The following boy members of St. Peter's choir took part in the choruses ; Edwin Perry, Tilomas Gee, Albert Lyons, and Fred. Weeks. Mr. P. Fairclough officiated as coaductor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>DREADFUL TRAGEDY AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>DREADFUL TRAGEDY AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED ATTEMPTED MURDER OF A SWEET- HEART.</p>
        <p>SUICIDE OF THE LOVER.</p>
        <p>Shortly after nine o'clock on Tuesday night, the neighbourhood of Cowley Hill, one of the residen- tial districts of S. Helens, was alarmed by hearing several shots from a resolver indicating to even the most uninitiated ears that something of an extra- ordinary nature had occurred. Several persons at once sought for the police, and Mr. C. Sharples,J.P. personally conreye I the information to the Town Hall. Police Sergeant S:s•;on immediately proceeded to the place, and there .found a young unmarried man named Joscph Pickavane7, an apprentice glass inaker,at Messrs. Pilkington Brothers sheet g!ass works, lying on the ground bleeding from a terrible wound in the right temple. In his right hand he was tightly grasping a revolver. An examination showed that death had been instantaneous, and the body was removed by Police- sergeants Sisson and Moffat, and Police-constable Platt to the public mortuary. The body then presented a terrible sight, the bullet having entered the temple, and apparently lodged near the right eye. It was stated, though at the time of writing the rumour had not been confirmed, that the young man had quarrelled with a young woman with whom he had been keeping company and that having invited her to meet him in a lonely place near St. Mark's Church, he suddenly drew a revol- ver, presented it at her head and fired. The shots passed her in dangerous proximity, but so far as could be gathered she escaped unhurt. The young man then ran short distance, shot himself in the head, and fell to the ground. Police-constable Platt, who lives in Chapel-st. about 200 yards away, heard two shots fired and a scream, and he at once went to the place. Further inquiries show that Pickavance,the dead man was about eighteen years of age; and the girl, whoso. name was Elizabeth Johnson, was about the same age. She was a domestic servant. They had been courting about twelve months,and she had given birth to a child. He was, of course, questioned as to what assistance he intended to give her, but his wages were small, and he was in a dilemma. On Tuesday morning he sent her word that if she would meet him near St. Mark's Church at nine o'clock on Tuesday night, he would arrange matters. She proceeded to the place carrying her baby and had some conversation with him. He appeared to be somewhat strange, and on turning to go away she heard the click of a revolver, She lifted her hand to protect her liead,and was struck by a butlet,which pene- trated the hand, and also injured the neck. She fell to the ground, and Piekavance, believing doubtless, that he had killed her, ran a few yards, and then dischargeu the contents of another chamber into his right temple. Several persons who had been attracted to the place took the injured woman to her sister's house in Freder- ick-st., where Dr. Wallace (from the surgery of Drs. Twyford, Reid, and Masson) was promptly in attend- ance. It is believed that she has the bullet either in her hand or neck, but an examination was not made or., Tuesday night. The injured parts were bandaged, and orders were g,iveu to keep the patient as quiet and as free from excitement as possible. The revolver was a rather old weapon, and had six chamberz,three of which were loaded, and three discharged. The injured girl was removed on Wednesday afternoon to the Vrovidence Hospital, where Dr. Reid and Dr. Wallace examined her injuries. The bullet was found embedded in the scalp, and it had evidently passed through the girl's hand. The girl is expected to recover although similar injuries have been known to take a. bad course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES AND CONTRACTING OUT.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES AND CON- TRACTING OUT.</p>
        <p>The annual Conference of Friendly Societies was opened at the Foresters' Ball, Clerken well on Wednesday,, and various matters of interest to the Society were dealt with. On the motion of Mr. T. Bailan Stead a resolution condemning the contracting-out clause of the Employers' Liability Bill was passed, with one dissentient ; but a proposal to send a deputation to the Home Secretary to urge the reintroduction of the bill was negatived by eleven votes to sir.</p>
