      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ERANCHES EVERYWHERE,</head>
        <p>ERANCHES EVERYWHERE,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. MARCH 24, 1894.</p>
        <p>LORD ROSEBERY has seriously dis- The New Trernier. appointed the Unionist party. A considerable proportion of the intel- Ligent people who follow Lord SALISBURY and the uuke of DEVONSHIRE had, by a subtle process of 31f-delusion, persuaded themselves that Lord Itostßurn( while accepting the leadership of the Liberal party was about to abandon some of the Inest vital articles in the faith of the party. These ~Pe°Ple professed to think well of the now Premier, but 1t was a very poor compliment that they paid nirn• Lord ROSEBURY has taken the very earliest °Prlertunity of dissipating the ridiculous notions Winch some not altogether disinterested parties have carefully cherished. As he said in his fine "eech at Edinburgh on Saturday, he does not Intend to imitate the tactics of the Saxon army at the battle of Leipsic, which went over with all its arms and accoutrements from the side of Napoleon to that of the Allies. His idea of the duty he °w!s to the party and the policy with which he has been connected during</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>the whole of his political life is of a higher order than that ; and he has made it clear that he accepts in the fullest sense the creed ,of pro- gress that is embodied in the Liberal programme. This seemed clear enough before to all who had followed Lord ROSIMBERY'S career, but in view of recent events and of the attempts that have been made to confuse and mislead it will be all the clearer for the additional emphasis it received from Lord ROSEBBRY himself on Saturday. On the Irish question, in particular, some not over scrupulous attempts have been made to raise dis- sensions between Lord ROSEBERY and a part of his supporters. It is now, however, quite evii!ent that the present Premier has no more intention to minimise the urgency and importance of the Irish question than had his predecessor. He may have arrived at the conclusion that Ireland has grievances that can only be adequately redressed by an ample measure of self-government in a way somewhat different from that by which many other Liberals have been brought to a similar conclusion, but the result is the same. Home Rule is with him a great question of policy which must be settled in order that the Government of the Empire may be carried on satisfactorily ; and; it should be borne in mind when we are told that there are various kinds and degrees of Home Rule, that there can be no settlement that is not satisfactory to the great bulk of the Irish people. When, therefore, Lord ROSERERY contemplates the settlement of the Irish question he must of necessity mean the conferring of a degree of self- government upon Ireland which will be considered worthy of acceptance by the Irish people. Other- wise, there can be no settlement, and the Irish question will remain as great a danger to the Empire as it has ever been. With regard to the House of Lords, Lord ROSEBERY candidly confesses that he is a "second chamber man." But the House of Lords is not the kind of second chamber that he desires. It does not perform an yof the functions that should be expected from a rationally consti- tuted second chamber. When the Tories are in power it does not revise any of the measures that are sent up to it no matter how harmful they may be; but when the Liberals hold office it exercises its revising at d vetoing powers unsparingly, and with very little regard to the popular demands. Such a Chamber can not be allowed to exist in these days, and really it is not now a question as to the merits or d emerits of the Clouse of Lords, but as to the best method of abolishing or reform- ing it. This is a knotty con stitutional ques- tion and it can only be solved by a strong and unmistakeable demand from the people of the 1 country. Lord ROSEBERY asks for the inspiration of such a demand, and we believe that ere many months are over ho will receive all that he asks for.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE people all the world The Maine over have this week done honour Liquor Law. to the "Father of Prohibition," for by that name General NEAL Dow is known. This veteran temperance reformer was born on March 20,1804—n0 fewer than ninety years ago —at Portland, Maine, U.S.A., and for more than half a century he has been a prominent figure in the temperance movement. He was the chief author of the famous and much-det)ated Maine Prohibitory Liquor Law, by which the sale of liquor is completely prohibited. This law has, of course, been very adversely criticised in many quarters, but it is an undoubted fact that, al- though passed so far back as 1851, it still remains in force. And, not only does it still remain in force, but in the State which has enjoyed for over forty years the benefits it conferred, there is prac- tically no demand for its reversal. It has, in fact, in spite of the evasions that have been tolerated by a lax administration in certain districts, been a , ! decided success. General NEAL Dow himself, in ! an interesting ;otter to Miss Willard, says that in more than three-fourths of the State, contain- ing more than three-fourths of the population, the traffic is practically unKnown. Taking the com- munity as a whole,a generation has sprung up who have never seen a drinking saloon or the effects of one. From a material point of view the change has been of the most encouraging character. From being one of the poorest States in the American Union, Maine has become one of the most prosperous. This is in the highest degree encouraging to the temperance reformers in this country. They are striving to secure for localities the prohibitory powers which the Maine legislature exercised on its own behalf, and they may reason- ably.contend that the results which will follow a pretty extensive use cf the veto will be as good her as on the other side of the Atlantic. The American experiment is, at the very least, prima facie evidence of the wisdom and justice of the demand for prohibition in England; just as the mental and physical vigour of General NEAL Dow at four score years and ten, may be said to be one more demonstration of the advantage of abstinence from intoxicating liquors to the individual.</p>
        <p>THE feelings of the Liberals of War. - Mr. Gladstone. rinoton with regard to the retire- _ _</p>
        <p>m3nt of Mr. Gladstone were very fittingly voiced by Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Mr. T. Birchall at the meeting in the Liberal Club on Monday evening. The great statesman has no- where had admirers more ardent or devoted than in Warrington, and the regret expressed in the resolution which was moved and seconded by the two gentlemen named has been widely felt amongst Liberals in this locality. Fortunately, although Mr. Gladstone has relieved himself of the immense responsibilities which the office of a Prime Minister entails ho still remains, and, it is to be hoped, will nmain for many years to come a great personal force in the country. A letter which he has written this week to Sir John Cowan, of Mid-Lothian, affords ample proof that he retains all his mental breadth and keenness, and, what is more, all the old enthusiasm for social and political progress. It seems clear,too, that he has not as yet formed any definite decision to retire from Parliament. We gather from the letter that it will depend largely upon the condition of his sight or hearing whether or not he retains his seat for Mid-Lothian. Considering the superb phy- sical constitution with which he has been endowed and the wonderful powers of recuperation he has always displayed, it seems not at all improbable that he may yet perform notable services in the Parliamentary arena.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>BILLIARD MATCH.</head>
        <p>BILLIARD MATCH.</p>
        <p>A match at billiards 1,000 up for £lO a side was concluded on Tuesday evening between Mr. R. Bennett of Earlestown and Mr. W. Woods of War- rington. , The first half of the game was played at the Prince of Wales hotel, Earlestown, on Monday night, and the concluding half at the White Bull hotel, Warrington. Bennett ultimately winning by 83 points.</p>
      </div>
