        <p>OAKLAND-STREET WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCROOL, IDNES. On Tuesday evening an entertainment was given in the above school by the Widnes Victoria entertainment party, who acquitted themselves in a most praiseworthy manner. The proceeds will be devoted towards the bunday school fund. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker aid Jeweller, 51, Higk- st., Ituncern.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CONFIRMATION SERVICE AT THH RUNCORN PARISH</p>
        <p>CHURCH.</p>
        <p>- The Bishop of Chester (the Right Rev. Dr. Jayne) con- ducted a confirmation service at the Parish Church, Runcorn, on Wednesday evening. A numerous congre- gation assembled in the church to witness the ceremony, and the candidates,who numbered about 130,were from the following churches :—St. Michael's, Holy Trinity, Weston, Weston Point, Halton, and also the Runcorn branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen.</p>
        <p>lOSEPTI PRITCHARD, 51, High-street, Runcorn, keeps SPECTACLR'S to suit all sights, from shlling s pair.—Adat.</p>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE MEETING AT ANCOUN.</p>
        <p>The closing meeVng of the session in connection with the Brunswick-st., Rand of Hope Society was held on Monday evening, when there was a good attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. E. Marshall who was supported on the Wattorm by Messrs. A. R. Norman, D. Bisbrown, and T. Cooke,. The proer =me was of a diverthig nature, and included a violin solo by Miss Smith, for will* she was recalled, and a pianoforte solo by Mr. John Bisbrown. A brief address was delivered by Mr. Cooke,and the remainder of the proceedings was thoroughly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>When ha want of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WF.nnism. or BIRTHDAY PRESLiNT :U to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-street. - Air&amp;</p>
        <p>POLICE CHANGES AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The public of Widnes will be very pleased to hear that Inspector Strickland. who was promoted from the rank of Sergeant last week, is to remain in Widnes ; but they will no doubt regret tilt Inspector Pattison, who came to Widnes two years ago, lips been transferred to With- ington, in the Manchester hi vision. This has been done at Inspector Pattison's Nquest as he was anxious for a change in consequence of the health of his daughter. Constable Fritwell, of the Bolton Division, has been pro- moted to the tank of sergcant and drafted to Widnes.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS, RUNCORN: The members of the Brunswick Mutual Improvement Class mustered in full force at the weekly meeting on Wednesday evening in the Brunswick school to hear an essay delivered by Mr. J. H. Itcktiiall on " Photogra- phy." Mr. T. Simister pretided. Tne essayist dealt rather lengthily with the subject, and by his lucidity of expression mever failed to drive home the inform.ition he desired to imp Art. By way of in aking himself better understood he made several experiments on the platf;,,rin one of which was that of photographing the president. The proceedings werevery much enjoyed by all present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING AT _WIDNES.</head>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING AT _WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, several children were playing near the river when one of them, a little boy named William T. Roberts, of 58 Beaumont•street got into Ow water. There was a strong tide running at the time and the little fel- low was carried a great distance upstream. The atten- tion of Mr. Richard Mercer was called to the accident and he at one got into the water and suceeeded in rescu- ing the child, who was taken to the Accident Hospital where the usual remedies for recovery were applied by Dr. Donnelly. The child is now making satisfactory progress.</p>
        <p>- G. to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-st., Runcorn, for tits best FIVE GUINEA WATCH a working man can have. SUDDEN DEATH AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A young woman named Alice Maud Deakin, thitd daughter of Mr. Thomas Deakin, of the Simms Cross Hotel, died very skiddenly on Sunday morning. The deceased was 20 years of age,and had enjoyed very good health, but on Saturday morning she was taken ill with pains in her stomach. As she did not improve,Dr.Allen was sent for. He prescribed for her, but not not think the case serious. On Sunday morning about half-past si4 the servant, who slept with Miss Deakin, got up,but did not notice anything wrong. Shortly after nine o'clock it was found that she was dead, and on the doc- tor being called in, he gave his opinion that deceased had been dead some hours. Death was attributed to cramp at the heart following interitis colic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ARREARS OF RATES AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ARREARS OF RATES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Runcorn Improvement Com- missioners was held at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Tuesday evening, Mr. Samuel Taylor occupying the chair. There:were also present—Messrs. Bishrown,Lea, Imison, Hankinson, Lightburn, Meadowcroft, and Col- lier, and Dr.Mangan. The business transacted was that of wiping off irrecoverable arrears and closing the ac- counts for the half-year. It was decided that the irre- coverable arrears of the second instalment of the general district rate amounting to £99 7s. be allowed,ancl.it was exolained that the amount in the previous half-year was £7B. The arrears were almost entirely due to empty property. It was resolved that cheques be drawn for the following amounts :—General fund,£4,o9o 143. 101; loan account, £42 7s. 9 1 ; district fund, £8 ; technical instruction account, £4 194. 61 ; private street works, £484 63 8.1.; library fund, £37 lls. 11d. ; water account, £422 16s. 11d.—This terminated the business. PRITCHARB'S have now stood the test for fifty years. and sosality and first class worlirn anship are still guaranteed. MIA,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BANKRUPTCY OF A RUNCORN GROCER</head>
        <p>BANKRUPTCY OF A RUNCORN GROCER</p>
        <p>rhe fllo wing information is given in Tuesday's " Ga- zette " : Anne Johnson, trading as Johnson and Co., St. Helens and Runcorn, grocer: The. court having required the bankrupt, as a condition of her discharge, to consent to judgment being entered against her by the trustee for the sum of £430, being part of the balance of the debts provable in the bankruptcy, which is not satiq- fled at the date tier of, and the bankrupt having paid the said sum of £430 to the trustee, this court thinks fit to revoke such requirement, and it is ordered that she be and she is hereby dischar.,:ed ; bankrupt's assets are not of a value equal to ten shinings in the pound on the amount of her unsecured liabilities ; that she had omit- td to keep such books of account as are usual and pro- per in the business carried on by her, and so sufficiently disclise her business tranmetions and financial position within the three years immediately preceding her bank- ruptcy, and had continued to trade after knowing be:- self to be insolvent.</p>
        <p>QUARTERLY MEETING OF THE RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. Wilkes presided over the quarterly meet- ing of the Runcorn Wesleyan circuit on Monday evening in the Bounswick-street vestry. There was also present the Revs. W. Arrowsmith, and J. Hanby, the circuit stewards and several district representatives. An epi- tome of the work which was being carried on in Halton was submitted, and gave rise to the expression of much satisfaction. From the financial statement it is apparent that the expenditure had exceeded the receipts bv about £4-. The circuit stewards reported that they had received a small balance from Mrs. Philip Speakman. This was a result of the recent bazaar after all expenses incurre I in re-furnishing Greenway-road house had been paid. There was an increase of six during tke quarter in the membership, 690 now being the total. The Sun- day school, day school, and temperance societies annual schedules, presented by the Rev. Hanby showed that each department was accomplishing a good work These were adopted. The meeting decided to ask the Rev. James Brackenbury of Huddersfield to take the sunerin- tendancy, and the Rev. Wm. George of Lancaster, the second ministership. The choice of a third minister was left for a future period. With regard to the sug- gested alteration in the location of ministers in the cir- cuit, the cemnaittee recommended that no change should at present be made. BRITISII AND FOREIGN_ BIBLE SOCIETY: MEETING AT</p>
        <p>WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A public meeting was held on Monday evening in the Parochial Rooms,Widnes,under the auspices of the above qociety. There was a large attendance, Mr. Stuart Deacon, 8.L., presided, and was supported by the fol- lowing :—The Revs. W. Flodgson, M.A., C. J. Preston, W. E. Lead, W. Stanton Jcnes, F. Barnes, 8.A., Messrs. F. Neil and F. Lawrinson. Mr. Deacon in his opening remarks. said he was pleased to take part in that meet- ing. lie urged the claims of the society on Christian people, and also urged them to help in the great work it was doing. Mr. F. Neil (the local treasurer of the society) said the total amount sent from Widnes last year was £ll 16s. 5. Though this showed an increase on the previous year, he thought it was still poor for a place like Widnes. He urged all to become annual sub- scribers, as by the said of the society the bible had been translated into 313 different languages and dialects. He proposed the adoption of the report.—The Rev.F.Barnes, of Lhester, who attended as a deputation, in seconding the adoption of the report, said the society had been established 89 years, and during that time 4,497,056 copies of the bible had been sent out to foreign lands. There had also been 135 million copies of various por- tions of scripture sent out. He then gave a very inter- esting speech describing the work of the society and the great amount of good it was doing.—The Rev. %V. Hodg- son then gave a very interesting address on "The Torn Bible," or" Huhert's Best Friend," which was illustrated by limelight views carefully manipulated by Mr. John Robinson. The customary voti,s of thanks followed. The collection taken in aid of the society amounted to £2 03. 10d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>TUE EX NITNE", SATURDAY, MATICH 24 1894.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL ASSIZES,</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at these assiz,-s..ttichard Tonkies. aged 28, labourer, was found not guilty of having assaulted Annie Poole, at Widnes, on the 27th Nov. last, and was discharged.</p>
        <p>THE BAZAAR AT HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>The total receipts at the two days' bazaar held in Hill Cliffs Baptist. Schoolroom last week amounted to a little over £lBO. Getx Thus.s.—GARLAND Ironmonger, Warrington and Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>GOOD FRIDAY AT WIDNES</head>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY AT WIDNES</p>
        <p>Good Friday was observed in Wiones as a general h(vliday amongst tradesmen, but work was not totally suspended at most of the large works. Special services were held in several places of worship and were well attended.</p>
        <p>THE BILLIARD TOURNAMENT AT THE RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Much interest is being still manifested in the competi- tion of billiards at the Liberal Club, wh;ch has now been in progress for about three weeks. At the commence- ment of the tournament there were thirty-two competi- tors but this number is now considerably reduced. So keen is the contest at present that it would be unsafe or premature to make any prediction as to who will turn out the ultimate prize winners. THE NEW TECHNICAL INSTITUTE FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Such satisfactory progress has been made in excavat- ing the ground so as to receive the stone, that it has now been definitely settled that the ceremony of laying the foundation stone shall take place on Saturday the 14th April. Our member for the division,Mr.J.T. Brun- .ner,has kindly expressed his pleasure by letter at having been chosen to perform the ceremony, and has promised to do so should nothing unforeseen at present occur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>THEFT OF_AN UMBRELLA AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THEFT OF_AN UMBRELLA AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court, on Thursday morning, a girl named Sarah Ann floug,hton, 17 years of age, was charged in custody with having stolen at Runcorn on the 19. h Drcember, 1893, an umbrella valued 10s., the property of Pncebe Dunn.—Prosecutrix stated that she was an assistant at the Dolph Bridge Post Office, Run- corn.—lnspec,tor Cooper infcrmel the Bench that the prisroner had been convicted of stealim„,, a gold watch chain on the 9z.li o.;tober, 1893.—Committed to linuts- ford gaol for twenty-one days with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>At.LhOED Itomw.riv By JUVENILES AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>At.LhOED Itomw.riv By JUVENILES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Oft Tfittrs, ac morning at the Rtmw• rn lioe Court, thr, e girls 'imbed Mary Bradbury, liarrison, and Beatrice Burgess, aged 14, 14 and 12 years respec- tively, were charged with stealing from the shop of John Peebles, boot and shoe dealers, Church-street, Run- corn, on the previous day, one pair of shoes and an odd shoe of the total value of 6s. 81.—Mr. John Peebles having given evidence, Inspector Cooper applied for a remand until Tuesday next in order, if possible, to enable the police to recover the stolen property.—The application was granted, prisoners being allowed bail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BETHESDA LITERARY AND SOCIAL CLASS, RUNCORN</head>
        <p>BETHESDA LITERARY AND SOCIAL CLASS, RUNCORN</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Simpson, through indisposition, could not deliver his promised lecture on " Burns," at the weekly meeting of the above class on Tuesday eveniniz. The secretary of the class, however, kindly acted in his stead, by giving an able reading from Sir John Lub- bock's work on "Books," followed by Longfellow's dramatic poem " Robert of Sicily.' Both items were delivered in a most capable manner and deeply interested the audience. The Bev. J. Simpson will give his lectute on " Burns " if possible, on Tuesday next, and will illus- trate it with numerous extracts from the poet's woiks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above committee was held on Wednefday evening at the Town Hall, Runcorn, Mr. IL J. Grice occupying the chair. The only other mem- ber of the committee present was Mr. S. Pope. The Librarian, Mr. D. V. Jones, reported that during the month ot February, 1884 books had been issued out of the lending department, and 44 out of the reference department, being a decrease of 20 on the former, and an increase of seven on the latter. Twenty of the vol- umes Int treated upon theology ; nine philosophy ; 106 science ; 158 history Szc. ; 23 poetry ; 1398 fiction ; anti 170 miscellaneous. Those volumes out of the reference deparinent were all of a miscellaneous nature. Mr. Jones also reported the receipt of several books as gifts to the library.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>STRANGE CONDUCT OF A WIDNES YOUTH.</head>
        <p>STRANGE CONDUCT OF A WIDNES YOUTH.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Tuesday, a young man, of 10 James-st., Wet Bank, who gave the name of William Wall Ward Morris, was charged with being a wanderhig lunatic.—P.C. Davidson stated that he found the prisoner in Mill Brow, on Tuesday morning about seven o'clock, behaving in a very peculiar manner. In court Morris acted in a very extraordinary manner and was wearing a volunteer artillery man's cap. He said he was a gentleman of leisure and was 3harged with elntricity. He was, he said, the strongest man on earth, and made an unsuccessful attempt to pull the rail of the defendant's box away. Mr. Hampson (one of the relieving officers) he persisted in calling " Mr. Edison." The magistrates ordered prisoner's removal to the Work- house for 14 days. The prisoner did a step dance in court before being removed below.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CONGREGATIONALISTS' "AT HOMIL" On Wednesday evening a most enjoyable "At Home," in connection with tne Bethesda Congregational Church was held in the Betheida Lecture Hall, Runcrri. The interior of the building was very tastefully decorated by Mr. J. Steele, the windows being hung with curtains of various pretty tints. There was a large gathering of Con- gregational friends,and the proceedings were of a bright and cheerful nature. The programme consisted of piano- forte solos by Misses L Ainsworth, Forrester. and Car t- wright ; songs, Misses Johnson and Ainsworth, and Mr. E. Worrall ; vocal duets, the Misses Heatherington and the Misses Temple ; pianoforte duets, Misses Ainsworth and Cartwright ; Mr.l3. Outhwaite contributed a clario- nette solo ; Misses Ainsworth and Johnson, and Messrs. Ainsworth and Worrall two quartettes, and Miss Rogers a recitation. The following, ladies kindle prepared tea : Mesdames Royle, BaJcliffe, and Johnson, and Miss Lrdiate.</p>
        <p>OAKLAND-STREET WESLEYAN IMPROVEM F.NT SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>IDNES.</p>
        <p>The final meeting of the session, in connection with this Society, was held on Friday evening. A suhsta.n- tial tea had been prepared to which a large num her sat down. An entert liniment followed, which was presided over by Mr. J. Newburn. The following programme was gone through in an admirahle manner :—Pianoforte solo, Master W. A. Hague; song, " Measure your Wants to your means," Mr. P. Davies (encored) ; song, "Chil- dren's Home," Mrs. Locke ; recitation, " Virginius," Mr. J. Anbers ; song, "Do your duty boys," Mr. W. Hague; violin solo, Mr. R. Sweet (encored) ; son,, " Life is a River,' Mr. S. Thomas; duet, "I know a bank," Mrs. Locke and Miss Forrest; song, "'rue old woman and her pig," Mr. T. Davies (twice encored) ; song," Ora pro nohis,' Miss Forrest (encored) ; song, Mr. S. Pope. Masters %V. A. Hage and T. W. Stanley accompanied the singing very efficiently. Votes of thanks to the enter- tainers brought a most enjoyable evening to a close.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY NIGHT'S CONCERT, WIDNES. Another of the concerts, promoted by the United Alkali Co., was held in the Blths Assembly Rooms, Widnes, on Saturday night. There was a large attend- ance. An excellent programme had been arranged and was ably sustained by employes from Messrs. Sullivan's works. The following were the items : Song, "The l'..)wder Monkey," Mr. F. Wilkinson (encored) ; concer- tina solo. Mr. G. Plumpton ; song. "My sweetheart when a boy," Mr. J. Monks (encored) ; humorous song. Mr. H. F. S. Bout!, (encored). ; trio, " Dame Durden,'' Mes,rs. Motdo, Ashall and Wilkinson ; song and dance, "The O'Gallaghans," Bros. Burke (encored); song, "The Old Turn-key," Mr. 'l'. Hanby (encored) ; song, " Norah the pride of Kildare," Mr. J. Monks ; humorous song. Mr. H. F. S. Boult (encored) ; song, " Harbour Lights," Mr. W Hague (encored) ; song," The'Midshipman," Mr. F. Wilkinson ; humorous sketch by the Bros. Burke. Mr. E. M. Harrison was an able accompanist.</p>
        <p>RuNcourr SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. The Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, presided over the annual meeting of the Runcorn School Attendance Committee, held on Tuesday morning at the Workhouse, Dutton. Four candidates ror the position of school attendance officer or Weston presented themselves and eventually Mr. J me Cooper, of Mersey View was elected. The following school attendance officers were re-elected : Mr. George Jones, Frodshain district, salary £35 ; Mr. F. A. Jenkins, Iludworth district, salary £3O ; and Mr. Samuel Toinlinson,Halton district, £l5. The clerk en lA- mitted his estimates for the Michaelmas half-year 1894. The balance in hand for Michaelmas 1893 was £102; extiinated expenditure this Lady Day half year £75 ; probable balance Lady Day 1894 £27 ; estimated expen- diture, salaries £B3 ; books, postage, and legal expenses £2O ; balance and contingency fund, £6O ; probable balance £27-19 ; amount require 4 £136. The rateable value of the district as per valuation lists in force £111,626, and a rate of one fourth of a penny in the £ would realize £ll6. It was decided to lay a rate of /in the £ for the ensuing year. ORFORD WARD ELECTION EXPENSES.</p>
        <p>The expenses of Councillor John Baird, the Con- servative candidate at the recent election in the above ward, have been returned to the Town Clerk at .£lB 6s. 4d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BURTONWOOD SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>BURTONWOOD SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the above board was held in the classroom of the Board schools on Tues- day evening, There were present Messrs. James Warburton (chairman),John IVarourton (vice chair- man), Thomas Norcott, Thomas Perks, and William Fairclough ; H. Gawthorpe (schoolmaster), John Forster (school attendance officer),and Wm. Webster (clerk). It was stated that A. Ilewitt had passes his first year's certificate examination in the third division. The resignation of Miss Houghton, , the infants' mistress,was received. The triennial report was considered and adopted. At the conclusion of the business a vote of thanks to the chairman and vice chairman for their services during the past three years was proposed by Mr.Fairclough,seconded by Mr. Perks, and carried with acchtmation. The Chairman in reply thanked the members for the kind sentiments expressed towards him; and ex- pressed the great pleasure it always gave him to meet them; and his thanks to the stuff for their conscientious discharge of their duties.—Toe vice- Chairman having also replied, expressed the hope that they might all meet again after the elec- tion, which was the first contested eltction since . the formation of the Board in 187 G.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>We would again remind our readers that the per- formance of" Sweet Lavender" takes place next Thursday and Friday, and the opportunity of wit- nessing this charming piece, which will be played for the first time in Warrington, should not be missed. We feel sure that the patrons of the Society will feel gratified by the manner in which the play will be set before them both as far the acting and staging are concerned. The caste is an exceptionally strong one, and judging from the merits of the last performances, an excellent entertainment may be looked forward to. The stage is so arranged as to permit a full view of the whole scene from every part of the house. The plan of the dress stalls, second seats, and balcony, is now open at Mr. Pear- son's, Bold-st., and we would recommend those who have not already done so to book at once. Further particulars will be found in our advertising columns. UMBRELLAS RE-COVERED,—J. and W. DurrToN, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Ad• t. PRESENTATION TO AN Ex-POLICEIIAN.</p>
        <p>Ex-police constable Fleming, who for some years has been'stationed at Padgate, has been presented with a beautiful marble clock, which has been sub- scribed fqr by the members of the Warrington Divison of the Lancashire County Constabulary on the occasion of. his retirement from the force on superannuation. P.C. Fleming some time ago met with a serious accident, and received a compound fracture of the knee, which occasioned his retire- ment. During his residence in Padgate, he had won the respect and confidence of the inhabitants. No public presentation was made, owing to Mr. Flem- ing being unable to undergo the railway journey to Padgate. TI-,e clock, which is of marble with a gold cencre-piece, was supplien by Mr. E. V. Morton, of Winwick-street. It bears a silver plate with the inscription " Presented to P.C. Fleming by the members of the Warrington Division of the Lanca- shire County Constabulary, on the occasion of his retirement on superannuation. January, 1894."</p>
        <p>REL'ADLE " KID GLOVE, in all shade 3, 2s 6d. per pair. EvANs', 17 Sankey-st.-- dvt.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM BUSBY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the remains of Mr. William Busby who oied on the previous Wednesday after a short illness, were interred in the Smithdown .Road Cemetery, Liverpool. The deceased gentleman was the founder of the firm of undertakers, Messrs. W. and D. Busby, Limited, which has existed for about 50 years. He was also closely identified with the interests of the tramway and other vehicular ser- vice of Liverpool and elsewhere. At the time of his death he was vice-chairman of he Liverpool Tram- ways Company, a director of the Warrington Car- riage Company, and a director of various tramway companies on the Cheshire side of the Mersey. The chief mourners at the funeral were Mr. W. Busby (son), Miss Busby (daughter), Mr. R. A. Busby (son), Mrs. PaNuhar (daughter), Mr A. K. Busby (son), Miss A. Busby (daughter), Mr. Herbert Busby and Mr. Sidney Busby (grandsons), Sir D. Radcliffe (chairman of the Liverpool Tramways Company), Mr. T. R. Lee, J.P. (chairman of the Wirrall and Wallasey Tramway Company), Mr. T. W. Wright, Nfr. G Swift (director of the Warrington Carriage Company, Limited), Mr. T. Wright (director of the Chester Tramway Company), the Rev. Manners Saunderson (vicar of Huyton), Dr. H. Gorst. Mr. J. Warburton (director of the Warrington Carriage Company), Mr. J. O'Neil (secretary of the Liverpool Tramways Company), and Mr. J. A.S. Hassal (secre- tary of the Wirral Tramways Company). The coffin was of panelled oak, and was covered with a large number of handsome wreaths, amongst the senders being the employes of the Warrington Carriage Company, and Mr. James Warburton (Dallarai). The interment took place in the family grave, which was lined with ivy, and the Rev. M. Sannderson performed the funeral ceremony. The coffin bore the inscription, " William BusV, died 14th March, 1894, aged 89 years."</p>
        <p>THE LATEST STYLES in Mantles, Jackets and Capes. EveNs', 17 Sankey-st.—Adtt.</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD SCHOOL BOARD-TRIENNIAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The following report of the above namod board was presented at a meeting on Tuesday evening. "Within the last three years the passing of the Ele- mentary Education Act 1891 enabled the Board to abolish the school fees. Though this entailed a slight reduction in the receipts during the iirst year the increased attendance im the infants school and consequently the increased grant earned, ha l made up for any aoparent loss. A penny bank was estab- lished in 1891 at the end of which year the deposits amounted to £l4' G., at the end of 1892 liev had</p>
        <p>increased to £l6 14s. Id.,and at the end of 1893 had decreased (probably owing to the coal strike) to £IO2. Bd. The number of depositors being 32. Drawing is now taught to the boys who have twice passed a fair examination. The playground wall has been rebuilt, new offices added to the schools, and the classrooms have been ventilated. These repairs and alterations necessitated a slight increase in the rate whiah has been as follows: 1891, 21. ;</p>
        <p>1892, 2,4944. ;1893, 34884. ; and for the half year just ended id. in the -C. In the Autumn of last year a public inquiry into the title of the school was held before Mt. Darnford,Assistant Charity Commissioner The Board was represented at such inquiry and supported the intentions of the trustees. The mat- ter is still under the consideration of the Commis- • sioners. Several changes have taken place in the staff. By the death of the Inv. Wm. Wilson, on October 28th 1891, the Board lost, the services of their clerk who was appointed to ~hat o=flice on the 28th November 1878, on the death of Mr. Thomas Southern, the first clerk to the Board, and had been intimately connected with School Board work in the district from a short time after the formation of the Board in 1876. On the 10th November 1891, the present clerk was appointed. Miss Sharpies the infants mistress resigned after eight years ser- vice and Miss Annie Houghton was appointed in her place. The pupil teachers (Miss Norcott and A .Hewitt) have successfully completed their course. A. Hewitt remains on the board as assistant master. The school staff now consists of the head master. one assistant master, infants' mistress, two pupil t.,achers, and a monitress."</p>
        <p>HOT-WATER HF:ATING,—GARLAND, Ironmonger, Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. W. PILLING.</p>
        <p>Many Warringtonians will hear with regret of the death of the Rev. W. Pilling, of Ulverston, who was from 1878 to 1885 pastor of Hill Cliffs Baptist Chapel. The Rev. J. S. Hughes, made allusion to the death on Sunday, at 11111 Cliffe Chapel. At the afternoon service he said an element of P adn ess had entered into the joyful experience of the past week, reminding them very forcibly that in the midst of life they. were in death, in the knowledge that one who had often stood there and ministered to them in holy things, and a dear personal friend, had passel away. The distressing news came some- what unexpectedly, for they had hoped that Mr. Pilling had passed through the critical r. eri od of Ins sickness, and was likely to regain his strength, but the gracious Lord—who doeth all things well— had taken him to Himself, and they mourned the loss of a good and noble man. He (Mr. Hughes) had known Mr. Pilling for nearly twenty years. Mr. Pilling entered Manchester College just a few months after him and from that moment they be- came closely interested in each other. Mr. Pilling was the son of humble parents. His father was a respected deacon of the Zion Baptist Church,Bacup, ' under the ministry of the Rev. D. Davies. Mr. Pilling joined the church at Zion, and at once be- came a power for good to the church, labouring incessantly in the Sunday school and other Chris- tian organisations, and was soon known as an ear- nest and acceptable local preacher. He was urged to enter college, and did so in 1874, remaining at the Manchester College for three years, when he settled at Hill Cliffe,and after seven years of devoted service, lie left for Great Broughton, Cumberland, and from there he went to Ulverston, where he had laboured successfully for over six years. Mr. Pill- ing was endowed with mental gifts of no mean order, and was generally considered an earnest and effective preacher. His character was blameless, and his devotion to the interests of the Redeemer's Kingdom unmistakable and exemplary. They could ill afford to lose such: men, but God had called him to a higher service in the very prime of life—only 42 years of age. It seemed so strange that in so short a time, only a few months, he (Mr. Hughes) occupied the unique position of being the only hy- ing minister who had had pastoral connection with that church, his three predecessors there having de- parted this life within the last two years. Mr. Pill- ing was buried yesterday (Saturday) in the ceme- tery of his native town of Bacup, amidst many tokens of respect and sorrow. Let them not cease to pray for the bereaved widow, the aged and • stricken mother, and the dear fatherless children. , The hymn " Servant of God, well done ! Rest from thy loved employ," wasthen sung.</p>
        <p>THE ASSISTANT MARKET INSPECTOR. The Assistant Market Inspector, Mr. W. H. S. Crabtree, has been appointed assistant inspector in the service of the West Riding County Council, at a salary of £l5O a year. There is therefore a vac- ancy in the ‘Varrington post.</p>
        <p>UMBRELLAS RE-covEnEn.--J. and W. Durros, Sankey-st., Warrington,_ .Advt</p>
        <p>THE DISTRESS OF THE FLORENCE MILLS EMPLOYEES.</p>
        <p>The emplo3 6i of the Fl—o-re-n-e-WMill, Howley, are in a very destitute condition owing to lack of employ- ment at the present time. Previous to the coal dis- pute of last year, they had been on short time for two months, and during the whole of the sixteen weeks' strike, they were out of work. After the strike for a short time, they only worked from nine o'clock to four each day, and for the la-t six weeks there has been no work whatever at the mill. Mr. Wm. Diggle has been very active in endeavouring to obtain relief for them, and during the past week he has distributed a considerable quantity of bread and groceries. A meeting in connection with the relief fund was held at the Queen's Hotel, Sinkey- street, on Wednesday evening, but there was only a small attendance. It is stated that work will re-commence on Tuesday next. DEATH OF Mn. T. ROSE.</p>
        <p>Preaching at the Golborne-street Baptist Chapel on Sunday evening from the 23rd psalm, 4th verse, the Rev. D. W. Roberts, spoke of the great consola- tion which had been derived by many from the words of the text. Especially was this so in the case of a late member of the congregation, Mr. Thomas Rose, who had recently passed away. The preacher then made a touching personal allusion to the deceased, whose relatives and friends formed the large portion Gf the congregation. Th deceased who succumbed to a long and trying illness, was a prominent member of the congregation and was well known in the town, He was a member of Mr. T. J. Down's military band, and took an active interest in local cycling clubs. A pathetic feature in connection with Mr. Rose's death was that it occurred a few days before his wedding day, for which arrangements had been made.</p>
        <p>UMBRELLAS RE-COVERED.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Adrt. HOCKEY: GRAPPENHALL v. BOWDON.</p>
        <p>The last match of the season was played at Grap- penhall,. on Faturday, resulting in a win for the visitors, who were strongly represented. Grappen- hall were badly represented, several of the team being unable to play. From the bully off thp ball was run into the Bowdon 25, where it remained for some considerable time during the first half. How- ever, towards the latter end Bowdon broke away, but Burrows made a grand stand in defence of the Grappenhall goal, clearing the ball repeatedly from the circle. Neither side scored up to half time. On resuming, the Grappenhall forwards broke away entirely, Kirkconnel and Bennett working the ball neatly along the touch line, and from a centre of Bennett's. CArter was enabled to score. Sometime later Edmondson scored for Bowdon, Galloway coon adding another. The play was then taken to the Bowdon quarters, but Grappenhall were unable to score. The score at time was—Bowdon 2, Grap- penh all 1. Burrows played his usual grand full back game, but the rest of the team lacked their usual good form and spirit.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL VALUE in Kid Gloves in Tan. Brown, and Black— Is. 11ti. per pair. EVANS', 17 Sankey st.—Advt. WYCLIFFE BIND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>In connection with this society the annual tea meeting was held in Wycliffe Hall on Tuesday even- ing, when about 100 members partook of tea. An adjournment was afterwards made to the upper schoolroom when a miscellaneous entertainment was taken part in by the members of the society. In the absence of the Rev. J. Yonge, the chair was occupied by Mr. T. rlawthorn. The following pro- gramme was cone through. Song" Leaving it off by decrees," W. Parker ; recitation " King Charlie and I," James Bill; dialogue " Ruined Brother," Misses C. Wilcox and F. Christian; duet "List to the Convent Bells," Messrs. W. Parker and J. Foster; pianoforte solo " Bright Helmstone," W. Barnes ; recitation "The Temperance Crusade," Lily Todd; song " Laddie," Miss Wilson Green ; recitation "The dou and the sausages," J. D. OWen ; duet " Touch those chords again," Messrs. G. Gosling and J. Foster; ditrlocue "Doctor Quack," Messrs. W. Ford, Critchley, T. Forster and A. Hayes. Short addresses were delivered by Mr. J. Owen who has conducted the Society, Mr. C. Mills, and the Chairman.</p>
        <p>HousEkoLD LINEN hemmed and markel, free of chargo.—J. and W, DUTTON, Sankev-st., Warrington,—Advt. THE LAST OF THE SATURDAY NIGHT CONCERTS. One of the most successful and commendable institutions which the town has possessed during the wirier season, came to an end—at least for the present session—on Saturday evening. The plea- Emit Saturday night concerts were commenced and carried on by the Social Questions Union, and how acceptahle they have been to those for whom they were intended, has been testified by the large audience which attended each ot the concerts. The ' chair on Saturday evening was occupied by Mr. F. Monks, C.C., who et the outset addressed a few words to the meeting relative to the efforts of the Social Questions Union, and to the excellent work it was accomplishing. The programme was one of the most perfect of the session, and lacked nothing in its various departments to make an entertain- ment of high excellence. It opened with an instru- mental trio by Mrs. Variev and Messrs. 11. Bond and R. If. Pearson, and the item was executed with a skill which added to the reputation which the in- strumentalists individually enjoy. Another trio was contributed by the same party with a success which called forth unstinted applause. 'AI rs. Pearson's excellent voice was heard to great advantage in the "Sunlight Highway," and so vigorous and generous was the attendant applause,that this popular singer had no alternative but to respond, which she did by singing in a delightful manner that sweet Irish song, " Kathleen." Afterwards Mrs. Pearson sang " Sum- mer Shadows," and was again warmly applato,ded. A capital interpretation of "King of the Camp " was given by Mr. F. Brownlow,and Mr R. Gronnds provoked roars of merriment among the cud ience by his clever ventriloquial and humorous sketches. A word of praise is due to Miss Dutton who contri- buted two humorous readings which were thor- oughly enjoyed. The refreshment department was left to the control of Misses Laverock,Ashdown,and R.Brown, and Mrs. Stringer. At the conclusion of the entertainment Mr. S. B. Stringer proposed a vote of thanks to their chairman for presiding and in doing so alluded to the success which had at- tended the whole of the 27 concerts which they had held that season. The motion was carried and the chairman suitably responded:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EASTERTIDE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>EASTERTIDE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>So far as Runcorn is concerned, Good Friday was observed in the most appropriate manner, the usual special services being held at the various chapels and churches, and also at the Seamen's Institute. At two or three of the religious centres in the town, tea parties also took place. The beautiful weather which prevailed,oresented opportunities to a large num- ber of people to indulge in an excursion to the preVy little pleasure resorts which the district of Runcorn is proud of boasting. Should the fine weather hold. out, Easter promisrs to be a period of enjoyment for a great many folks in Runcorn. Locally there are several foot- ball matches and concerts, whilst the London and North- Western Railway Company are, as in former years, catering for those who would rather spend the holiday elsewhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MESSRS. – FULLERTON AND SMITH'S AD VENT.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. - FULLERTON AND SMITH'S AD VENT.</p>
        <p>UNITED EVANGELISTIC MISSION AND SONG</p>
        <p>SERVICES.</p>
        <p>The undersigned ministers of the Gospel in War- rington, earnestly call the attention of the inhabi- tants to the forthcoming visit of the Revs. W. Y. Fullerton and J. Manton Smith, of the Metropolitan Tabernacle Evangelist Society, who will conduct a mission from March 31 to April 8. By the blessing of God they have been made signally successful for a long, number of years in many towns and cities of the king- dom. They conduct their work upon strictly undeno- minational lines, and seek to help all Evangelic +1 churches, by presenting the Gospel of Jesus Christ both to ordinary church and chapel attendants, and to those who are not identified with any place of worship,in such a way as to benefit them all. These Evangelists come to Warrington upon our invitation, and we, as ministers deeply interested in the moral and spiritual welfare of our fellow-townspeople, hereby respectfully and affec- tionately appeal to the Christian public of all denomina- tions, to accord their sympathy to this effort, and,so far as they can, their presence and support.</p>
        <p>_ Signed. ^ - J. S. HUGHES, Baptist Minister. JOHN KIRTLAN, Wesleyan Minister. W. J. KIRKLAND, Primitive Methodist Minister. G. D. MASON, Wesleyan Minister. E. MOORE, Baptist Minister. E. T. REED, Reformed Church of England Minister. D. W. ROBERTS, Baptist Minister. J. WARNOCK, Presbyterian Minister. OWEN WATKINS, Wesleyan Minister. J. W. VATILEY, Baptist Minister. J. YONGE, Congregational Minister.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Some of the magistrates who attend the War- rington Borough Court do not appear to under- stand the conditions under which the working-man live, to judge from their language at the court. No doubt a great number of the cases sent before the magistrates by the School Attendance Committee are well worthy the fines inflicted. But the advice some of the magistrates give to the defendants is not, in the highest degree, reasonable. Only on Thursday a defendant said that he had to go to his work and could not see his son to school, when a magistrate gravely told him that he °ugh:, to stop away from his work in order to see that his son did attend school. This is ridiculous advice, for if the man did stop from his work for that purpose, he would not only lose money through being away, but he would run the risk of losing his situation altogether. It is no doubt extremely desirable that children should attend school regularly, but when advice such as this is given—and it is given pretty frequently— it is going rather too far.</p>
        <p>The Bands Association is already preparing for action during the coming season. A meeting was held on Thursday evening in order to discuss the arrangements and it was there stated that there were more bands joined to the Association th an ever. There will therefore be plenty of variety in the bandsmen during the season. Another luxury which the Association has not previously experienced was that of a balance in hand over last searton's</p>
        <p>expenditure. With this good luck, the committee of the Association at first thought of raising the bandsmen's fee, but wiser councils prevailed and it was decided to defer the matter until another sea- son had passed. It was a gratifying piece of news that the public had contributed more than previ- ously to the boxes in the nark. It is to be hoped that they will continue to do so, for the money is not wasted. The balance in hand at the end of the season is divided among the various bands in order to buy music and new instruments. The Associa- , tion is 10 king forward to a most successful season and I sincerely hope that bad weather will not spoil these anticipations.</p>
        <p>A little difficulty seems to have arisen amongst dis- senters in several townships near Warrington owing to the non-erection of a mortuary chapel at the new cemetery at Hollinfare, and this was brought before the magistrates at the Warrington County Petty Sessions, on Wednesday morning. Several persons were summoned for non-payment of rates and one of the defaulters was Mr. Henry Morgan, of Glaze- brook, who .was summoned for non-payment of a special rate which had been levied on the ratepay- ers of the district for the purchase of a cemetery at Hollinfare. Mr. Morgan told the bench that his reason for not paying the rate was not that he was unable to do so, but that he wished to raise an objection to it. It appears that a short time ago the churchyard at Ilollinfare which had been the burial ground for that and adjoining townships was condemned and a cemetery was ordered to be insti- tuted. A piece of ground at Hollinfare was pur- chased and the cemetery will entail an expenditure of .E 1,660 to meet which sum a special rate has been levied.</p>
        <p>It was to this rate that Mr. Morgan objected, because there had not been erected in the cemetery a mortuary chapel. He said he belonged to what was called " The Glazebrook Army," which had about 1,000 adherents, and Glazebrook was situ- ated about 1-1; miles away from the new cemetery. Without a mortuary chapel at Hollinfare they were unable to conduct the burial service there as the Episcopalians refused to allow them the use of their church for the purpose. Thus the burial service had to be conducted at their " temple " a mile and a half away from the grave. The Church of Eng- land and Roman Catholic churches were not very far away from the new cemetery, so that they did not require a building erected at the cemetery in which to read the burial service, as did the Glaze- brook Army and the Wesleyan denomination. The magistrates were unable to do anything in the matter and ordered the payment ot the rate, the Chairman remarking that the Parish Council would probably be able to deal with the matter when the new Act came into operation.</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CONCERT AT WYCLIFFE HALL.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT WYCLIFFE HALL.</p>
        <p>if evidence were needed of the efficiency and all-round ability of the Wycliffe Church choir, it was forthcoming on Wednesday evening, on which occasion the choir—with a slight augmentation from other quarters—gave a performance for the purpose of raising funds on its own behalf. Mr. G. Wallington (theconductor), and Mr. W. H. Payton, had the getting together of the programme, and in this regard they displayed much taste. The pro- gramme included the interesting and highly descriptive cantata, Romberg's "The Lay of the Bell," followed by a miscellaneous programme comprising' vocal and in- strumental selections of a choice character. Judged from an artistic standpoint, the execution of the cantata re- flected much credit upon the choir,and those responsible for its training. The performance of the caatata was an undoubted success, and the frequent and generous applause of the auditory attested its entire apprec:ation. The chorus was about 20 strong, and they, together with the soloists, occupied an extemporised platform, which had been rendered pleasant to look upon by a display of glow-lamps, plants, and other furnishings, kindly lent by Mrs. R. H. Pearson. " The Lay of the Ball," is full of interesting matter, and includes some charming numbers for solo, quartet, and chorus, the latter especially having occasionally some powerful tone- pictures to portray. In his capacity of the" Master," Mr. W.H. Wallington (baritone), had a very heavy task, and being, unfortunately,in a somewhat delicate state of health, he did not sing with his usual energy. Consider- ing, however, the demand that was made upon 'him— , for his was the principal part—Mr. Wallington acquitted himself more capably than those who knew of his indis- position anticipated. Mr. A. Griffiths (tenor), added not a little to his laurels, and in his third solo—" Tho' pas- sion gives way," &amp;c.—scored a most pronounced suc- cess. The solo is probably the finest in the work, and is full of delicate, and delightful touches, which were caught with great effect in Mr. Griffiths' rendering. So appreciative were the audience of this number, that there was a demand for a repitition,to which Mr.Griffiths responded. The quartet which followed, " And the goodinan with cheerful eyes," &amp;r..t., by Messrs. W. Brown and R. H. Pearson (first and sf cond tenpr), and Messrs. J. Knowles and A. Knowles (bass soli) was very effec- tive. Mr. J. Knowles also interpreted other short bass solos, and displayed the possession of a voice of good range and power, though lacking sonnewhat in expres- sion. The sopranos were Mrs. R. H. Pearson, and Miss N. Roberts. The former, in the opening passes, sang with excellent taste and judgment, and in a duet of delicate pathos—‘• 0 tender longing, hope delightsome" —she was equally succesafi,l in company with Mr. Griffiths. In the two solos which fell to the lot of Miss Roberta, she was exceedingly successful and was enthus- iastically applauded by the audience. Her first solo, descriptive of the death of a mother, lost nothing of its tenderness and sweetness of expression in Miss Robert's hands. The next solo .as of a descriptive character, and its effect was heightened by the introduction in parts, of the tenors and basses, and the chorus. Another noteworthy piece of concerted music followed in a quartet by Mrs. Pearson, Mr. Scattergood, Mr. Pearson, and Mr. J. Knowles, in which the different voices were excellently blended. The chorus throughout acquitted itself admirably ; time and tune being everything that could be wished for. There was one particular number, however, in which the chorus shone. This was a chorus descriptive of the awful powers of fire, and there was here some effective blending of the picturesque and the weird. The second part of the programme embraced violin solos by Mr.R.H. Pearson, whose well-known skill called forth the most unstinted applause. In this part, too, Mrs Pearson made a gust "hit" with the new song "The Promise of Life," (Cowen). This is a song with a high and genuine ring of pathos in its words,fittingly set to music, and requires but to be submitted as it was on Wednesday evening, to make it popular with any audi- ence. Mr. A. Griffiths sang" Sailor's Grave," with such good effect that he was encored and responded with "Good Comnany." Mrs. S. W. Jeffrey sang "Angel of . " in good style, after which Mr. W. H. Wallington —overcoming for a time his indisposition—rendered "The Singers" (with obligato for chorus and organ), with much expression and feeling, and was deservedly encored. The performance was brought to a conclusion with a part-song "Moonlight," by the choir, but there was wanting in the rendering of it much of that delicate variation of time and volume of tone, on which its due : interpretation largely depends. The accompanists were . Messrs. W. H. Payton (pianoforte) and W. A. Wool . (organ), and both these gentlemen performed their allotted parts with taste and skill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>CONCERT AT PADGATE.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>In aid of the Padgate Bible Class a concert was held in the Padgate Wesleyan Sunday school on Friday evening. There was a fair attendance, and the chair was occupied by Councillor Arthur Bennett. The programme was of a miscellaneous character, Misses Bennett, Pemberton and Cosgrove and Mr. A. Grounds, the well-known Warrington comic artiste, being the chief contributors, The crnce3 was a Tno3t ftble on.</p>
      </div>
