      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. L. Voisev and John Geddes. TRAVELLING WITHOUT TICKET. —Michael Sheehan (fire- man) last from Garston was charged with travelling on the Cheshire Lines Railway between Ganton and War- rington on Friday the 16th inst. without having previ- ously paid the fare, and with intent to avoid the pay- ment thereof. Evidence for the prosecution was given by Samuel Dutton, ticket collector at the Warrington station.—The magistrates imposed a fine of 53. including costs or in default seven days' imprisonment. MoNDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Mr. J. W. Hayward, and Mr. S. Wright. DRUNKENNESS.—Martin Hart, 14 Friars' Green. and Thomas Brown, 32 Brick•st., were fined for this offence. A NEGLIGENT DRIVER.—John Rimmer, junr., Cadis- head, was fined ss. including costs for riding on the shafts of a lu,rry in Manchester-road on March B.—P.C. Amson who proved the case stated that the defendant had no control over the norse whatever. Defendant stated that he was tired with travelling,and was having a rest.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. E. J. Guest (chairman) and ihr.</p>
        <p>J. H. Fleming. TaEFT OF COAL.—Three boys, John Shaw, Henry Rat- cliffe, and John Dolls' were each charged with stealing from John Polilt, 123 Orford-lane, on the previous day, 431bs. of coal.—P.C. Marsh said that as he was going along Orford-lane the previous day he saw the three boys with their arms full of coal. As soon as they saw him, they dropped the coal and ran away. He caught Ratcliffe near Sharp-st. and brought him to the police station and afterwards brought the others down also. He discovered that the coal belonged to Mr. Pollit.—The defendants were each ordered to receive three strokes of the birch rod, and to be detained until six o'clock. TRURSDAY.—Before Mr. S. Chandley (chairman), Major Ridgway, and Alderman Sutton. MAINTENANCE ORDERS.—DerIiS ReOrden, Newton-le- Willows, was ordered to pay is. 61. per week, and Wm. Redhead, 69, Priory-:t., Liverpool, is. per week for the maintenance of their respective fathers,at the Warring- ton Union. Mr. Basil Shearer represented the Guar- dians. STONE THROWING.—Henry Jones, 71 Catherine-street, was fined is. and costs for throwing stones in Bewsey- road on March 15.—John Dunbobbin, 45,Lord Nelson-st. Wm. Dunbobbin' 70, Lord Nelsom-st., James Ward, 1, Admiral-st., and Christopher Barry, 41, Lord Nelson-st.. were also-charged with committing the same offence in Lord Nelson-st., on March 18. Mrs. Kelly and P.S. Smith proved the case in which it was shown that the defend- ants had broken a window in Mrs. Kelly's house. They were fined 2s. 6d. each. OBSTReCTIONISTS.—Patrick McGrath, Thomas Stanton John Stanton, Peter Scully, Patrick Murray, William Coyne, James Balmford, and Edward Dooney, were each summoned for asstructing the footpath in front of the Central station and refusing to move when requested to do so on March 19. Only the defendant Scully appeared in the court but the case was heard in the others' absence. P. C. Clough said that the defendants were continually bothering passengers coming from the station, and they defied him when he requested them to go away.—A collector for the station corroborated the officer and the defendants were fined 10s. and costs or 14 days imprisonment. Scsroor, Casss.—The following were summoned for not sending their children regularly to school : Joseph Griffiths, Guardian-street, fined 2s. 61. ; Elizabeth Gri- ffiths. Eldon-street,2s. 6d.; John Fitzpatrick, Earl-street 2s. 6d. Mr. G. Riley prosecuted. A BOYS QUARREL—Wm. Kelley, was summoned by Thomas Gibson for assaulting him on March 18. The two parties are boys about 15 years of age, and both reside in Lord Nelson-st. On the above date, Gibson alleged that Kelly threw a stone at him,as did also some other boys who had been fined that morning for stone throwing. The boys threw stones at Kelly in return, and broke a window. The stone which Kelly threw hit the complainant on the forehead. The magistrates consid- ered that one side was as bad as the other and dismissed the case. •</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY.—Before Major Pickmere (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Mr. Jam Crosfield. - DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—John Murphy and William Pudrow, Sankey Bridges, were fined 5.1. and costs each for being drunk and disorderly at Warrington on the 18th March. DOG OwNErts FlNED.—Martha Guy, Sankey Bridges, was fined 7s. 6d. costs for ke c, epin a dog without a license on the 24th February.—John 'Hunt, Penketh, for allowing his dog to be at large in Warrington-road, Penketh, on the Bth March was fined the costs 7s. 6d., and Joseph Craik, Warrington, was mulcted in a similar penalty for allowing a dog to be at large at Orford on the 2nd March.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Mr. John Brunnrit and Sir Richard</p>
        <p>Brooke. A MAN WITH A REcoo.—John Baguley was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the 10th inst, and with furiously driving a horse and cart on the 12th inst., in Regent-st., Runcorn. To the first charge the defen- dant pleaded guilty, but denied the second one.—P.C. GregoAy having given evidence supporting the first charge, went on to state that at 7.30 p.m. on the 12th inst., he saw defendant driving a horse and cart at a furious pace in Regent-st., Runcorn. When called upon by witness to stop, he refused to do so, but urged the horse on at a faster rate.—Superintendent Atherton said the defendant had appeared that morning for the 51st time, having been convicted before limn various charges.—Defendant was fined 53. and costs or seven daya for the first offence, and for the second 20s. and costs ol•cne month's imprisonment with hard labour. AN UNLICENSED DOG.—Albert Ravenccroft was fined 59. and cests for keeping a dog on the 3rd inst., without having a license for the same. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Edvvard Godfrey, boatman, for having made use of obscene language on the 24th April, 1893, was fined 53. and costs or seven days' hard labour. A RIINCOLIN GUOCER HEAVILY FINE.—John Stringer, provision dealer, of Heath-road, Runcorn, was charged with exposing margarine for sale and not having the same duly labelled and also with having in his possession a certain measure which was not in accordance with the Board of Trade standard on the 15th February.— Mr. W. D. Laird appeared to prosecute.—The defendant admitted the first offence but stated that the measure alluded to was not used as a measure but as a funnel through which he was in the habit of pouring small quantities of vinegar.—Alfred Timmis, inspector of weights and measures, deposed to calling at the shop kept by the defendant on the 15th ult. He saw a par- cel of margarine on the counter which was not labelled. Be asked for a pound and dcfendasit then told him that it was margarine. Witness explained to the defendant the object of his visit and on receiving the margarine he divided it in the usual way leaving one portion with the defendant. The county analyst has certified that the packet of margarine witness had sent him, and which was bought from defendant, was margarine. On the same day he (witness) asked defendant to serve him with some vinegar. Defendant used the measure spoken of and upon witness examining it he found it bore no •-mark whatever. This same measure bad been tested and</p>
        <p>bad been found to hold an ounce less than half a pint.— Defendant was fined 20s. and costs or one month's im- prisonment for the first offence, and for the second ss. and costs or seven days. HAD BEEN CELEBRATING ST. PATRICK'S DAY.—James Feeney as summoned for being drunk in Waterloc-road on the 17th inst.—P.C. Wright deposed to finding the defendant lying helplessly drunk in Waterloo-road on the day named.—Superintendent Atherton said defendant was so drunk that he required to be brought to the lock- up on the ambulance cart. Evidently he had been cele- brating St. Patrick's Day. (Laughter.)—Fined ss. including costs. ROBBERY Fnom Tins PERSON.—William Rigby, alias William Ball and Berry,was charged with having feloni- ously stolen one pilot coat and a silk handkerchief, valued 7s. 6,-L, the property of Mary Taylor, and also with stealing from the person of Patrick Taylor one knife, a box of matches and sd. in copper. The first theft wag alleged to have been committed on the 30th March, 1893. and the robbery from the person on the 17th inst.—Mary Taylor, landlady of a lodging-house,in Fryer-street, Runcorn, depased that on the 30th March, 1893, the prisoner came to her house and asked for lodg- ings there for the night. She refused to allowed him to stop, however, but he would not go away.—The coat referred to, containing the silk handkerchief, was hang- ing at this time behind the door. She shortly afterwards left the kitchen and when she returned the prisoner had left the house and the coat and handkerchief were both missing.—Michael Taylor, son of the prosecutrix, testi- fied to having seerr the pris-mer take a knife, some matches, and money from the rockets of his father, on the 17th inst., whilst the latter was lying on the floor of the kitchen asleep.—P.C. Gregory proved apprehending the prisoner on the 17th. When chargQd with the theft prisoner told witness that he would bring the coat and silk handkerchief back to Mrs. Taylor if he was liberated. Subsequently he denied, however, having stolen the articles.—After further evidence had been given the magistrates dismisses the first charge and sentenced prisoner to fourteen days hard labour on the second charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before his Honour Judge Wynne Foulkes. CLAIM BY A LOCAL JEWELLER.—This was an action taken by Samuel Claff, house furnisher and jeweller of Runcorn for the recovery of £4 us. 61 from James Fay, balance of a debt of £5, the cost of a silver watch supplied on the 4th :_eptemher, 1893, by the plaintiff to the defendant.—Counsel for the plaintiff (Mr. F. A. Lake) stated that the defendant, had signed an agree- ment to take the watch on hire at the rate of 23. per week, and had Failed in his engagement inasmuch as he had only paid 83. 6d.—Mr. Seagull, plaintiff's business nianaer, supported the statement of Mr. Lake as also did Sidney Temple, plaintiff's assistant.—Defendant pleaded that he was ignorant of the nature of the docu- ment he signed being unable either to read or write pro- perly.—Judgment was entered against the defendant for the payment of £2 12s. and advised defendant to return the watch to the plaintiff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evening Mr. Asnumt stated, in answer to a question, that £lOO would be paid to the families of each of the two men who were killed in the Featherstone riots, and that a further sum of £2OO would: be distributed among nine persons who were more or less seriously wounded, and as to whom it had been proved that they took no part in the disturbances. Asked by Mr. SEXTON whether, in view of the intro- duction of the Evicted Tenants Bill, a statement could be presented o Parliament respecting the Irish Church Fund, Mr. J. MoRLEY said he thought it desirable that such a statement should be prepared. In the course of a statement on the business of the House Sir %V. HAR- COURT said the Government would propose the first read- ing of the Electoral and Registration Reform Bill on Thursday, April 5. Lord IC CHURCHILL moved a resolu- tion declaring that the Earl of ROSEBERY had infringed the libertie,. and privileges of the House of Commons by his speech in Edinburgh on Saturday, made while the Leith election was pending. Sir W. HARCOURT denounced this attack on the Premier as a trumpery proceeding, and moved as an amendment that the House proceed to the orders of the day. Mr. BALFOUR said it was now conclusively demonstrated that the sessional order of the House was a farce. The motion was negatived, and Sir W. Harcourt's amendment was agreed to. On the motion to go into Committee on the Navy Estimates, Mr. KEARLEY moved a resolution declaring that it was expedient that the Government should pay in the dockyards and naval establishments wages equiva- lent to trade union rates. Mr. ROBERTSON opposed the motion. He said that this fresh instruction to the Ad- miralty was not needed, and he mentioned that it had been deckled to introduce a forty-eight tr,ur week hi the naval establishments. Mr. FORWOOD said an end ought to be put to the constant agitation which the members for dockyard towns gave voice to in that House,and Mr. BANBURY said the pressure that was being put on the members of the House both by dockyard men and Viae Civil Service was becoming intolerable. Mr. CAMPBELL- BANNERMAN warned the dockyard men that these appeals might lead to a larger use of the services of private yards Lord G. HAMILTON supported this view. The amendment was negatived without a division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>TUESIYAY.</head>
        <p>TUESIYAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening Mr. ASQUITH, replying to a question put on behalf of Sir J. Lubbock, said that, so far as the Government were con- cerned, it was impossible to introduce legislation in the present session on the subject of the hours .f labour in shops. Replying to a question put by Mr. HOPWOOD, the right hon. gentleman said it would be a waste of time to in- troduce this session a bill to carry out the recommenda- tions of the Vaccinatban Commission. Mr. BurroN, replying to Sir G. Baden-Powell, said the chiefs of East and West Pondoland had signed a deed making a cession of their country, and the High Com- missioner had accepted the cession and declared the country British territory. Mr. J. MORLEY informed Mr. Sexton that he was too well aware of the urgent importance of V•ie Evicted Tenants iill to allow the measure to fall back. Sir W. HARCOURT, in reply to Mr. A. Morton, said it was the intention of the Government to introduce a bill to abolish the property qualification of county justices in England and Wales. On the order to go into Com- mittee on the Navy Estimates, Mr. CREMEE took objec- tion to the proposed increase of naval armaments. Sir W. FIARCOURT defended the proposals of the Govern- ment. He said it was owing to his horror of war that he had always been an advocate for the supremacy of the British fleet. He desired our navy to be strong in order that we might he neutral. -After some criticism of the Government's proposals by Mr. Forwood, the House went into Committee, and Sir U. KAYE-SHUTTLEWORTH made the annual statement. 11.• said it was the deli- berate resolution of the Government, if they had the opportunity, to carry their programme for the next five years into 'full effect, and they hoped that it would be once and for all understood abroad that nothing that any other state could do would prevent us from obtaining the security of eur commerce. the defence of our pos- sessions, and the command of the sea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>The Nouse of Commons went into Committee of Sup- ply shortly after assembling on Wednesday, and the Chancellor of the Exchequer moved a vote of £4,199,868 on account of the Civil Servico and Bevrnue Estimates. Mr. Grant Lawson,dealing with the requirements of the Education Department, said it was well known to every inspector in England that Mr. Acland preferred the School Board to the voluntary system. This Mr. Arland denied. A succession of lively scenes afterwards occurred, chiefly on points of order, Dr. Clark in the first place joining issue with the Chairman. After this episode had been settled, Mr. Acland said he believed no needless orders had been made by the Elucation Depart- ment upon voluntary schools, and he denied that any unfair pressure had been placed upon such schools as compared with Board schoo)ls. After further discussion Mr. Grant Lawson withdrew his motion for the reduction of £5OO on the item for public education in England and Wales. The Army Annual Bill was brought in by Mr. Camp- bel!-Bannerman,and read a first time. The Consolidated Fund (No. 1) Bill passed throuqh 'committer:, with an amendment including in the bill the amount of the ex- cess votes and the Navy pay vote. Amont, the bills in- troduced and rea,l a first time was one by Sir Flc.nry James to amend the Factory and Workshops Act, 1891.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PALM SUNDAY SERVICES._</p>
        <p>Special services in —commemoration of Palm Sunday were held at St. James Church, on Sunday. During the afternoon a children's servi,e was held and palms were distributed among the children. There was an even- sone' in the evening, with a procession ani lantern ser- vice the subject of which was "Types of our Lord's sacred passion and their fulfilment." There were large congregations at each service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Corney presided at the meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon. He spoke of the duty devolving upon parents in regard to the religious training of their children, and urged his hearers to send their children to the Sunday school. Police Inspector Burrows, of Salford was the spanker for the afternoon and delivered an admirable address from the words "Cast thy Bread upon the Waters." Miss N. Roberts sang as a solo" These are they" from "The Holy City." Messrs. Pendlebury, Ffa;7iour. and T.Walliagton also took part in the service. The meeting was largely attended.</p>
        <p>The Medical Annual advises Practitioners to remember that when recommending COCOAS as food and beverage for invalids, the name CADBURY on any2packet of Cocoa is a guarantee of purity.—itdrt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 24, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 24, 1894.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS WIN THE SECOND DIVISION CUP. Sr. HELENS V. WALKDEN.—The last competition match of the season was played at St. Helens on Saturday, befere nearly 5,000 swctators. Prior to the match Mr. Harry Varlev, on behalf of the miners who benefited by Mr. Liptrot's soup kitchen during the recent strike, pre- sented Tom Sudlow, wing three-quarter, with a cap, for :ices rendered at the kitchen. Half-time score Helens 3 tries (9 points), Walk Jen nil. In the second half St. Helens had matters mostly their own way, Sud- low, Doherty, and Ashcroft each adding tries, hut the place kicks were unsuccessful. Final result :—St. Helens 6 tries, Walkien nil. By winning this match, St. Helens team become champions of the Lancashire second division and holders of the old West Lancashire Cup, which was passed over to the Lancashire Union at the commencement of the competition. The players will also receive medals. There has been a most excit- ing contest for the honours, Leigh finishing one point behind St. Helens, and Ulverston are third. There will probably he a demonstration in St. Helens when the cup is presented, the winners of which have qualinpd to play the "wooden spoonists "of the first division (Broughton) on neutral ground. ST. HELENS RECREATION V. MILLOM.—MiIIom kicked off in glorious weather, with the wind in their faces. Each side pressed in turn. Tyson early left the field injured. Buckett missed a good chance of scoring. 'The game was one of the pleasantest seen on the Miaow ground for some time, the passing being grand. Millom was rather overplayed. A good kick of Molyneux',a brought play on to the Millom line, where some light work was indulged in for some timeuntil a good kick by Moore averted clinger. Play was not to be kept from Millom lines, chances being missed that should certainly have lowered the hcme citadel. At half-time nothing had been scored. Millom pressed at the commencement of the second half, and tries were obtained by Kitchen and Rowe, and Cunningham for them. Briers put one •on for the Recs. • Final resul.:—Millom 9 points, St. ' Helens Recreation 3 points. Mossemc v. WIDNES.—These teams met at Mossley on Saturday. Peters, for Widnes, effected a good run, but was tackled when getting dangerous. Widnes were hav- ing much the best of the play, and kept the home team busy defending, which they did very effectively. Drum- mond, of Widnes, gained possessioe of the oval several times, but was too well watched to do much scoring. The hemesters now broke away,but in a very short time the visitors were again on the attack. Half time arrived with a clean sheet. The second half was as void of scoring as the first part. Thus the game ended—Moss- ley nil, Widnes nil. RUNCORN V. ROCHDALE HORNETS.—An evenly contes ted gains was played on 'Tuesday evening at Ro chdale bythe above teams in the presence of some 2000 specta- tors. In the first half Runcorn was the first to score through the ins'rumentali!y of Johnson. Play was much mere stubborn in the second half of the encounter and the visitors were kept very busy through the improved tactics of their opponeats. The final score was :—Rnn- corn one goal, one try ; R.chdale Hornets two goals (one penalty).—A draw of eight points.</p>
        <p>sery</p>
        <p>MiIk:CORN RECREATION' 17. SOUTH LIVERPOOL.—These te ms engaged on Saturday afternoon in a r,-t urn match at Runcorn, and proved most exciting and disastrous to the visitors, who wciv defeated by 1 goal (4 points), 5 tries, and 4 minors to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>*.* Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Satu-ciay must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street, Warring'son, not later tlan ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>GEORGE G. BUSSEY and Co. are leading makers of Footballs, Hockey, Golf, and requisites for Sports, Games, and Gymnasia. Catalogue may be had on application to the Sports Manufactory, Peckham, London, S.E. BEST BRAND.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON V. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>It is possible that no match ever played at Warring- ton, created more excitement than did the one on Satur- day, in which our premier team met Swinton. That they were to meet a team dehich was running neck and neck with them in the competition, and one which had never, during the competition, encountered defeat, was sufficient cause for excitement; but when the team in question happened to be Swinton, then there was small wonder that 10,000 spectators crowded and " thrutched " their way on to the Causeway ground. The match was the last which Warrington plays in connection with the League and on all hands it was admitted that they showed better form than in any match during the season. From the commencement of the game until the close, the Wirepullers maintained a splendid form, ad at times, where they gave ample demonstrations of their superiority, they were encouraged with applause of a character so generous and enthusiastic, as perhaps was never witnessed before on that ground. When, after a period of hard fighting, the last try was scored by Broady, there occurred a scene of wild, indescribable enthusiasm. Cheer followed cheer frem almost the whole of those 10.,000 pair of lungs, until the people in the middle of the town must have been thrown into a state of perplexity aad amazement as to the cause. And for a few seconds after the nimble three-quarter had crossed the line their rained upwards quite a shower of hats. There is no doubt, whatever, that had Warring- ton shown, during the early stages of the competition, the same impregnable form that has lately characterized them, they would have carved their way to the head of the list like very Trojans. Saturday was a day for the team and its admirers to be mond of, and there is only a small balance against the Wirepullers which prevents them coming forth from the prolonged fray in the high- est and most victorious position. The following was the composition of the teams : Warrington : F. fleesom, back ; C. Potts, J. W. Evans, J. T. Massey, and F. Broady, three-quarter hacks; James T. Bate and John I'. Bate (captain), half-hacks; Fair Turner, J. Jolley, W. Nevins. J. Taylor, G. Dakin, S. Buckley, J. T. Thorniley, and J. Berry, forwards. Swinton: T. R. Bowker, full back; J. T. Lewis, W. Pearson, J. Valentine (captain), and G. H. Murrry, three-quarter backs • W. Bumby and H. Broekbank, half-backs; N. Hotchkiss, I'. Bothwell, T. Hallam, W. Evans, R. Berry, J. Berry, G. Sharpies' and F. Knowles, forwards. Referee: Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes. Neutral touch judges : Mr. R. T. Lombard. Broughton; and Mr. T. E. Huntingdon, Barrow. Valentine started towards the pavilion goal, and Heesom's reply being accurate, the first scrimmages toek place at midfield. The home for- wards early demonstrated their smartness, for wheeling the scrimmages cleverly Swiaton had to fall back. Lewis accomplishing a couple of fine saves. A couple of pen- alty kicks against the locals eased the pressure slightly, but the respite was only brief, for another pretty display by the smart Warringtan eight, followed by clever play on the part of the Bates Evans, Potts, and Maesey,eave Valentine and Pearson Plenty of work in the tackling department. Another free kick taken by Valentine was well utilized, hut Potts regained the advantage, a smart follow-up flooring Bowker near the posts. The Swinton front rank now got the better of a tight scrimmaee, and Bumby dribbled well, only to see Evans save well, whilst another kick by Potts saw Bowker clearing finely. Swin- ton were next penalized, and Turner piloted safely, quick following up by Berry, Taylor and Jolley preventing Brocktank replying. -Cross dribbling saw the leather worked over the line, whilst a few minutes later passing by the Bates took the oval over the line, and a try claimed for but not allowed. Potts. from the scritn- mage which followed, accepted from his captain, and essayed a shot at goal, hut the oval failed to rise, and another minor was chronicled. Following the drop out came a pretty display between rhorniley and Dakin, whilst Warrington's position was considerably improved through the accurate passing of Fair Turner, John Bate, and Massey. Broady, who had had no chance of showing his abilities, was now tested, but the pass to him was defective, and Swinton cleared. How- ever, after a spell of fluctuating play, a round of passing travelled to Massey, who steadied himself for a shot at goal. The well-directed attempt was mulled by Bowker, and Berry being in close attendance an easy try was notched, but the cheers gave way to groans when Potts missed the easy place. Warrington still claimed the advantage of the drop out, and passing by the Bates and Massey saw the three-quarter making gal- lent bids for a score. For the first time in the match Swinton had a look in the borne quarters, good passing by Bumby, Murray, and Valen.tine bringing about the change, and further progress was made by Pearson,but a rush by the locals and clever play by Taylor and Jolley soon reeained the half way flag. Potts next intercepted a pass from Valentine, and raced away to a few yards of the Swinton line where Buckley knocked on and a capital chance was lost. Valentine did yeoman service en the defending line, but a moment later a brilliant ex- position by the whole of the Warrington backs was snoiled by the final transfer to Potts being defective. The oval was worked to within a few feet of the posts, and Bow ker had effectel another clever save when the whistle sounded for half time with the score in Warring- ton's favour by a try. On resuming Warringbsn even improved in their form. A fine dribble by Jolley sent the excited spectators into ecstacies, and -Broady made one brilliant run along the popular touch, finding at last the Swinton defence a little too accurate. Valentine cleared, but on returning to the attae.k through the medium of Berry, Taylor, and Thorniley. John Bate dodged over, but was held. Hallam struggled harcLand practically succeeded in clearing, whilst Pearson had a shot at goal, but without effect. Again did Valentine fail to utilize a couple of fair catche3, and then came . . _</p>
        <p>the last few minutes of the game with Warrington attack- ing vigorously. Each piece of play was duly applauded but the climax was reached when Turner scooped the ball from the loose, and passing to James Bate, that player tricked, arid handing over to Broady, he took the proffered chance in brilliant style and a str-ng run landed him a scorer amidst the waving of hats, &amp;ft., of the eycited spectators. Turner was entrusted with tho place, and a fine goal resulted. Soon after restarting from centre field the whistle went for no side, and Swin- ton had thus sustained their first defeat in the competi- tion by a goal and a try (eight points) to nil</p>
        <p>WATMINGTON "A" V. SWINTON "A."—At Swinton. Warrington commenced operations against the wind with the sun in their faces. After the first minute the home team started to press,and before the visitors were aware of the dangerous proximity of their opponents the Swin- ton forwards wheeled the leather over the line and scored. Then Warrington livened up, and from a pack near midfield Clark passed to Forster, who threw to Carey and this player getting away grandly, punted over the full back, and Cross racing up scored in an easy position, and a, gaol was kicked. The drop out enabled Swinton to attack, hut a brilliant loose rush headed by Hemp„,, Holmes, Wilcox and Bandies carried the warfare to the home 25. Hampson and Honediton dribbled fairly, hut Stephens intercepting a pass raced to the visitors' quarter flag. Warrington were pressed, but Lawless and then Burrows, Fairhurst and Clark cleared to midfield. Then Booth intercepted the ball, and punted over the line, Carey saving in the nick of time. Swinton were laving the better of affairs their forwards being stronger, and twice over Gibson eased the pressure v.-WI smart kicks. Houghton was very prominent in the loose. and then Clark getting splendidly away passed to Cross, and Swinton were pressed. A kick was charged, Swinton racing away to the other end, Holmes flooring Berry in trying to dodge through. Some short passing by the home team bothered Warrington, Carey tackling Gadd in splendid fashion on the line. At length Swinton started some passing, and the ball being passed forward the visitors stopped appealing, but Little got in, and gaining the referee's verdict Gill placed a goal. With the resump- tion of hostilities play became very fast and Wareington were allowed several minors. Towards the close Carey raced in under the posts, but the try was disallowed, after this the home tram attacked, but although several chaNces presented themselves could not score, Swinton defeating their opponents for the first time by 1-1-3 to 1-04.</p>
        <p>EIINcORN V. BIRKENHEAD PARK.—On Saturday after- noon a contest between these teams teok place t Run- corn, both clubs being well represented. Runcorn kicked off, and at once began to prcss on their opponents, but the latter were not so very easily overcome, and the home team experienced some difficulty in scoring. At half time one goal had been secured by the homeAters, whilst the viitors had not made any headway. After the resumption of play,some capital tactics by the Run- corn team were witnessed,who operated in a most com- bined manner. They were successful in scoring a second goal, and their defence was so sturdy that Birkenhead could not get a look in. The game ended : Runcorn, 2 goals, one try ; Birkenhead Park, nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BIRICENIIRAD PARK " A " TEAMS.—The Runcorn men paid a visit on Saturday to Birkenhead, where in beautiful weather the return match with the "A" team of Birkenhead Park took place. Shortly after thia start the home team became rather dangerous, and but for the good form of the Runcornians would have succeeded in getting through several times. During the first half no goals were scored, but in the course of the second half the visitors pulled themselves together, and presented a most determined front to their oppcnents. Again and again the Park men were beaten, until at the conclusion of the contest the visiting team found them- selves in possession of a victory of 1 goal 3 tries to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>FRIENDS SCTIOOL, PENRETII, V. WARBINGTON BASEBALL. —The above match was played at Penketh on Saturday The Baserall kicked off with a slight wind in their favour but a free kick transferred play to the other end, and the goal keeper had a warm time of it. However, he kept his charge intact. Both ends were visited in rapid suc- cession, then the School gradually pressed and Stanley scored with a fast low shot. At half time the score stood Friends' School, one, Baseball, nit. On resuming Smith of Fenketh came from half to centre forward, and by that means the score was increased by six goals—Stan- ley one, Smith two, Reynolds two, and Coarnock one— and the game ended in a win for the School by seven goals to one. For the school Reynolds played a sterling game on the outside right, and Pollard worked hard and did a lot of good work at half back. v</p>
        <p>ORFORD CRESCENT V. FENKETII RESERVE.-T return match was quite disastrous for Penketh, they having to return home defeated, by eight goals to none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>POLICEMAN AND THE RACE MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE POLICEMAN AND THE RACE MEETING.</p>
        <p>HE WAS DETERMINED TO BE THERE.</p>
        <p>There is, says " Cassell's Saturday Journal," a police- man in a certain northern town, a good-natured Irish- man, of some six feet four. A few months ago this jovial giant was desirous of attending a race meeting which was taking place at a town a few miles distant, and being on night duty,he saw nothing to prevent him having his wish, until the night before the date of the meeting. But on this particular evening, when going his rounds, he chanced across the body of a woman, hopelessly intoxicated, lying on the footpath. Here was a dilemma. If he took her into custody, what of his chance of attending the races to-morrow, for it would be necessary to appear in court? On the ether hand, it was manifestly impossible to leave her there ; and after a few moments' cogitation, he picked her gently up, and carried her to the beat of a comra le, where he de- posited her to be found by someone else. He congratu- lated himself upon having got out of his difficulty very neatly, until, returning over the same spot on his next round,he was surprised to find her lying there as before. This was strange ; and wondering to himself hcw she had managed to walk, in her helpless condition, he took her up again and replaced her on his comrade's beat. His surprise may be imagined, when he returned an hour later, to find her lying again on the identical- spot from which lie had first carried her ; but, determined to solve the mystery, he took her to the place to which he had previously taken her, and concealing himself in an ad- jacent doorway awaited the progress of events. Presently the stillness was broken by the sound of approaching footsteps, and his fellow constable hove in sight. A puzzled look overspread the latter's face at the sight of the woman,but after a few seconds lie quietly lifted her on his shoulder, and was walking away, when his fellow watchman emerged from the doorway, and an explanation ensued, which resulted in their unwelcome charge being deposited on the territory of a new begin- ner. Next day the son of the Emerald Isle was con- fronted with a fresh difficulty to prevent his little holi- day. He is blessed (or the reverse) with a wife who likes to have a little say in the direction of his move- ments, and she insisted that a visit to the races would only result in her lord and master returning home penni- less and probably intoxicated. In vain he assured her that such would not be the case. She absolutely refused to listen to him, and announced her intention of accom- panying him if he went. This did not suit his p!ans,and despairing at last of setting her mind at rest, he lost his temper. Taking her up in his arms, as if she had been a baby, he placed the hem of her skirts between the rollers of a mangling machine, which stood in the kit- chen, and turned the handle until she was wound close up to the machine and perfectly helpless. Then taking the handle off, and gagging the cog wheels, he hurried off to catch his train. Her shouts soon attracted the neighbours, and after a whileshe was liberated, but too late to catch her better half, who was by this time des- cribing to his conipanions the ludicrous position in which he had left his wife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>DAISY.</head>
        <p>THE DAISY.</p>
        <p>Wko does net love the little flower, With golden crown and snow-white frill ; That braves the gale, survives the shower, A tiny thing of beauty still. While yet the ling'ring winter lies, All chilly o'er the gloomy scene ; The daisy points to sunny skies And landscapes cloth'd in richest green. Charm'd by the hope its presence yields, The lark with joy-elated breast ; Flies sun-ward f,'er the dreary fields, Or hovers warbling o'er his nest. Nor in the rural vale alone Comes forth the daisy, nature's child ; It cheers the forest depths unknown, And blooms upon the desert wild. No matter where our feet may tread, Content at home, or wand'ring stray ; The daisy, as by instinct led, Peeps up to bless our devious way. Loy'd floweret, in our childish hours, Ye were to us a thing of joy; Our playmate in the woodland bowers, A bonny gem without alloy. 0 fair was life's young morning then, And beautiful its skies of blue; For no deceitful ways of men, Upon our path their shadows threw. Ney can we in old age forget, The mem'ry of those early years ; Still link'd with thee it lingers yet, A sunbeam to our cares and tears. And when life's latest breath has fled, And earth receives the clay she gave; May some dear hand be kindly led To plant a daisy on our grave. Sweet flower, like thee, in humble trust, Be ours to sleep beneath the around; Till wak'd like thee, from mould'ring dust, • We rise with spring eternal crown'd. .Jis. 11. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
        <p>Lord Sa!isburF hthitually dictates official papers into one of Edison's phonographs.</p>
