        <p>NOTIS ON GAMES AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>. The Warrington team surpassed all expectations in the manlier in which they accounted fore the famous Swinton team on Saturday afternoon. In al- most if not every department,the Warrington team were the superior of their opponents. They are to be congra- tulated upon performing a feat of which no other team in the competition can boast,that of gaining three points out of a possible four out of Swinton. They have now played their last league match and reached the second place in the league, and though they may yet have to take the third place again, their position on the table is a rroud one when we consider the wretched start they made.</p>
        <p>At the commencement of the competition it appeared as though the club was on the decline,as they suffered a series of defeats both in league and friendly fixtures which took almost all confidence out .f their supporters. They hqwever buckled to, and commencing with the Salford match at Warringtm they have been victorous all along the line with the exception of a defeat at Old- ham and a draw at Swinton. They have performed equally well at home and away,and it is safe tc say that never was the team in better form that they are at the present moment.</p>
        <p>The gate on Saturday was the record one of the season and there were numerous Swintonians present. The Warrington forwards at the very commencement of the match demonstrated their superiority over Swinton both in the packs and in the loose, and it was the forward play whieh caused Warrington to press continually dur- ing the early part of the match. In this department Nevins was especially prominent as also were Jolley, ' Buckley and Berry, the latter of whom scored the only try of the first half. The two Bates were not in the best of condition glut they gave a brilliant display of half back play. Among the three quarters, Massey was very con- spicuous, his sharp picking up and quickness of foot being of great advantage to his side. He,would probably m,ke a good hurdle racer, for generally when he cannot pass an opponent, he tries to jump over him, sometimes with great success. He tried this mancevre with Valen- tine', but that gentleman was rather too big tor him and he came to grief. Evans played very consistently and he was of great Ilse to his side on Saturday. Potts worked hard and managed to" bottle up" Valentine, which quite made up for several chances he missed and his poor kick at goal. Broady was not fed very much, and therefore did not shine until at the close of the match, when he scored about the best try that has been scored this season on the Warrington ground. Heesoin had not very much to do but he showed himself much superior to his ris-d-vis Bowker.</p>
        <p>Now that the competition is over as far as Warrington is concerned, a lot of the interest in the first team's do- ings will he taken away. However 'friendly" Rugby matches have not yet fallen into the same disrepute as in association circles,and we may yet look for some good gates in the matches yet to come, for there are still some warm encounters in view. For instance, on Monday, Warrington have a defeat to revenge upon Lancaster,and after accounting for Swinton they may be trusted to do it, if they have a team anything equal to their full strength.</p>
        <p>" The order of things changeth, and the old givath place to the new." However acceptable this truth of evolution may be in most matters, there are occasions when it is greeted with sorrow. Many of the old players have gorse from the Warringtm football club, and their places have been filled by younger men. Though these changes may not have hurt the club Will as each " old veteran " has taken up his laurels and " left to abide with them in peace," a note of sorrow has been sounded in the hearts of those who have seen the veteran build up the club to what it is. The last case of retirement— after that of H. Aslitm—is that of " Fair." Turner". It makes one recognise that youth is not always with 'us,—likc the poor—and the herculean " Fair " must feel that he is now commencing to settle down to the quietude of family life, after one of the most successful and brilliant athletic careers in the north. " Fair" Turner played his last match on Saturday as a regular member of the Ist team but I hope that we shall see him for many years to come taking some sort of active interest in the club, which he can claim,withpride to have done much for in its devel- opment.</p>
        <p>The arrangements have been completed in connection with the Warrington junior teams championship. The draws took place on Monday night at the Norton Arms Hotel, Mr. Bowyers in the chair. The following teams were represented : Warrington Rangers, Latchford Ran- gers, Dallam Brewery, Stockton Heath, St. Mary's, Latehfork.l, and St. Peters. In the first round Dallam were drawn with St. Luke's ; referee Mr. F. Turner, the match to take place on the second of April. Second round:—Latchford v_Dallam or St. Luke's; referee Mr.W. Dillon, match to be played on April the sth. St. Peter's v Latchford Rangers ; referee,Mr. S. Wright J.P.,match to be played on April 9th. Stockton Heath v Warring- ton Rangers ; referee Mr. W. Speakman, match to be played on April 12th. St. Mary's v Dallam Brewery : referee, Mr. T. Barnes (junior), match to be played on April 16th.</p>
        <p>April 16th will close the second round, and then, I supp6se, " the fun will commence." The clubs have the choice of either playing on their own grounds, or upon tho Warrington • Football club's ground. I hear that within a few days the cup will be on exhibition in the window of some prominent tradesman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN AND ' NEWTON</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN AND ' NEWTON</p>
        <p>DRUNKE NNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Town HO, Newton, on Saturday morning, Pat Ruddy and Pat Keeffe were charged before Mr. T. D. Grimke-Drayton, with being drunk and dis- orderly in Earlestown on Friday night, in Legh-st. They were fighting and causing a disturbance. They were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs.—At the same Court on Monday morning Pat Kelly was charged with being drunk in Earlestown on Saturday night, and was fined ss. and cogs. EARLESTOWN MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL. The draw for the position of Queen in the forth- coming May Queen festival, took place in the Paro- chial Mission Hall, Earlestown, on Monday even- ing. There were a good number of entries, and the favoured position fell to Elizabeth Chesters,aged 12, of 12 Regent-street. The draw for the positions of maids of honour, resulted as follows :—Annie Aud- ley, Elizabeth Malwern, Agnes Mottram, Lily Hol- man, Frances Rice, Jane Massey, Edith Edmonds, Harriet Howarth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>EASTER. HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>EASTER. HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Easter holiday season, the various works in the districts are closing in order to enable their workmen to enjoy the cheap facili- ties offered them by the railway company, who are making special provision for excursions to various places. The Vulcan Foundry Co., will close on Thursday evening and re-open on Wednesday morn- ing. The Viaduct works, and Messrs. T. and T. Vicars close on Thursday evcning and will re-open on Tuesday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a tea meeting in connec- tion with the Wesleyan Society was given in the lecture hall. About 60 members were present, and a nicely served tea was partaken of. After tea a meeting was held presided over by the Rev. T. Wynne-Jones, and a most instructive and interest- ing gathering resulted. Several discussions ensued in relation to the, financial condition of the church, ani proposed improvements were laid before the meeting:</p>
        <p>ST. MARY's CATHOLIC DAY SCHOOLS, NEWTON. St. Patrick's Day was celebrated by the Catholics of Earlestown and Newton, by a concert given in t. Mary's and St. John's Halls. There was a very large audience, and a good programme of songs was submitted, and thoroughly appreciated. The glees given by the school children were loudly applauded, and the contributions of Miss Beatty, were given with her accustomed taste and ability. The follow- ing took part in the programme, Misse,s Bartley and M. J. Beatty, Mrs. Gibbons, Messrs. J. Gibbons, J. Burke, J. Higham and J. linddleston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>VULCAN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL!</head>
        <p>VULCAN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL!</p>
        <p>On Siii:jcria:Y .evening a concert arranged by the Vulcan Picnic Club, was given in the !lining-room of the Vulcan Foundry, in aid of the funds of the above school. Mr. W. Crawshaw presided, and there was a very large audience. The programme embraced instrumental and v.Gcal music, and was sustained by artistes from Wigan, Warrington, &amp;c., and was as follows :—Flute solo Valse Graceuze," "Souvenir" (c)," Tyrolean Airs," Mr. L. Daintith, (Croft) ; songs" The distant shore," and " Mona," Mr. Dowswell (Wigan); songs "Twickenham Ferry," and "Banks of Allan Water," Miss Roberts (Earlestown); humorous songs " 4n-jah," and "They're all at home," Mr. J. Sumner (Wigan) ; songs " Dreamtide," and "The better way," Miss Conway; songs "Pilgrim or Love," and "My sweet- heart when a boy," Mr. P. Mooney; humorous sketch, Mr. Scarisbrick (Warrington) ; songs "The gift," and "Three fishers went sailing," Miss Thomp- son (Earlestown) ; Professor Visto gave a couple of ventriloquial sketches, which caused much amuse- ment. Mr. J. Johnson, Golborne, accompanied during the evening.</p>
        <p>filE CONDEMNED ST. HELENS MEN.</p>
        <p>EFFORTS FOR A REPRIEVE.</p>
        <p>Efforts are being made to obtain a reprieve for the three men Carney, Leahey, and Riley condemned to death at Liverpool for the murder of Police Constable Gordon at St. Hp lens. One of the jurors states that the strongest around which affected his mind in making the recommendation to mercy was the youth of the pri- soners. He described the verdict as having been reluc- tantly arrived at, and concludes, "None of the jurymen wanted them to be hanged." A member of the Bar of the Northern Circuit, who states that he was in ccurt during the trial, requests that his name may be added to the petition for mercy, and concludes, "I have no doubt that the members of the Bar now attending the assizes would, without exception, sign such a petition." A St. Helens doctor writes :—" I don't believe that the ' medical evidence establishes cause and effect between the alleged or proved violence and the presumed shock in the late Constable Gordon's case." Mr. Henry Lin- don Riley, solicitor, has taken the matter in hand, and has despatched a memorial to the Home Secretary re- garding the propriety of the verdict." Pending the Home 'Secretary's reply to this, it is not likely that much will be done with the petition intended, if necessary, to to more popularly signed. Mrs. Riley, wife of Frank Riley, one of the condemned men, received the following letter from the Home Secretary in answer to her appeal for mercy : _ .</p>
        <p>" name Office, March 19,1894.</p>
        <p>" Madam,—l am directed by the Secretary of 'State for the Home Department to acknowledge the receipt of your letter on behalf of Frank Riley, and to say that it shall receive due attention.—l am, &amp;c., (Signed) " GODFREY LIISHINGTON. "Mrs. Lucy Riley." THE DOCTORS AND THE POST-MORTEM.</p>
        <p>Now that the petition for a reprieve of the three young colliers condemned to death for the murder of Police- constable Gordon is being formulated, public attention is being directed to the evidence of the doctors who made the post-mortem examination. Two of these medical gentlemen gave evidence at the assizes, but for some reason or other the third medical witness,Dr.Paul of Rod- , ney-st., Liverpool was not called. Probably it was that the prosecution were under the belief that his evidence would merely corroborate that of Drs. M'Nicoll and Knowles. But the council for the defence, on ascertain- ing that Dr. Paul was not to be put in the witness box, desired to call him as a witness for the defence, but was unable to get him to court before the conclusion of the case. The rum,ur has,got about that Dr. Paul had formed the opinion tha!-, the deceased man cams by his death, not as a result of violence or "deferred shock," but from 1 some other cause—probably the drinking of a poisoned fluid when, subsequent to the affray, he get a drink of water in the adjacent chemical works. We have ascer- tained from Dr. Paul that there is no foundation for this theory. He asserts that there was absolutely nothing whatever in the appearance of the body or its cont-mts indicating a cause for the constable's death. Further than that he does not care to go, as he is preparing a stateme2t of his opinions to forward to the authorities. It is extremely likely that the evidence of Dr. Paul will kelp the Home office in arriving at a decision in the mat- ter: Thore seems little probability that the extreme penalty of the law will be exacted,but much can be done in respect to the reduction of the alternative of penal ser- vitude for life to a comparatively short term of impri- sonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mclntyre presided over the usual fort- nightly meeting of the above Board held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday morning. The remaining gentlemen present were : Messrs. Joseph Knowles, C. W. Davenport, Enoch Jackson, Joseph Stokes, James Whitlow. Thomas Baxter, J. M. Williams, H. E. Heywood, Thomas Lewis, James Brandreth, Alfred Broome, William Ellison, Samuel Horton, J. L. Jones, Eaton Bretherton, Joseph Hall, W. Pickering,J. W. Baguley,William Whitlow, Thomas Handley, D. H. Jones, Thomas Lea, A. B. Norman, Samuel Taylor, Samuel Yarwood, C. T. Whitley, J. E. Bate, John Weir, William Turner, Edward Aaron, Tl:omas Ford, Charles Mounfield, George Priestner and the Revs. J. W.N. Tanner, Dr. Gastaldi, and James Hanby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>AN EXTRA. CUP OF TEA FOR THE FEMALE LUNATICS.</head>
        <p>AN EXTRA. CUP OF TEA FOR THE FEMALE LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read an order from the Local Government Board which stated that notwithstanding any previous order the Guardians, may, if they think fit, cause dry tea with sugar and milk to be supplied to such of the female inmates of the Workhouse as they may consider should be supplied,the quantity to be such as the Guar- dians may by resolution prescribe. This will be in ad.li- tion to the tea prescribed by the dietary in.force at the Workhouse.—The necessary instructions were given for the carrying out of this order where required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>REMUNERATION FOR THE CLERK.</head>
        <p>REMUNERATION FOR THE CLERK.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Heywood, seconded by Mr. Aaron it was agreed to pay the Clerk the sum of £B5, as his usual salary per annum, in addition to £l5 for his services with regard to the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany, making a total of £lOO.</p>
        <p>THE PROP ,SED SUPERANNUATION OF DR. ROBINSON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bran-dreth submitted the report of the Committee appointed to censider the question of granting a super- anuation to Dr. Robinson, and stated that the Commit- tee had decided with great reluctance, not to comply with the request. They did this on principle, and acknowledged the valuable services he had performed during over 30 years.—Mr. Hanby said the present was not a time for complying with such requests, seeing that taxes and rates were accumulating. They had a very high regard for Dr. Robinson and he hoped that he would not take the decision of the Com,cittee as a reflec- tion upon himself.—lt was ultimately decided to adopt the report of the Committce rejecting the proposal to superannuate Dr. Robinson. DR. ROBINSON'S SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that there were three applications for the post of Medical Officer of Health to the Board, the applicants being Dr. Mobberley, of Stretton ; Dr. Carruthers, of Halton ; and Dr. Selby, of Frodsham.— The election of a successor was by means of a ballot, which resulted in Dr. Selby obtaining the post by a large majority.—On the proposition of Mr.Brandreth,seconded by Mr. Hes:wood, Dr. Selby was appointed Medical Offi- cer to the Board for the ensuing year. THE PAVING OF THE GIRL'S PLAY GROUND AT THE</p>
        <p>WO RKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. E. Heywood moved that the following resolu- • tion passed on February 6th last, be rescinded :—" That Messrs. Dunbar Kelly and Co.'s tender for paving the girl's yard with gra,nolithic flags at 43.9 d. per square yd. ha accepted, and that the contract embodying the terms of such tender be entered into with Messrs. Dunbar Kelly and Co." Mr. Heywood said he moved the re- scinding of the resolution referred to with fr!elings of eatisfaction and dissatisfaction. With satisfaction be- cause he was the mouthpiece of many Guardians who had not had an opportunity of speaking upon this ques- tion ; and with dissatisfaction because he did not like to rescind or undo the work of any committee. La he said on the last Board day there were many yards in which school children played which were much worse than the one which was occupying their attention at that moment. He had seen a large number of such play grounds paved with cinders,and he thought that cinders were quite suitable for this yard. It was • simply because of the expense , wlfth the work of paving the yard curtailed, that he pressed the rescinding the resolution, which, he was sure, had not been sufficiently considered. The matter was only brought before the Board to consider in the light of 4s. 9d. per square yard, the number of yards not being mentioned. The yard had that day been measured, and , it was found that the cost would be something like £l2s.—Mr. Weir said they could notAat the present time run away with £l3O out of the ratepayers' pockets.-One or two members enquired which yard was being spoken of. They did not know, as they had not been present when the subject was under discussion before.—lt was then suggested that those members who had net seen the yard should go and inspect the place, and probably cn their return they might have some suggestion to make with • regard to the matter.—Mr. Norman said he would like to offer a few words of explanation on behalf of the Committee who had had this question to deal with. It had been said that the work would cost £l5O. The yard had been measured and it had been found that the work would just cost £lO6 17s. 6d. The Committee only had three proposals at first before them when considering this matter. Having carefully considered the schemes they came to the conclusion that the best course to pur- sue would be to have it done with granolithic flags, which would have the effect of making it bright and cheerful for the children, and would not want attending to for a long time.—The members who had inspected the yard now returned and Mr. Stokes, on their behalf, said they were of the opinion that what required to be done to the yard could be done ,by the inmates themselves. Such a large amount of money as that named should not be spent on this work more especially at the present time when the rate payers were anxiously looking forward to a reduction in</p>
        <p>the rates. He thotig'nt that the yard would do very well if it was patched up by the inmates wit!i cinders and tar.--The Chairman then put Mr. Ihywood's rn?- Lion to the meeting and it was carried.—Thelr- man said it would now perhaps be best to. refer the matter back again to the Conam tee.—Mr. Norman said he would simply decline to serve on that Committee if the question WO referred back to it. He suggested that the matter 190 now settled • before the whole Board.—Mr. Stokes prO- - posed that what repairs were necessary in the yard be done with cinders and tar, &amp;c., by the inmates there selves.—Dr. Gastaldi suggested that if this yard was t. be repaired by the inmates all the others should be don.' in the same way.—Mr. Stokes said he was quite willing to include that suggestion in his motion which was po to the meeting and carried.—. The Chairman afterwafclB expressed a hope that the proceedings in connection with the pavement of the girls' yard would not lie taken aS precedent. It did n t add dignity to the BJard to revtso• its work every week.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL STATEMENT. Mr. Brandreth submitted the lioancial sfaternent which showed a balance in hand of £3,731 is. Sd. ape' the checres for payment- were :—For outdoor relief, Raul corn, £9O; Frodsham, £3O; Bud worth, £22. The 0L1! townships had paid their calls.—Mr. Aaron remarked that that was the first time in twenty years that the calls' had been paid during March. His observations on OW previous Board day had evidently taken effect, and h 0 therefore waived his motion. "FEE</p>
        <p>EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the petition he had prepared to be for: warded.to the House of Commons in which the petr tioners urged that public lementary schools should be' free and that Boards of Guardians should not he called upon to pay fees as at present. They asked the House to take the subfret into consideration with a view of an amendment of the law as it at present stood.—On the motion of Mr. Lea, seconded by Mr. Jones, the seal of the Board was affixed to the petition.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL CONTRACTORS. The following tenders were accepted :—Shoe rcpair- ing, Mr. Joseph Bazley, Vine-st., Runcorn ; new bowtS, Mr. John Elobson,Nantwich ; clothing, Messrs. linswortb- bros., Runcorn ; coal, Messrs. Biram and Co.,St. Helens' coke, Runcorn Gas Company ; meat, Mr. Joseph Dobson, Warrington ; milk, butt,r and eggs,Mr. Samuel Basnettr. Preston Brook ; drapery, Messrs. Fletcher and Loe9 Warrington ;drugs, etc., Mr. John Henry Weston,Devorr shire Buildings, P.uncora ; grocery,. Mr. Walter Copier' Runcorn ; conveyanco of -books, Mr. Thomas Booth* Runcorn ; funerals, Mr. Henry Jackson, Runeorn ; cart- age, Mr. William Grounds, Aston ; coffins, Mr. Lightfoot, Aston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ATTENDANCE OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>THE ATTENDANCE OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The following list of attendances Was read out by the Clerk who intimated that the list did not include the attendances fnr that day :—W. H. J. Jenkins, attended 6 Board and Committee meetings ; Arthur Greening 2. J. W. N. Tanner, 35; C. W. Dtvenport, 16; JeselA Knowles, 20; John Parkinson, 25 ; Enoch Jackson, 11 ; John Mclntyre, 33 ; Joseph Stokes, 17 ; James Whitlovh 4 ; Alfred Broome, 9 : Thomas Baxter, 19 ; H. E. Iler wood, 63 ; J. M. Williams, 43 ; Thomas Lewis, 39; James Hanby, 33 ; James Parr. 7 ; James Brindretb,3s; Samuel Woodward, 29; William Ellison, 2 ; Samuel Hor ton, 4 ; J. L. Jones, 7 ; E. Bretherton. 14 ; Jsepti Hall, 4 ; W. Pickering, 10 ; J. W. Baguley, 9 ; W. WhitloW.9 J. B. Gastaldi, 17 ; Thomas Handley, 28 ; D. H. Rine°, ,32 ; Thomas Lea. 35 ; A. R. N(\rman, 13 ; Samuel 'fan " !or. 28 ; Samuel Yarmnd, 27 ; George Priestner, ' 'C. T. Whitley, 34 ; J. E. Bate, 1; J..hnAVeir, 24 ; Turner. 5 ; Edward Aaron. 46 ; Thomas Ford, 14; Mounfield, 6. Ex-Officio :—Henry Nield, 3; P. Spea' I man, 1 ; W. V. Fox, 2 ; Samuel Woodhouse, 1 ; Charla , Reynolds, 11 ; Dr. Steele, 5 ; A. Thomas, 9; Clerk. O'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN AND THE SHIP CANAL. DATE OF THE OPENING CEREMONY. The Queen (says the " World") is to open the AlIt: chester Ship Canal during the last week in June, Whe- .on her way South from Balmoral to Windsor. The func- tion will take place either on Tuesday, the 26th, Or P .11 Wednesday, the 27th. No definite arrangements will„ can be made until after the Qiieen has returned from t_Any Continent, but I hear that her Majesty is to procetr, along the canal for some miles in a state barge or la,uneu; It is probable that the Qaeel I will be the guest either 0' the Duke and Duchess of Westminster at Eaton Hal], 47r of Lord Egerton at Tatton Park. A DIRECT LINE To THE BRAZILS. .th For some time a rumour has been in eircutati9n ..4 regard to the establishment of a direct line of steaol°'" Nate. between the canal and the Brazils and the River The rumour appears to have been well founded. DitesSet: Lamport and Holt now announce that their stearnet Herschel (1,947 registered tons, 311 feet long, 34 fees, beam) will load at Manchester for Bahia, Rio and Sant° closing on Tuesday' the 3rd April, and sailing at daec break the followingdly. This steamer will be follovi a week later by the steamer Maluris (2,234 register° tons. 309 feet long, 36 feet beam) to load for Peraal; buco, Victoria, and Rio Grande do Sul. Cargo for bc)....'0 these vessels is now being received at No. 5 shed, 11°' of No. 8 Dock, Salford.</p>
        <p>ADDITIONAL PRECAUTIONS AT THE SWINO BRIDGES. 11,1 To obviate the unpleasantness which is causes- tig each vessel blowing its horn as it approaches the var`, °,O swing bridges which cross the canal in the Warring:7,4 district, a mast has been fixed at each of the swe'iy, bridges on the canal in the Warrington district; 1a5333 Net Stag Inn, Stockton Heath, and Latchford, with a Woo cone, which can be hoisted as required, for the PtEilloor of letting those in charge of approaching vessels "41 De that they have been sighted, and that the bridge Wlw` turned. Another improvemeat is the fixing Of ail+ netting on the gates, which has been rendered necesse et on account of the difficulty which the men in charg the bridges have experienced in preventing children . clambering through the bars of the gates and thervo exposing themselves to the danger of falling int° canal. ' 101' VISIT OF A MISSIONARY STEAMER TO RUN‘-r-q-) 0,1 The missionary ship " John Williams," which .0, moored to the right side of the Merse..y at Weston Runcorn; was visited by a large number of people. ~.vAvoi on Saturday and Monday. The vessel has been WE° doe built for service in the South Sea on behalf of the .11.114 Missionary Society. Durino Saturday afternoon a ~r 0 made to ate order of the ladies of the Manchester vi'as Salford anxiliary of the above natnA society. wile unfurled by Mrs. Jessie Haworth, of Bowdon. 11; composed of blue silk and bore a betutiful represeaol tion of a dove boneath which were the words Tidings." Mr. Harold Lee, of Manchester, preside" :•',o4' the proceedings. A hymn having been sung the,lS Dr. Hodgson offered prayer, during which he sa'.. hoped that the ship would be protected front storol'..lar the assaults of men, and that she would be the 17,00 ment of doing much good. The flag to be unfurl°, tile the symbol-of peace.—Amidst enthusiastic cheerisaily flag was then hoisted by Mrs. Haworth, the cu,ni, Pelia , subsequently joining in the singing of the hymn 1, the out the banner! let it float." The library whic‘fedo vessel contains has been stocked by the Youngti,litlor Committee of the Manchester Missions. A larae,?..iciai of scholars from the Ilethesda Congrec,rational „q",;pclai Schools. Runcorn, went on board the vessel on afternoon. MESSRS. FAIRCLOUGH AND THE SHIP CAS COMPANY. • ce .105" he In the Chancery Division; on Wednesday. Kekewich had before him the case of Faircloug."a WA' Manchester Ship Canal Company. This actl°„aitt thf,, brought by the plaintiffs some time ago to rest "gerse? defendants from extracting water from the river to.oeit in such a way as to interfere with the etIPPIYai Cof mills, and the Act of Parliament giving the Ca-„ as 6,1' party certain powers relating 0 this matter 97,,s to itt mitted to his lordship, who made a declaration,"to 091 construction, and the matter was to be referrlotont tration. Application was now made by INlr;sonapalli; QC., on behalf of the Manchester Ship eanal to vary the order made. Arrangements previoo3.Yboile.l: between counsel provided for the interferenc!ot; asitetuo at a certain point of the river, and it was 11 owing is that a different point should be substitute„d to h engineering difficulties. Mr. Moulton subu:''oP ~ lordship the other points which he refer eaotei: the plans would answer as well, and would P..'„. asked l'er for the Ship Canal Company to carry out. /-1 which° Id lordship to sanction the variation, and t you_ point the arbitrator decided the works nesessu'o' 'sad be carried out. Mr. Warmington, Q.C., cleirt variation on behalf of the plaintiff. After hear ~,e or, sel at some length his lordship refused to vary and dismissed the motion with costs. wit), as3jes' irte THE BEST NIGHTCAP. —Have your sheets Hudson's Soap, and they will always be sweet roe: as sea breezes. There will be none of thee (:t .114:46pkp ' smell, so objectionable Mother soapt.—A Par fine powder.—Adrt. (2). •</p>
