      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EARLESTOWN LIBERAL CLUB,</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN LIBERAL CLUB,</p>
        <p>THE LAND QUESTION. Under the auspices of the,Earlestown and dis- trict Liberal Club, a meeting was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening, to hear an address by Mr. Ben Riley, of the English Land Restoration League, on "The Monopoly of the Land." Mr. G. W. Rawlins was announced to pre- side, but he was unavoidably prevented from being present, and Mr. Wm. Davies officiated in his stead, and amongst those on the platform were Messrs. G. Ratcliffe, J. Cordingley, and other officials ef the club. The Chairman, after apologising for the absence of Mr. Rawlins, and said Mr. Riley went through the agricul- tural districts some time ago with the" Red Van," and was well acquainted with the land question. He was I connected with the English Land Restoration League. I Mr. Riley would not suggest the taking anything from I someone else which rightfully belonged to him, but he would advance the theory of restoring something that had originally been lost ; that was his (the speaker's) view of restoration. The object of the Land Restora- tion League was was the abolition of landlordism, and the method was to abolition of taxation upon labour, the products and earnings of labour, and the increase of taxation on land values, and for the revenue for this latter to be devoted to local purposes. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Ben Riley said he would deal with the question from an agricultural aspect, and an urban aspect. The land question resolved itself into two divisions,it had an agricultural aspect on the one hand, the town aspect on the other, and one aspect was entirely distinct from the other. In the first place he wished to point out that the importance of the land question, to their minds arose from the fact that during the past 25 years, there had been gradually coming to the front in England, two problems, and they were, on the one hand, the gradual growth of the unemployed question in every one of the large towns and industrial centres through- out the country,and the gradual but enormous decrease of the rural population, which had been taking place in I rural England during the past 20 years, but between I the two there was an intimate and vital connection. It ras somewhat strange, but true, that it was during this period of the last 20 years, that the unemployed ques- tion had been becoming prominent. During the present century we had witnessed in England a somewhat singu- lar spectacle ; we had witnessed the fact of the popula- tion of his country, going up from time to time by leaps and bounds. If they took the year 1801, they would find that the total population in that year was 9,0000,000 people. We had in England at that time about one third of the population engaged in the agricultural industry The singular fact was that if tbey came down to the year 1891, and took the last census returns presented by the Government, whereas the population of the country had gone up from 9,000,000 to 29,000,000, that out of that we had only got 4,000,000 who were concerned in the agricultural industry in England and Wales. In 1861 they had the first intimation of this movement of the rural populaticn from the rural districts to the towns. One of the consequences had been that during the past ten years especially, they had formed in all parts of industrial England the question of the unemployed becoming more prominent. Since the rise of the new trades unien movement, they had had a number of indications of the effect that this decrease of the rural population had been having on the industrial population. He wanted to point out how the monopoly ot agricultural land had had the effect of sending agricultural labourers from their native parishes and villages to the industrial towns and centres to com- pete against workmen whose wages were already low enough. The reason lay in th 3 monopoly which the landowners of agricultural England had been able to obtain over the land in the district in which they lived. This monopoly of the land had had the effect of reducing the agricultural labourer simply to nothing more than a serf, and whenever the opportunity had presented itselt of clearing out, he had done so as fast as he possibly could, in order to try and find better conditions in some other part of the country. The monopoly of agricultural land had had the effect • of destroying our agricultural industry altogether. The responsibility for the destruc- tion of English farming lay at the doors, and lay in the the hands of the landlords of this country. In North Lancashire the farmer was compelled to pay his labourer 163. or las. per week, because in his district there was the mine and factory, and if he would not give him this wage, he went either to the factory or mine, and the farmer could not help it, and he was thus compelled to pay the wage. The monopoly cf the land in Eagland, especially in the Southern and Eastern countries, had had the effect of reducing the agricultural. labourer to the verge of slavery. In a place called Compton Chamberlain, where the land belonged to one man only, and was rented by one farmer, who paid his men Cs. 7s. and 103. per week, and to a boy 14 years of age, act- ing as a shepherd boy, he was paying 21c1. per day—. (shame)—a branch of the labourers' union was formed in the place and the farmer when :he got to hear of it, sent out word to the first man who took up his card, would have to leave his house and clear out of the village, and this was the power that the monopoly of the land gave the farmers. They could not wonder at men from the Southern and Eastern counties leaving the place and coming to Liverpool and Manchester, in order to obtain better wages. If he had been oppressed by a landlord in his native parish, and he went to an industrial centre or a town or city, he found that there he had not by any means escaped the evils of landlordism, he found that while he had left one in his own pa,rish,he transfered his labour to another in the town or centre to which he had gone. The monopoly of the land in the towns was equally as rampant, and equally as important. Just as the Local Board spent their money. in improving the roads, they were simply doing nothing more than to make their town more valuable in order that their landlord might charge them teore rent. That was taking place in every part of the country. Mr. Griffin in his report said that during the past CO years,the rents of houses in towns had gone up 150 percent. The effect of landlordism in the towns was to bring toll upon the people who lived in the particular town, and just in proportion as the people in the towns increased in numbers, just at the same time, the levy or toll increased in proportion. Take London as a case in point. The working community, consisting of all sections of the working class,were mulcted for the right of living and carrying on their business ; for the right to use the site of London, for the right to stand on London and to carry on their business, they had got to hand over no less a sum than 16 million pounds per an- num to the Dukes of Bedford and Westminster. Not only that, but just as London was growing to-dayeand as its population increased, the capital value of the London lands was increasing at the rate of Vs millions per annum. What was taking place in London was taking I place in Farlestown, in quite al large a proportion as in I London. He did not knew whether he was quoting cor- rectly or not, but The was told that some 20 or 25 years ago that an acre of land in Earlestown could have been rented at about 22 or 22 10e.per acre. They could have had agricultural land at about £3 per acre, and he was told that the same land was now bringing to its owner about -270. By every legitimate means that could be devised ,this power of a landowner to levy tolls ought to he restricted. The time had come when the people of keigland should speak out with no uncertain voice, and should say, thst by constitutional measures of self local government, they shall have complete compulsory power to acquire land either in large or small quantities in the particular district without let or hindrance from the landlord under whom they reside—(applause)—and .to use that land in order to make it profitable without being hemmed in, and restricted, as they were now. (Hear, hear and applause.) He then proceeded to deal with the question of acquiring land at a nominal value pointing out that if the Local Board desired to acquire any land, they should suggest to the Imperial Parlia- ment should confer upon them the power to purchase land at any future time, at its original valuation. (Heal, hear.) The democracy had in their hands two weapons —trade organisation and political power, and it was the duty of every man who had got any aspiration to leave the world a little better than he found it, in season and , out of season to sharpen these weapons, and to have the highest wage he could. He felt: convinced that the power of capitalism was as sure to go down, as to- morrow's sun was going to rise. (Applause.) A vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer on the motion of Mr. Cordinglev. Songs were given during the evening by Misses L. Clare, H. Hill, Messrs. P. Hastie and J. Mooney.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>STOCKING AND PRICE-CUTTING IN THE COAL TRADE. IMPORTANT MINERS' CIRCULAR.</head>
        <p>STOCKING AND PRICE-CUTTING IN THE COAL TRADE. IMPORTANT MINERS' CIRCULAR.</p>
        <p>A circular is being distributed among St. Helens and Haydock miners stating that the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation have had before them the question of the large quantity of coal now being stocked throughout the county. Competition had already begun its deadly work, but as stocking and cutting down prices were also going on to an enormous extent in other coun- ties, the Lancashire Federation conclude that the ques- tion is a national one, and that the National Federation should lee asked to consider it as early as possible. The circular further states :—" There is no way of prevent- ing coal going into stock when there is not sufficient de- mand for the quantity raised only by restricting the out- wit. This can be done by working less hours per day and less days per week, or stop a week or more at one time. The latter may be agreed to at a national confer- ence; if so, you are requested to consider the advisability of carrying this out successfully. You must be prepared to play a week without expecting a week's pay from the funds of your association. If it is decided to restrict the output by working less hours or days you will expect nothing from the funds. To stop a week at one time is only another form of restriction, but cannot be carried ut if you demand to be paid. You are requested to consider the foll )wing suggestions, and decide upon them at your meetings : —(1) Are you in favour of restricting the output of coal; if so, by what process? (2) Are you in favour of working less hours per day; state the nar4a- ber ? (3) Less days per week; state the number? (4) Are you in favour of playing a week at a time? Meet- ings should be held at pits or lodges, and result'sent to officials of diatricts."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>TOWNSHIP MEETING AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>TOWNSHIP MEETING AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the ratepayers for the township of Newton-in-Makerfield for the passing of accounts and appointing overseers was held in the Town Hall, Newton, on Tuesday evening. Mr. John Adams presided, and there were present— Messrs. G. Ratcliffe and J.Sankey (overseers), R.Stuart, W. H. Hindley. V. Gilmour, G. Clarke, T. Wilkinson, &amp;c. Mr. H. Hbughton, assistant overseer, read the notice convening the meeting, and also the minutes of the last, the latter being duly confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>THE ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>He also read accounts for the past two half years. For the half year ended September 29, 1293, the receipts including a balance of £343 2s. 4.1. brought forward from the • previous half year, and a collected rate of £1,887 7s. 011, were £2,3131 us. 21d.,, and expenditure £1,847 4s. 60. Half year ended March 2, 1294, includ- int", a balance of £544 6s. 8-1-d., and a collected rate of £1,751 17s. 5d., the receipts were £2,320 us. Bd., and expenditure £1,758 85. Bd., leaving a balance of £562 3s.—Mr. Stuart said he would like to know what that district contributed to the union.—Mr. Houghton said about £3,274 for last year.—The accounts were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PARISH COUNCILS BILL.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCILS BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour asked what would be the position of the Guardians when the Parish Councils Bill came into force.—Mr. Houghton said he had only received a copy of the Act that evening, and had not been able to go through it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>APPOINTMENTS,</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENTS,</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Speakman, seconded by Mr. Major, Mr. H. HoughtQn was reappointed assistant overseer for the ensuing year. The following gentlemen were nominated for submission to the magistrates as overseers :—Messrs. J. Sankey, G. Ratcliffe, Stuart, (;rouchley, Clarke, Simpson. Messrs. E.Fairclough and B. Harrison were re-elected auditors. Messrs. Hibbert and W. Winstanley were appointed common lookers. Messrs. Z. Rindle!y, John Bridge, atrel W. Longton were appointed burleymen.</p>
        <p>_ _ INCIDENTAL RATE. An incidental rate of a halfpenny in the pound was laid.</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS TO THE TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>The following letter was read from Commander Royse :—" Newton-le-Willows, March 20, 1801. Dear Sir,—l understand there is to be a meeting of the over- seers to-night. Could you draw their attention to the great desirability of erecting a permanent platform of a sufficiently large sin to accommodate the various con- cert and other performers who so often use the lown Hall ? A stage could very easily be erected at the end - opposite the present small bench, and as Mr. Brierley's office is closed, and the Commissioners' room no longer used by them, as I gather, capital green rooms could be made of them, with an entrance on to the back of the stage. At present it is a severe tax upon all concert or other managers to have to pay for the erection of a temporary stage, besides the unnecessary and undue wear and tear to the hall, and loss of time and money putting it up and taking it down. People often complain to me of the paucity of entertainments or anything to amuse the young people of Newton, but it is no wonder to me that managers fight shy of the present inconveni- ence of the Newten Town Hall.—Yours faithfully, Ed C. ROYSE, Corn !pander N."—M r. Gilmour thought the suggesti,n contained in the letter was a capital one,and as the Commissloners had now left the hall, it was a good place for concerts, and about the only place in Newton, and if they could only make it so that it could be adapted for concerts, they could easily put a little more on the charge for the convenience.—Mr. Stuart asked whether the overseers had sketched anything out as to the alterations intended to be made in the hall ; if so he thought they should take the ratepayers into their confidence.—Mr. Gilmour suggested that it was about time the overseers did something to the hall, He understood that Mr. Brierley's late office was being used for no purpose, and it had been suggested to him that the workingmeir's club downstairs being sometimes inconvenient, it might perh-ips be a better thing if the reading room was placed there, and the bottom room used for more popular papers and a smoke room, and they might have an opening from the hall into the Commissioners' old room, which could be used as a retiring room during the concerts. Another complaint he had was that the lawyers had no accom- modation for consulting with their clients on Court days, and if the' had that room opened out it would make a most convenient room, and the expense would not be very great, and unless there was some very substantial objection raised to it, he should put it in a form of a proposition.—The Chairman said the idea had occurred to him that if they were to pass a resolution empowering a committee to carry out the recommendation, it might facilitate matters. They were questions which required a little consideration.—Mr. H. Houghton thought there would be no difficulty in altering the building to the ,convenience of the ratepayers if a reselution was passed. , He thought the suggestion of the Chairman to appoint a • committee to assist the overseers would be the fairest way of going about it.—Mr. Stuart proposed "That in the opinion of this meeting it is desirable that extra ac- commodation be found, and an additional retiring room with an opening into the large room of the Town Hall, and such other alterations as the committee may deem desirable be provided, and that Messrs. Clarke and Simp- son be a committee to confer with the overseers en the matter.''—Mr. W. H.Handley seconded.—On the motion of Mr. Speakman, Captain Royse and Mr. I'. Wilkinson were added to the committee.—The motion was then put and unanimously carried. It was proposed by Mr. Speakman, and seconded by Mr. Stuart, that the accounts for the ensuing year be printed and sold at the next annual meeting at a penny per copy.—This was all the business.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S COCOA.—GRATEFUL AND COMFORTING.— "By a thorough knowledge of the natural laws which govern the , operations of digestion and nntrition,and bye careful applica- tion of the fine properties of well-selected Coma, Mr. Epps has provided' for our breakfast and supper a delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doctors bills. It is ty the judicious use of such articles of diet that a constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every tendency to disease. We may escape many a fatal shaft by keeping ourselves well fortified with pure blood and a properly nourished frame."—" Civil Service Gazette."—Made simply with boiling water or raiik.—Sold only in packets, by Grocers, labllied--" JAMES EPPS Ft Co.. Itd , nceapathic Chemists London."—Also makers of Epps's Cocoaine or Cocoa Nib-gxtract A thin beverage of full flavour, now with many beneficially taking the place of tea. Sold in Warrington by Bowden, Bamford, and Perclval. —A dvt.</p>
      </div>
