      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>TUE TOWN CLOCK.</head>
        <p>TUE TOWN CLOCK.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. F. W. Reynolds, has evidently- not, as yet, attained to that philosophical love of correction which ,shall enable him to bear having his own faults of method and his especial darling's errors of periormance being shown. lam grieved that your correspondent should.. have exhibited such petulance at the criticisms I made upon his methods. Now, Horology is a scientific art, and as such shou!d be treated scientifically ; but when 'a man proposes to rate a turret clock to 0 255. at inter- vals of seven or eight days he shows himself either incapable, or neglectful in this. F. W.R.accuses me of ,making" unjustifiable reflections upon the accuracy of the Town Clock." Now, as this sentence has a tearful sound, I hasten to assure Mr.Rey- iaolds that, up to this date, I have cast no slur upon the clock's accuracy, but I have distinctly so, upon his methods of detecting these. A love of truth rather than respect for the feelings of F.W.R. impels ice to remark that Trinity clock is one which none need to copy as a model ot horelogical perfection. It has the one radical defect of had pendulum suspension—a defect fatal to good time keeping, let the rest of the clock be as good as can be. A turret clock pendulum ought to be hurz from the most solid of solid masonry—and not from a gimcrack iron box, swaying by every breeze of wind. Your correspondence columns have of late—very lately—shown sonic wonderful examples of people prosimg upon purely scientific roatteri while minus the science necessary to deal adequate:y with them. But lam convinced that this is not the case with Mr. Reynolds—he has merely forgotten, or overlooked these fundamental facts. I may perhaps without presumption, say I have some knowledge "of the principle of the gravity escapement " ; but again perhaps Mr. Reynolds has forgotten more horology than I ever knew. As Mr. Reynolds has so chivalrously thrown if his anonymity I can but in courtesy follow his exam- ple. In doing this, may I assure him that hitherto, as now, my sole wish is that the " truth shall prevail."— Yours, kc.</p>
        <p>JOHN PEERS.</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH AND ITS SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—A few weeks ago the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton read a paper before the Warrington Debating Society, taking the above caption for his subject. It is right ta say that I persosially feel obliged to the rev, gentleman for the open and kindly spirit in which he treated the subjest. Ground was covered that does without doubt, include the two most important sociological factors now disturbing the human mind, the Church as an ideal .society, and the relationship of labour to capital. The rev. gentleman-said, " When I speak of the Church, I do not speak of the Church of England, but the Church at large everywhere and in every age, of which the Church of England only claims to be it part." The read-. jog of this caused me to pause a little and think. To think was but to ask a question : Was it always thus? Nay, to ask a more emphatic question, because, bearing ia mied that the speaker's underlying principle was the solidity or oneness of the Church,the speasser went on to say : "Now the Church I think we may take it, is that ideal society whose constitution is indicated with more or less clearness in the bible." Is it quite true? Now, I ask, has the Church ef England at any time in its history, or does it to-day, recognise itself as being only a part of an ideal church, whose constitution is in- dicated with more or less clearness in the bible I must confess to a litrle haziness just here, because I am not quite clear as to the essentials of a church. The eccle- siastic may say, "I am the Church," or he may say the " Church is built upon the more or less clearness of the bible," or, if lie be a little " Eligh " lie may fancy the ritual of the prayer book as the foundation stone. I take it that for all practical purposes we must accept the ecclesiastic as being the Church,and this is just the rock upon which we all founder. Dean Alford wrote twenty- five years ago :—" . . . . Now as we pass onward and look at other characteristics of the Christianity of the present, v. e find a defect closely connected with that which we have already con,idered, and that is, want of any well-defined idea of what constitutes the Church. What I am about to say applies perhaps less to Noncon- formist bodies ; but I can testify to the utter confusion which prevails amony rxembers of my own body on the subject. Bound to a hard and dry theory of salvation in a certain form of Church alone, and yet unable to deny to thousands outside that Church the faith and charac- ter of Chris'ians, they take refuge in amiable inconsis- tencies, allowing to their fellow-Christians salvation by God's uncovenanted mercies, or finding by some equally unmeaning terms an excuse for that conviction which is forced upon them,that these outsiders, as they regard them, are in the way.of salvation." My anxiety is not so much to disprove Mr. Carleton's claim to a united ideal Church, whose existence is "everywhere," so much as it is to the firelina• of a Church which shall provide the means for bringing to- gether the children of one common Father. It would be useless quoting authorities in disclaim of Mr. Carleton's ideal united Church. They are numerous almost as the snowflakes around me, and it is well that it is so. A church that forms itself into a " property protection society—one for the rich and one for the poiv," has out- lived its time, and its farther existence is hut a pro, longation of the death struggle between labour and capital. It is not true to say the Church embraces within its folds the capitalist and the artisan. It is only necessary to look inside any church for disproof of this. It is only true to say that the intellect of Europe and America as well as the artisan easa leave the Church severely alone, and that the Church dspends almost en- tirely upon the tradesman and the capitalist, the latter feeling that it is the only whip he has with which to keep us wretches in order. The rev, gentleman tells us that the Church is a "vol- untary society." One is almost forced to smile at the simplicity of such a statement. This society of which he speaks in such sweet honeyed words is, and I think he knows it, nothing loss than a huge political machine. During the discussion of the Parish Councils Bill it has been shown time and again how much voluntary action the poor peasant would have accarded him could the Church of England branch of the ideal church work its own sweet will. When Lord Salisbury said that it was a circus and not a Parish Council that the peasantry wanted, he spoke for his class, and, I opine for Mr. Carleton too. If he will confess with shame that it is too true, he may be forgiven the part he has taken in the past in supporting. Sir G. Greenall to ignore those words in the Lord's Prayer, "Load us not into tempta- tion. but deliver us from evil." I tell Mr. Carleton that the brewery standing at Wilderspool is the strongest argument against his speech that could possibly be given in evidence. It stands for capital against labour for the classes against the masses, for the Church of England against the people, and it has the ecclesiastic for its tool at the bottom. If lam charged with hastiness in mak- ing this statement, I answer that every writer on the social condition of England points to drink as its great- est curse. Every unprincipled employer hopes to gain his ends through the ruinous work of drink. If the ideal Church of England of which Mr. Carleton is a member were to at once withdraw itself from the drink traffic, refuse to in any way to support a brewer, and neither directly or indirectly take money which had been gained</p>
        <p>in the curse of sod's, it might then, and not till- then, turn to the cure of soolQ with a clear conscience..</p>
        <p>It would be untrue I,f body to speak of itself as having a voluntary existence. Mr. Moody speak- ing some years ago said. "If I believed there was no hell I am sure I would be off to-morrow for America. I would go back to my own country and take things easy." Where is the voluntaryism in this? It is because of such preaching that we leave the Church ,and it is the existence .sf such a dPctrine that preronts the construc- tion of anything like an ideal united Church. The time is now upon us when ecclesiasticism should receive its notice to quit. It is out of joint with the times. Count Tolstoi in his last book,the " Kingdom of God," tells ecclesiastics that " the Church has been one of the princiry I hindrances to the proper comprehension of Christianity." Although the estrangement between the industrial classes and the churches is complete it is only fair to allow with Dr. Fairbairn that the disbelief is in the churches rather than in religion. Cotter Morison tells us in his bock "The Service of Man," that " a Catholic Christian of the fifth, and one of the 19th cen- tury, would, if they could meet in the flesh, agree in reference to the creeds of the Church, but they would be able to agree in little besides." This is the trouble; the creGd is forced upon us instead of the Sermon on the Mount. The Church demands belief in a dogma defined in the fourth century, a dogma that carries with it CO meaning. We now ask that the meaningless worship of deities shall give place to the "Service of Man," and, as Ruskin has put it, the endeavour shall be to please God rather than to praise him.—Yours truly, Worcester, Mass, U.S.A.</p>
        <p>'EL WHITHAM,</p>
        <p>SOCIALISM. Sir,—Mr. T. Y. Howcroft is not he says, prepared to be taught doctrines which his teacf%r does not adopt. I have shown to him that individualism is opposed to Christian doctrine, and he resents the lesson because I as a Socialist do not put that doctrine into practice myself! We will, if you please, Mr. Howcroft, keep to a compari- son of the two systems, and exhibit an enlightened Christianity by embracing that which cows nearest the noblest ideal. It is our duty, I take it, to reach that ideal and towards that end we must remove all obstacles aad systems that stand in our way to it. Man is to a</p>
        <p>great extent a creature of circumstances, or to use another word, of systems. It is quite possible for a member of the House of Lordl to be convinced of the harmfulness of a second chamber but he cannot be other than he is until the law is altered. It is the same with the Socialist; he will be a " real Socialist " when it is possible for him to practice Socialism. At present it is not.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howcroft's argument that the capitalist by his thrift is enabled to cheapen the cost of production and provide tor the labourer the same degree of comfort with a less expenditure is a characteristic specimen of his reasoning. He fails to distinguish between the capital- ist and capital. Capital may unquestionably assist in the production of advantageous machinery, but it is not necessary for that capital to be owned by one individual. Besides the capitalist does not always obtain his capit ii (or wealth). by thrift, but frequently by less honourable means. So that little argumentative barricade of the individualist reasoner crumbles to the ground. But here is another beautiful illustration of our Yorkshire friend's—quite a little gem in its way. I mean about the farmer _putting in the potatoes and the labourer being hired to dig up the crop, and then claiming nine out of every ten—giving only one back to the farmer for every one lie put in. He cited this as an illustration of my position as a Socialist. Think it worth while deal- ing with this absurdity, Mr. Editor ? It is within the realm of probability that a labeurer of this description does exist, but I have not the exquieite pleasure of his acquaintance, and lam always on the look-out for cur- ious phenomena. Nevertheless, when Ido come across him I will let you know Mr. Howcroft. As a set off to Mr. Howcroft's potato story, allow me r to present an illustration of Mr. How,roft's individual- ism. Here is a farm of one hundred acres. A man who calls himself the owner has an income of £2OO a year from it. Another one who undertakes to cultivate itelfter paying his labourers, has an income of £183; the two labourers have each 154. a week. Which of ttie four men does the hardest work? Oh, says the gentle reader, the man with the £2OO income, of course. Dear me, what inexperience ; that is the man who does no- thing at all except receive the money; and the two men who work hard from morning till night make a bare living. Who will dare say after this that de are not a justice loving race? That farm is handed down from sire to son from generation to generation, and not one is required to do a stroke of work for the £2OO a• year which comes out of the labour of the farmer and his men. The man of the Middle Ages seems to be a rather tough individual for Mr. T. Y. H. I showed him by figures and by means of the quoted opinions of Hallam and Mr. Wm. Morris, and by a reference to Professor Thorold Rogers, that as regards the means of living, the man of the -Middle Ages compared favourably with the ninteenth century workman ; and he is astounded at my audacity. Mr. H. spurns the recognised authorities alluded to, an I as he gives no authority in support of his own contention, except his own ipse dixit (which he will excuse me if I decline to accept) I shall be justi- fied in leaving this point to the judgment of your readers. But I notice in order to get out of the predi- cament into which his rashness has led him, he actually contrasts the sanitary system of the Middle 'Ages with our improved methods of to-day! In my former letter I ventured to express the truism that there were higher Ideals than mere money making. Mr. H. agrees,but says we cannot carry them out with- out money. Exactly ; without time and without a suffi- ciency you cannot. If a worker's time is all taken up in producing hurtful luxuries for others to consume, he is kept in a state of degradation. Mr. Howcroft says. it is a fact that a man has to work to live. It is, indeed, a great fact. I daresay I could show him more in- stances than he would care to be acquainted with, , where it is a terrible, a cruel, and a shameful fact. My , God! I !save wondered at the patience of these people. Oh, how I have wondered. Mr. Howcroft declares the an who carefully culti- vates his talents should not be brought down to the level of the man who neglects his. Strange arguments from a man who wants things as they are. Everything depends upon how the talents are used. If a man em- ploy's his talents to the injury of the community, it would, relatively, be a kindness if he could be induced to neglect their cultivation. But if there be anything in this argument it is that the moneyed men are all talented men, that is, that their talents have been of advantage to the community. Which, of course,is put- ting too great a strain upon one's credulity. Arthur Helps says that the preponderating number of inven- tions have come frown the humble classes—and by the way how many of theiti have been robbed by men who have sedulously cultivated the talent of appropriation, and how many of them have died poor ? As to Carlvle, and Emerson, and Darwin and Ruskin, I quoted these as examples of men who had not done their great work merely from selfish motives, and Mr. Howcroft retorts that we should not have had their work at all, or rather Darwin's, if the latter had not belonged to the class who reap where they do not sow. That, of course, is another argument altogether. Observe that my point was that money making does not constitute tile only stimulus to useful work ; that under Socialism more elevated incentives would be developed ; but that in spite of the present system which fosters the bucca- neerin!, spirit there is sufficient of altruism in some aien to life them above the circumstances of their lives. At the same time it was very brave of Mr. ll.s.vcroft to halloo so loully before getting out of the wood. Does the Carlyle-Emerson-Darwin-Ruskin argument support mv contention that money getting is not the sole incen- tive to exertion? Does it not proye,as I said before,th at the best men consider their ability should be employ ed for the public good and not merely for their own selfish gratificat on ? If so then hew is it " a proof of the other side." The other point however, raised by Mr. Howcroft, needs an answer. That is : It was necessary for D.tr- win to be possessed of wealth, otherwise we should not have had his researches. In other words a man must ! have money to be a geeius. Could there be anything so utterly absurd ? If I had time I could compile a for- midable list of men who have mil:erred benefits upon , the people, and all of them poor men. Ebnius can , invent a machine without money, but tri3ney cannot , inventa machine without genius. A man of ability exercises his ability because he likes to exercisa it. Edi- son, who was originally a poor man and has always worked hard, was asked why he went on inventing. Edison looked as if the question had never occurred to him before as he answered "I like it." It was not nec,:s- sary for Edison to be a rich man in order to be an inven- t r. I has,e no desire to subj ~ct Mr. Elowcroft to the peculiar process of " bouncing " against which lie has so pronounced an objection, but I do wish to ask him if the most industrious men are the wealthiest, if the best men an, best paid; if they ever get rich in feet; whether Handel and Shakespeare, Darwin and Columbus, Steph-</p>
        <p>prison and Humpliry Davies were richer men than Jay Gould or the, &amp;stors. 'There II think that is enicilt for one week, and I must close, if I have any re,ard at all for the editorial detnand :or brevity, ad if I mean also to maintain my reputation for</p>
        <p>COMMON JUSTICE.</p>
        <p>OWNSHIP MEETING AT lATCHTORD.</p>
        <p>On Thumlay the annual meeting of the rate- payers for the township of Latchford for the pass- ing of accounts and appointing overseers, was held at the Norton Arms Hotel. Mr. John Lunt presided, and there were els° present Councillors J. Shaw Green,and James Fairclough ; Mw:srs.James Strange, J. Sampson, Edmund Johnson. J. Wainwright, Mr. Tunstall, assistant over4eer, and Tr. .1. Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>EXPECTED INCREASED RATE.</head>
        <p>EXPECTED INCREASED RATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tunstall read the balance sheet for the year ended 29th September. 1893. which showed that the receipts from rates w:7re £635 53. 2d., and also an incidental income of 93. 6d. The balance in hand was L 219 173. 41. The accounts were passed on the motion of Mr. Sampson seconded by Mr. Johnson. Mr. Sampson wished to know. whether the extra 21. rate, imposed last half-year, was ' to be again charged this time. The extra 21 rate was charged on the strict understanding that it was to laFt for six months only, the reason being that Mr. Shaw Green said there was not sufficient in the bank to pay , the calls without an extra 21 rate. Mr. Tunstall said he thought it w,- uld be necessary to put something more on. The Overseers received the precepts from the Town C,4uncil and from the Guardians for certain sums of money and these sums had to be paid. They therefore had to make a rate accordingly. Be was of the opinion that the rate would be 4d. or 6d. more. The county ra'e was double, the borough call he expected would be an increased one and the call for poor law relief would ha very largely increased as compared with the corres- ponding half-year. he thought it would be fifty or sixty per cent. more.—Mr. Faircloug,h : It is a very serious matter.—Mr. Green: Latchford ha 3 been more highly rated than the Warrington borough.—Mr. Fairclough thought the coal strike had something to do with the increase.—Mr. Shaw Green : The county rate is certain to incruase.—Mr. Fairclough said it appeared to him that the County Council got all the credit and other people did all the work. THE ASSISTANT OTIRSEER'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Chairman proposed and Mr. Strange seconded that the remuneration to the collector for extra rates collected should be £lO for each extra rate. Mr. Fair- dough asked how many extra rates there were. It was explained that there was cne at present—the rural rate. The motion was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>APPOINTMENTS.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>It was moved by Mr. Fairclough, seconded by Mr. Johnson and carried unanimously, that the following names be submitted to the magistrates as overseers : Messrs. John Lunt, James Strange, Edward Greenall, A. Hill, G. Dakin, William Pierpoint, Joseph Ashton, John Sampson, James Dalton, James Hallwood, David Wm. Finney, Wm. Fairhurst and Wm. Twiss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PARISH COUNCILS BILL.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCILS BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tunstall mentioced that when the Parish Coun- cils Bill came into force the township would have to be divided into two townships,and a Parish Council elected for the rural area. But it would not interfere with the township rnePtings which would be held as usual. The Parish Council woutld deal with separate business altogether. A vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman, on tho motion of Councillor Fairclough, seconded by Councillor J. Shaw Green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EXAMINER SA T [RD AV. MARCH 21, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SA T [RD AV. MARCH 21, 1894.</p>
        <p>:lealt with. Of course there are two sides to the ques- tion, but on the whole it has been brought home to us'-- and I for one have no hesitation in saying absolutely and unequivocally, and for reasons so ably stated—that the contracting out clause is in principle wrong, and I do not think that the position on this matter, taken by our Tory democratic friends, is altogether calcula!ed to in- spire their democratic supporters with confidence. (Hear, hear.) Their action in this matter, in support- ing the House of Lords,reininds one of another aphorism of Mr. Bright's, when he remarked that the Tory policy consisted largely in giving with one hand what is quietly taken back with the other. (Applause.) Ido not intend to touch upon all questions raised in the course of this debate, so I must pass on to more important indictments levelled against us. The main feature of the attack made upon us is that we are a miserable party of "LITTLE ENGLANDERS." (Hear. hear.) We are told that we mean to scatter the Empire to the four winds of Heaven, and that we ought to remember—to adopt a recent phrase—" that it is our business to pee nut. claims fot futurity." (Hear, hear.) Gentlemen, in Heavens name—if that is a fit expression in this case—what have we been doing? You have talked about the Dark Continent. What is our record there during the present Parliament ? Has our foreign policy during the last 12 months not been sufficiently progres- sive or aggressive for you ? (Applause.) We admit that the position of the Liberal party as a whole, on these matters, is not the same as it was half a century since— (Hear, hear,)—but from that period our position has , been changed in two important essentials. First of all , the pure individualism of that day, which in measures of emancipation did invaluable and indisputable work, has got through a large part of its prog,ra.mine.—(hear, hear)—and to-day we have to deal not with individual- ism,but with qualified and largely qualifiedindividualual- ism which will contribute, as I have said before, as much as anything to Conservatism, in the best sense of the word,and to a superiority of our people in the future. (Applause.) Another point I referred to amongst those questiors which have arrived with the times, is this very foreign policy, of which in one name or another, so much has been said this evening. (Applause.) Liberal opinion has necessarily advanced somewhat--though as to the breaking .up of the Empire and such nonsense, remember it was Cobden himself who said he would vote for the expenditure of a hundred millions if neces- sary to keep our ocean highways open. But, gentlemen, , it is the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WARkINGTON DEBA.TING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARkINGTON DEBA.TING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>THE POLITICAL SITUATION. SPEECH. BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. The last meeting of the session, in • connection with the Warrington Debating Society, was held on Tuesday evening, the subject for debate being "The Political Situation." Mr. A. H. Crosfield occupied the chair, and the debate was opened by Mr. E. J. HALL, who said the year had opened with rumours that Mr. Gladstone, the Nestor of politics, was about to resign. At first they were apt to be incredulous, but day by day the report had gathered strength and at the end of the long session just past, they began to realize that the man whose colossal personality had filled their lives was indeed about to relinquish his dignities and activities. (Applause.) For the past 25 years, politics in England had meant Gladstone, either for or against. Now they were called upon to believe that all this was ended. What of the Liberal party now—Glad- stonian without Gladstone? And what was the result ? Simply the creatian of an impossible situation. That one youne man, and he a peer, should lead Whigs and Radicals, "brewers and vetoists, Churchmen and Libera- tionalists, Nationalists and Parnellites, great employers of labour and trade unionists, and all the other items of that heterogeneous and ragged army of twenty com- panies marching rampantly on with so many different objects, would be like attempting to drive the chariot of . the sun. Labouchere had begun to play his little game, and anyone would be blind if they could not see that he had not by any means finished. He (Mr. Hall) asked whether the declaration of Lord Rosebery, "I am a ' second chamber man," would satisfy the rising millions who were constantly crying out at putslic meetings in Hyde Park and elsewhere, "End it, end it." The cry against the House of Lords was a cry against Lord Rose- bery. (A Voice: "Nothing of the sort.") At Edin- burgh he had made a speech and in that retracted a clear and unmistakeable statement, relative to Home Rule, which he had made the previous week. He ap- peared to be adopting a policy of evasion, and had clearly climbed down from his high estate. Mr. HORTON said he considered that Mr. Hall had made a misleading statement when he intimated that Lord Rosebery in being in favour of a second chamber was in favour of the House of Lords. (Hear. hear.) The House of Lords and a second chamber were two separate and distinct institutions —(hear, hear)—and Lord Rosebery in pronouncing himself a- "second chamber man" was in accord with the policy of his party. (Applause.) Mr. A. BENNETT said that in his opinion the Liberal party had selected as the premier the fittest person, in Lord Rosebery, and there were many features of the policy which he advocated that would induce him (the speaker) to attach himself to the Liberal party were it not for the insular and narrow policy of the the party on Imperial matters, which made the party a band of little Englanders. (Hear, hear.) It was the duty of England to look to the future in its foreign policy and " to peg out claims for posterity." (Hear, hear.) Messrs. Donellon, C. Lyon and J. Neil also spoke on the subject of debate. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who next addressed the meet- ing, said : It is with a feeling of hesitation, and I might almost say despair, that I rise in the vain attempt to sum up adequately, even a portion of the arguments of the various speakers, so discursive have they been and covering so much ground in the field of politics. (Hear, hear.) For a number of years I have taken much interest in the political questions which have come within the ranee of this debate, and if you will hear with me for a few minutes I will try to deal with some of the points, and particularly with some of the criticisms of the pelicy of our party which have been :raised. (Hear, hear.) First of all, in reference to the retirement of Mr. Glad- stone, I am sure we all appreciate the kindly and appro- priate rempel,- "-at have fallen. from Mr. Hall. (Hear, hear.) The. ea feature in the political life of the ex-premier whin ;a admitted on all sides. Apart from his immense energy and diversity of his intellectual attainments and achievements, such as have been the modern wonder of the world, Mr. Gladstone has gone into his campaigns with an evident earnestness And a moral weight, which he has not only possessed himself —(applause)—leut which he has been able to impress upon others. (Renewed applause.) And just as from this seurce lie has in a great measure derived, and still retains, an influence with the people, SO LORD ROSEBERY, though as a previous speaker had said, wrapped up in seclusion, and absorbed in the heavy work of•the foreign office, secluded as it were from popular gaze—we believe, though no sentimentalist in the objectionable sense of the word—is imbued ith a strong sense of moral obliga- tion, not the least important factor in the character of leadership, which the great bulk and mass of the Liberal party believe will stand them in good stead in the future. (Applause.) Lord Rosebery, it has been said this even- ing, under the exigencies of party discipline, " climbed down " from his high estate. In what manner has this change for the worse been shown ? I fail to sea it ? (Applause...) We stand, as Lord Rasebery said at the foreign office, "where we stood before the disas- trous change in men at the front." (Applause.) He stands there with us stiil, and in his recent speeches has both re-emphasized the opinions and polity held by the party to which he has always belonged. (Applause.) It was said in the opening speech this evening that Lord Rosebery would at an early date make shipwreck,finding it impossible to lead such .a heterogeneous, disoreanized, and ragged—l think that was the particular adjective used—party. We are quite aware of the many and strong shades of opinion included in the Liberal party, hut it is not the first time that we have had variety in our rank, and provided we go to work, with common sense, willing to adopt such a rational and n9cessary amount of the principle of give and takef as is essential in other branches of life in the interests of harmonious co-operation and progress, we shall be able, I think, to ' stand up against all machinations in spite of certain tied ar*qion.s to the contrary, and even condescending offers of patronage. (ApplAuse.) I think we must adopt this principle in common prudence and discipline. (Re- newed applause.) John Bright precisely hit off the posi tion when lie said "You have the van, the main body and the rear guard of your political army. The van must go a little slower and the rear guard a little faster" and se we get the momentum without which effort is often wasted. (Hear, hear.) I chink it is rather irra- tional to cendemn the whole Liberal party en masse, like .the speaker who preceded me, just because we did not see our way to pin our faith to this or the other extreme proposal which is made to is. (Hear, hear.) The now familiar question of the House of Lords has of course come up to-night. We have been very lone suffering towards thin; but it is in the nature of Englishmen, amongst all of whom there is a considerable substratum of Conservatism,the natural result of contentment which follows upon cemparetire comfort, for after all we are ! better off than other people. (Laughter and applause.) This is unquestionably the reason for that long-suffer- ine, forbearance on matters of class legislation and leeis- ' lative privileges which as we knew exist as an anomaly in our constitution. I have not got in my pocket any -cut and dried scheme for the mending of the second chamber ; but whatever WO may call it, let us have a. reform at least Radical in this, that we get rid of the present belated and unprofitable qualification, namely, the principle of heredity as the primary quelifieation,or noqualification at all for the secondchamber.(Applause.) Let us have a second chamber filled with competent men, and in which the Liberal party have something ' like a fair representation. (Hear, hear.) Given that much, I do not see why a second chamber should not prove on the whole advantageous. (Hear, hear ) Without such a Radical alteration we cannot stand it. (Bear, hear) and we are very certain that Lord Rose- bery is not the man to flinch when the time comes for action. (Hear, hear.) Lord Rosebery is n,t the sort of weakling to hark hack on his avowed political convic- tions, whether in respect to the House of Lords, or the re7st of the Liberal prry,ramrne to which he has now more serious obligations than ever. (Applause ) I will awn at once that it was a pity he usedlanguaee—or rather a chance phrase or two in the course of an answer to some question, owing to the hasty purposes of &amp;beta— open to misconstruction, (Applause). But I must sae that the gentleman who opened the debate made a great error in the insinuation he indulged in. For the sake of his brilliant reputation here it is a pity hese entirelr over- shot his mark (applause) in his endeavour—l think this was the gist of his retna'rks—to prove that Lord Rose- bery hes in his recent speeches shown a disposition to HARK BACK ON HOME RULE Lord Rosebery did not become a Home Ruler yesterday. (Hear, hear.) I believe he made a speech. I forgot the 1 exact year, but at a very early date. (Mr. Webster : " It was in 1884 at Paisley.") Jam much obliged to you for the information. In that speech he anticipated almost every other leader on the progressive side unlese it was Mr. -Morley (applause) who, though he did not espouse the cause on the platform till later, before that time paved the way by the views he made public in the " Nineteenth Century "still further back in the" eigh- ties." (Applause.) Lord Rosebery in fact has always been in the van of the question, and no more uncom- promising statement upan it could have been made than what he said in Leeds in, I think, 1887. In the face of this, do you think it possible that Lord Rosebery would venture to have taken the position of premier, and in one of his very first speeches afterwards largely recant the policy which Mr.Gladstnne had adopted and in which he has had the support of the bulk of the party? (Hear, hear.) Why such a suggestion is preposterous—(applause)—but if it were possible, do you believe Mr. Morley would have. taken office under the new Premier without a clear de- claration and knowledge that he intended to follow out the same lines of policy? (N0,n0.) He is not a man of that sort. (Hear, hear.) He is no mere office seeker. (Applause.) No one in the House is actuated more than he by the principle Of single-minded integ,rity,and we all know that he would scorn to curry ignoble favour, just because he thought the cause was unpopular, by recant- log one iota of the p(licy which he so ardently advo- cated for Ireland.</p>
        <p>THE EMPLOYER'S LIABILITY BILL is a subject on wkich I should like to say a word, if only in recognition of the able, and I will add, eloquent address, in which the contracting out clause has been</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>DARK CONTINENT</head>
        <p>DARK CONTINENT</p>
        <p>cthich has been referred to specially this evening, and which I think, each sneaker has chiefly had in rnind in referring to aur foreign policy. (Hear, hear.) Let us leave generalities then, and get to facts—(hear, hear)— though these are not always as easy to get hold of in such matters. (Hear,hear.) Controversy has,of course turned lately upon his Matabele campaign. I will not prolong these remarks by attempting to criticise either the relations between the Matabele and Mashonas, Ltbengula's overtures,and any of those difficulties on the frontier which had been forced upon us, or served as an excuse for this war. A frierd of mine writes me from the Cape that Rhodes is a second Clive, and that he has ability to build up a great South African empire. (Hear, hear.) Well, we all appreciate Mr. Rhodes' abilities,and I wrote my friend badi that the Liberal party, as he would observe, sanctioned the war. I could not say much more, for once I took a similar view to that of the "Spectator," which journal, gentlemen, as you know, has like some of our Unionist friends,gone over bag and baggage to the Tory party. H you view the matter from the standpoint of those who accept only the doc- trine of the survival of the fittest iii its most extreme form—if you say it is only a question between us and other European nations in acquiring the territories of the Aborgines—it may still he objected that there are pleaty of those regions in the Dark Continent where,as Mr.flar- rison pointed out, black people can thrive much better than white. But still on these, and such grounds, a fair case may be made out by those who defend the cam- paign. (Hear, hear.) We all admire the pluck and capacity of our soldiers whoofter all are the best in the world. But as I wrote my friend, for the campaign in general it would be easier to get up enthusiasm if we I ad not a true opinion of the motives which prompted it. (riear,hear.) As I say, gentlem-m,we may sanction this a necessary,and perhaps,in the long run,inevitable, but let us stop there. (Hear, hear.) What is the usc, of boasting of the spread of the Gospel among such people, as some have done to-night ? You forget to te'l us while you carry the bible with one hand you have the gin bottle in the other—rank poison to people unedu- cated and undisciplined in sell-control, and I must say there seems to me little credit in battles where modern machinery enables us to slaughter wholesale natives long before they have got big enough to strike a blow for themselves. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Let us then look at both sides of this question,the grim aide as well as the other,which is said to show such promises for the future. (Applause.) One other topic and then I have done : namely, what we call</p>
        <p>ROME RULE ALL ROUND. Gentlemen it is for Lord Rosebery to carry to the Eng- lish mind the conviction the, in devolution, and not in impossble and imprActical centralization, lies the chief hope and safty of the Empire. (Hear, hear.) But at Out same time, whatever the need for a Grand Corn- mite for B,ote.h affairs, and increased control of their local matters, we are not so short sighted as to think that the Nationalists of Scotland and %Vales stand ucon an equal footing or anything like it with Ireland. (Ap- plause.) And we have learned to recognize what the Unionists ignore, but what their own historian Leckey can best remind them of, and that is the great differenc.-, in the historical sides to the question, and the import- ance of this side of the Irish demand. (114ar, hear.) Let us deal with one thing at a time and let us seize the best opportunity we have at hand for relieving the Imperial Parliament,choked as it is at this day with accummulat- ed arrears of work. (Applause.) I must apologise for having occupied more than a fair share of the time of this debate—one of the mcst interesting that I have ever had the pleasure of atendine.,. (Applause.) Mr. Hall briefly replied to the various criticisms and the debate, ceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>SPECIAL MEETING OF THE RUNCORN</head>
        <p>SPECIAL MEETING OF THE RUNCORN</p>
        <p>CO lIMISSIONE 1? S.</p>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL BILL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning a special meeting of the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners took place in the Town Hall;Runcorn,for the purpose of receiving a report of the Parliamentary Committee in respect to the Manchester Ship Canal Bill, 1894, and, if necessary, to petition Parliament against the Rill. The meeting was presided over by Mr. S. Taylor, the fol- lowing gentlemen being also present :—Messrs. Grice, Hankinson, Bisbrown, Itnison, and Wiswall ; Mr. F. A. Lake, law clerk ; and Mr. E. Marshall, secretary. Mr. Lake produced the correspondence which had passed between himself and the local agents of the Ship Canal, from winch it appeared that he had asked the Company's solicitors to withiral,v the clause from the Bill which gave them the power to supply water to vessels in the canal, thus infringing the rights of the Board. In the event of their objection to withdraw the clause, he had asked the solicitors to submit one for the Board's protection. Thee solicitors wrote back stating that they- would advise their clients to accept a clause which in his (Mr. Lake's) opinion would Meet the re- quirements of the Commissioners. Should such a clause not be inserted they could petition Parliament, but un- der present circumstances he thought such a petition was unnecessary. The Secretary said that the Parliamentary and General Purposes Committee had reported in the following. t,rms " We beg to report that subject to the Manchester Ship Canal Company the following clause in .the Manchester Bill, 1894, we see no necessity to petition Parliament against such Bill at present." The clause ran as follows :—" Man- chester Ship Canal clause for the protection of the Run- corn Improvement Commissioners.—For the protection of the Improvement Commissioners of Runcorn, as an Urban Sanitary Authority for the district of Runcorn, in the city of Chester in this section called the Commis- sioners), the following provision shall have effect unless otherwise agreed on in writing between the Commis- sionera and the Company, that is to say: nothing con- tained in this Act shall authorise or empower the Com- pany to supply wat,r to vessels in any port of the said harbour and port which is within the district of the water supply of the Commissioners as defined by the Runcern, Weston and II dton Waterworks Act of 1865 ; the Runcorn, Weston and Halton,estimate (capital) Act, 1870, and the Ruacorn Commissioners Act, 1893. n The Chairman moved that the recommendation of the Parliamentary Committee be adopted.—This proposition was seconded by Mr. Bisbrown and carried.</p>
        <p>How GLOVES ARE MADE.—Under the title of " The Glove of the Period, and how it is made," there has been published a most interesting pamphlet, by Mr. Henry Miles, the celebrated glove manufacturer, of Church-st., Liverpool: In the trade, the author's name is well- known throughout -Lancashire, and he is reputed to be one of the most enterprising of business men, in his en- deavours to discover specialities which may win the patronage of the public, and in making additions to that class of gloves and drapery,which, in manufacture, have become a fine art. It was with the object of pro- secuting this laudable purpose, that Mr. Miles travelled the continent, and inspecten the manufactories of different nations. One of the results is this interesting brochif,.d. written in a racy entertaining style. It con- tains about 40 pages, and is profusely :illustrated with photographic sketches,and appears to be one of the mot valuable, of the many publications, which have been printid on the subject. When a tradeshaan goes to the trouble and expense which Mr. Miles has gone to, of seeking out improvements and novelties, it is an inevit- able result that the contents of his emporium will lack nothing in quality and originality.</p>
        <p>OFFER OF A CuP TO THI4 LANCASHIRE AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY. At the quarterly meeting of the Council of the Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society, held on Monday at the Bolton Town Hall, an off,r of a 50-guinea challenge cup by • Mr. Gilbert Greenall, was accepted with thanks.</p>
        <p>BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF THE WARRINGTON BRANCH.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, a meeting of the Warring- ton branch of the British Women's Temperance Association was held in the Co-operative Ball, Sankey-street, in commemoration of the ninetieth birthday of General Neal Dow, the author of the celebrated Maine Law for the prohibition of the sale of intoxicating liquors. Ihere was a fair atten- dance. Tne chair was occupied by Miss Emily Bennett. There were also on the platform Mrs. Gunnelk,Mrs. John. Yonge, Miss Bessie Dutton, the Misses Bradbury, Miss Gunton, and Mrs. Stockferd of Manchester. After the proceedings had been opened by the singing of a hymn, Miss Bennett said that she hoped that the meeting would be more of a sympathetic than critical character. She supposed that a person's first speech was called the maiden speech, and if that was the case, that was her maiden speech. The Federation in Warrington was formed not very long ago. She attended the first meeting which was held in Bold-street schoolroom. Al that time, the was very careless about such matters, but the earnest words of the speakers that afternoon had in- duced her to become a member. The president' of the Association Lady Henry Somerset, set them all a splen- did example, and, as the good old town of Warrington had a stigma resting upon it,it behoved everyone to make some effort to stem the tide of drunkenness in the town. They were celebrating the ninetieth birthday of General Neal Dow, the veteranlpioneer of temperance,-and who plight he called,the grand old man of temperance,and his example ought to stimulate the younger mien of to-day. She believed that teetotalism was gradually increasing throughout the country,and especially among the middle classes. (Applause.) They had been fortunate that even- ing in securing the services of Mrs. Stockford of Man- chester, and Miss Bessie Dutton who were always willing and ready to help forward every good work. (Applause.) She hoped that efforts would be made to get as many pledges as possible, as every pledge taken would help to strengthen their ranks.</p>
        <p>Stockford, of Manchester, next addressed the meeting and proposed the following resolution :—“That„ this meeting recognises that the greater part of the misery and crime of this country is in consequence of the present public house system and awaits with eager- ness, the passint, of the Local Liquor Control Bill by the Government, and begs the House of Commons to pass such Bill with all possible speed." She hastened to con- gratulale Miss Bennett upon having made her maidea speech. It was a most inspiring speech. (Hear, hear.) Intemperance, she believed to be one of the greatest hindrances to Christianity, and it was the duty of all to try to prevent the spread of drinking habits. Half a million persons were employed in the .drink traffic, and she was sorry to 5ay,200,000 of these were young women This was a deplorable fact. It was the duty of all to support candidates at the. general elections ;who would best represent their interests in Parliament, and women who had votes in municipal elections should go to the ,polling booths and vote, and not he ashamed of letting people know they were voters. Children should be taught to hate drink, and she also considered homes for inebriates necessary. For the latter, she believed that</p>
        <p>the brewers should be taxed for their maintenance (laugh- ter and applause.) The drink traffic ruined the body and soul. This was the case with thousands and to those who battled against it, there would return showers of blessings both directly and indirectly. Miss Bessie Dutton seconded the resolution, and wel- comed the presence of Miss Bennett, and offered her congratulations at having made such an excellent maiden speech. Miss Dutton also spoke of the good the Bennett family were doing in Warrington and the neigh- bourhood, and it was a pleasure to see Miss Bennett among them. With regard to the resolution,she believed that intemperance was one of the greatest evils they had to deal with. Referring to General Neal Dow, she said she must confess that she knew very little about hi m. His woi k was mostly done in America, and they had therefore not come into the same touch with him as with temperance reformers such as Father Matthews and others. Those men had without doubt, made the tern perance work easier than it would have been had they not lived. They of the present generation should try to teach the little ones to lead good lives,ed to teach them to avoid drink. They wanted help as they could not help themselves. They should always say " God helping me, I will do my duty." (Applause.) The resolution was put to the meeting and carr:ed unanimously. ' During the coarse of the meeting, Miss Gunton sang several songs in her usual good style. Miss Bradbury was the accompanist. A hearty vote of thanks was passed to the lady presi- dent and the speaker, and Miss Gunton, on the motion of Mrs. Gunnell, seconded by Mrs. Councillor Roberts. A collection was also taken in aid of the inebriate homes for women, after which the pr -needing terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>RUNCORN TOWNSHIP MEETING.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TOWNSHIP MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a meeting of ratepayers of the township of Runcorn was held in the Bethesda Lecture Hall. Mr. Edwin Imison was voted to the chair, and amongst those present were Dr.McDoug- all, and Messrs. Henry Light:burn, ‘Valter Jones, J. K. Diggs, Wm. Mellor, Wm. Sutton, Wm. Bell, D. Bisbrown, and Tho.Q. The Chairman read the notice convening the meeting, after which he stated that the receipts of the overseers for the year 18934 were :—Balance at the beginnine of the year, £33223. 11d. ; April rate, 10.1. in the £, £2,342 4s. 91. ; October rate, 9d. in the £, £2,101 193. 3d. ; Manchester Ship Canal Company deficiency, £l2O 164.10 d. ; voters list sold, 12s. 6 I. ; bank interest, £4 183. id. ; making a total of £5,402 14s 4d. Of this sum the following amounts had been expended 7.- -Justices' Clerk's fees, £26 103. 61. ; office rent, stationery, and assistant overseer's salary, £305 103. hi. ; reeistration of voters, £6O 93. 5,1. ; Burial Board, £120; School Board, £1,050 and Guardians, £3,173 ; total payments £4,735, which left a balance to the good of £667 43. 4d. On the motion of Dr. McDougall, seconded by Mr. Bisbrowy, Messrs. John Littler and John Diggs were appointed auditors )f the Burial Board's accounts for the ensuing year.—The day fixed upon for the production of the accounts to the auditors was the sth April, aed the date of the meeting to receive the auditor's report 19th . Mr. Diggs proposed, and Mr. 3ishrown seconded, the • nomination of. the undermentioned gentlemen as over- seers of the poor, to be submitted to the justices for selection :—Messrs. Edwin Imison, Samuel Taylor, Wm. Mellor, Richard Morris,Jabez Unsworth, W.H.Rogerson, Walter Collier, and John Savage. Dr. McDougall enquired if the rate known as the " cattle plague rate" was still being collected.—One of the - gentlemen present remarked in the nega- tive, and added that such a rate was never collected to his knowledge.—Dr. McDougall said it had often been collected as he had himself recovered it from his land- lord on more than one occasion. It was a rate which had given rise to many squabbles and much discussion. It was a rate peculiar to Cheshire, aad was most iniquitous. When first it was levied its term was for thirty years, and he (Dr. NicDougall) thoueht that that period had now expired. He would like to know if Mr. Sutton could enlighten him upon the subject.—Mr. Sutton did not remember such a rate. If it had been collected it would be through the county rate. Mr. Diggs proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman for his services.—Dr. McDougall in seconding the vote referred in terms of eulogy •to the manner in which Messrs. Taylor and Imison had performed their duties during the past six or seven years,and. he thought that as this was probably the last meeting of its kind that would be held seeing that the Local 6overnment Act would come into force towards the end of the year, it would not he out of place to review matters somewhat. He alluded to the present condition of trade which was not of the brightest description. Formerly the works used to be owned by a number of persons but now, they were in the hands ot a syndicate, and to a certain extent it depended upon the directors whether works in certain districts should or should not be kept going. During thelast five .or six years, through the construction of the Ship Canal, they had - had an increase of trade, but • now that the waterway was completed they had return- ed to their former position. The question remained as to whether the canal would be productive of great bene- fits to the town. There oueht to be an effort made to encourage other industries to come into the district,and this could be done by efficiently managing their public institutions. Ile was not a pessimist, but at the same time there was an absolute necessity for curtailing ex- penses to the utmost extant. He had known the town for 26 or 27 years during which period they had had some good men occupying the positions of overseers and , not the least efficient of them was their present Chairman's honoured father. (Hoar, hear.) He trusted that in the new condition of things -economy with efficiency would be exercised. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr % Imis-on. in acknowledging the vote remarked that assistant overseers had little to do with the spending of public money. The rate levied depended upon the pre- cepts they received from public bodies. It they were large then the rates was also large. The overseers had simply to see to the collection of the amount required. In conclusion Mr. Imison said he knew of Iva public officer who reflected more credit upon his office, or did his work more efficiently than their officer (Mr. Sutton) who sat at the end of the table (Hear, hear.) This terminated the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WYCLIFFE YOUNG PEOPLE'S SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE YOUNG PEOPLE'S SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>" Disestablishment and Disendowment or the State church "was the title of a paper read at the weekly meeting of the above named society on Monday evening. Owing to the illness of the essay- ist Mr. T. Brown, the paper was read by Miss E Brown. • There was only a fair attendance. Those who took part in the discussion were Messrs. J. Ball, C. Anderson, W. Travis, J. Podmore, jun. and the chairman (Mr. T. Hawthorn). A vote of thanks was passed at the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>, A tanner's labourer named Henry Aston, of 62, Dallam-lane, Warrington, committed euicide by hanging himself on Thursday night. He was 50 years of age, and for some time had been engaged at the Tanners-lane Tanning Company's works. A short time ago he was discharged and this seemed to have had a depressing effect upon him. On Thursday, so the police were informed, he went home and made a supper of three pints of beer and a meat pie. Some time after midnight his wife heard him get up and thinking he was going to sleep downstairs took no further notice until the next morning. Upon getting up, she went down the cellar to chop some chips, and there found the corpse of her husband suspended by a rope from a beam. She called in the assistance ot.a man named Geddes, who out the deceased down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN IT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN IT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>IST' AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>One of the most important local events looked forward to with special interest is the visit of Mr. J. T. Brunner, the popular Liberal member for the division, to Runcern for the purpose of laying the foundation stone of the proposed new Technical Instruction School. Active preparations have been in progress now for some time with a view to get- ting the site of the building ready to receive the stone on the 14th April. So far the work has ad- vanced with success and when the Surveyor visited the spot last week he calculated that the ground would be ready on the date mentioned and con- veyed this opinion to the members of the Techni- cal Instruction Committee on his return. Conse- quently Mr. Brunner was communicated with upon the subject and has replied to the Technical In- struction Committee expressing the pleas are it would give him to perform the necessary ceremony ef stone-laying. There is, therefore, every reason to believe that, providing no hitch occurs to mar the arrangements, we shall again have the pleasure of listening to a voice which has by this time grown quite familiar to almost every resident of Runcorn, and more especially to those who embrace his own political views.</p>
        <p>The rapidly increasing importance of education, and the growing anxiety of the public to possess a knowledge of almost every branch of learning, augurs well for the success of the Institute when it has once reached the completion stage. There is in Runcorn not much doubtful feeling that the Institute will be well patronized. .Certain it is that the provision of such a centre of learning will sup- ply a long felt want in Runcorn, a want which is found in few towns of the same size.</p>
        <p>The work of erecting a new Presbyterian church and schools for Runcorn is proceeding apace and, judging from the present stage it is probable that both the church and schools will be finished in the course of a couple of months or more. At the annual meeting of the members of St. John's Church some days ago, after the Pastor had sub- mitted a most encouraging report, he intimated that the ladies were actively engaged in preparing for a bazaar in aid of the building fund. It is to be hoped that their efforts will be crowned with suc- cess, as all efforts for the furtherance of a good object should be.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>WIDNES SCIIuOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SCIIuOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting was held in the Board Room of the Town Hall on Tuesday after- noon under the presidency of Mr. H. 11, ade Deacon, J.P., C.C. There wer€ also present the Revs. Dean Finegan and Father Clark: Dr. O'Keefe, and Messrs. J. W. Wareing, C. E. Barlow, S. Quinn, B. Brown, D. Lewis, C. Flynn and G. H. Danby (clerk).</p>
        <p>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee reportedthat there had been 13 ap- plications for free places, 20 prosecutions before the magistrates, and 19 convictions. Recommended that the salary of attendance office Grant be increased acc.srd- ing to the scale of the Board. On the !notion of Father Clarke and Major Wareing, the minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>SCIIOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCIIOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Recommended that the resignation sf Miss Stormont, assistant-mistress, Senior Girls' Department of the West Bank Board School be accepted. Recommended that the following teachers be appointed to the positions :—Miss Fallows, Certificated Assistant, Senior Girls' West Bank, £7O and Miss Naylor,Certificated As istaLt, Senior Girls, West Bank, £65. Recommended that the salaries of the following teacheas be increased each from £65 to 70:2 Miss Sarah Stout, Miss A. Turton and Miss M. Pilling. Recommended that the Board do join the Association of School Boards and that two members and the Clerk be appointed as representatives to attend the meetings. Recommended that a Committee be appointed to enquire and report upon the charges made by Mr. David Lewis, a member of the Board, against the management of the Board Schools.—ln moving the adoption of these minutes the Rev. Dean Finegan said after the charges made by Mr. Lewis at the last meeting of the Board, there was nothing left for the Corn tnittee to do but to ask for the appointment of a committee of inqUiry.—Mr. D. Lewis said he had no objection to a bona-fide inquiry hut he was surprised that the School Management Conlinittee had not themselves gone into the charges without asking for the appointment of a committee.—Dr. O'Keefe thought that the Committee in asking for the appointment of a committee of inquiry, had taken the proper course.— The minutes were adopted.—Dr. O'Keefe moved that the C,mmittee consist of the following members :—Major Wareing and Messrs. R. Shaw and S. Quinn.—This was seconded by Father Clark.—The Chairman in putting the motion said that it was a matter to be regretted but he thought that under the circumstances the enquiry was necessary.—The motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>SITES AND BUILDING COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>Recommended that the tcncler of Mr. C. J. Sutcliffe be accepted for the glazing of broken windows, &amp;c., in the various Board schools. Passed on the motion of Mr. S. Quinn and Father Clark.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Accounts amounting to £597 Bs. 31 were recom- mended for payment. It was also recommended that the tender of Mr. George Bfilamy accepted for print- ing. &amp;c.—The minutes were passed with the exception of the tender for printing, which was referred back to the Committee for re-consideration as it was thought there had been some mistake in some of tte tenders as there was such a vast difference between them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WIDNES POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Councillor W. W..Gossage and Alder-</p>
        <p>man H. S. Timmis.</p>
        <p>DRuNaENNEss.—Frederick fiig-g-i-n-s-o.n, 8, Castle:Areet; George 2nitalitus, Water-street ; and William Creighton Moss Bank-street, were fined for this offence. ROWDYISM.—Patriok Culvert, Nelson-street, was sum- moned for having caused a breach of the peace. It appears defendant is a cab driver and was in Victoria- road on Saturday night, and used abusive and threat- ening language to another 'cabby' named Worral. The magistrates said they were going to stop such rowdy conduct and ordered the prisoner to be bound over for six months, hims,tlf in £2O, and two sureties of £lO each or one month's imprisonment in default. THEFT OF A BARREL OF BRER.—Four men named Waiter Wilson, Thomas Brunt, Peter Walton, and Peter Houghton, all of Castle-st., Halton View, were charged with having stolen a six gallon barrel of beer, valued at 133. 61, the property of Messrs. Boddington and Co., brewers. They all pleaded not guilty. Richard Jones, a carter in the employ of the prosecutors, stated that on Friday evening, he was at the Horns Inn, Farnworth, where he stayed about an hour. After he had started for St. Helens he missed the barrel from the cart. Chas. Rathbune, of Lunt's Heath, stated th.t.‘, he saw defen- dants carrying the barrel in a sack through some fields of Nloorfield-road. P.C. Heaton proved apprehending the prisoners and finding the empty barrel in Walton's house.—Each of the defendants were fined 20s. or 14 days in default.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PETIY SESSIONS. TIIIIRSDAY.—Before Messrs. H. Wade Deacon and Coun- cillor W. W. Gossage. DRUNIKENNESS.—EIiztheth Spence, Maria Caine, Wm. Harvey and Martin O'Brien were each fined frr this offence, and Richard Mercer, Bold, was fined ss. and costs for having been drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cart. UNMTJZZLED Doos.—The following persons were each fined is. and costs for this offence :—William Farren, Runcorn; William Prescott, Farnworth ; John Carman, Davies-street; Walter B. Laytr, Cronton ; Arthur Beesley, Farnworth ; Charles Bill, Ann-street; and Thos. Winskill, Ditton. CHIMNEY FlRE.—William Leigh, 60, Dock-street, was fined is. and costs for having his chimney on fire. RIDING BICYCLE ON FOOTPATI4.—FOr this offence Walter Parkes of Marple Grove, was fined is. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M:C..A.</head>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT THE Y.M:C..A.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this society was held on Sunday and was largely attended. The address for the after- noon was delivered by Mr. F. R. Roberts, M.A., who spoke on "Social Customs versus Christian Prin- ciples," dealing with some of the labour problems of the present day. The chairman, Mr. James Horton also delivered a short address. Miss Ada Oxley was the soloist, and sang "Entreat me not to leave thee " from Ruth for one of her solos. Mr. Hickman was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF -- WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 11th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :—Births: Warrington, 42; Latchford, 5 ; t0ta1,47. Deaths: Warrington, 14; Latchford, 3 ;- total 17. The excess of births over deaths was 30. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 15.7 per 1,000, against 22.6 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 16 9 the corresponding week of last year. • The death-rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 1.8 per 1,000, against. 4.3 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and ' against nil the corresponding week of 1893. The ages of mortality were :-0 to 1,6; 1t05,4 ; 5 to 15, 2;15 to 25, 0;25 to 60, 1; 00 up ' 4; total, 17. The deaths included-2 from whoop- , ing cough ; 1 from tubercular; 2 from old age; 2 from premature birth ; 2 from brain disease ; : • efrom lung disease; 1 from drowning; 2 from debility. The following cases of " infectious dis- eases have been reported during the week :—Erysi- pelas 1; scarlet fever, 4; enteric 3 ; continued fever, 1. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 408 - Fah., and therefore 1.3 deg. colder than the preced- ing week. The maximum temperature 50 F., was recorded on the 12th inst., and the rnininaum temperature 25 'F., on the 17th inst. The rainfall measured -87 inch. The general direction was- N.W., the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1,400 miles.—JoHN H. GORNA.LL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-03-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>1 g t 1</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL CONCERT.—On Tuesday evening the ansual concert of the Penketh Football club was held in the, Wesleyan schoolroom. There was a very good attend- ance. The tea meeting this year was dispensed with a concert only being given,and the officers have spared no pains to make it a success. The proceedings were com- menced with a brilliantly rendered pianoforte solo by Mr. H. Galoway, and he was followed by Mr. A. Apple- ton with " Simon the Cellarar," in which he was a success. Miss Hobson sang " The Star of Bethlehem "- very nicely, and Miss L. Routledge "Ora Pro Nobis." Mr. George Parr was to the fore with two excellently rendered songs "Mary of Argyle," and "Mv Sweet- heart whpn a boy." Mr. W. H. Podmore with his twit, flute solos was a real treat, and the audience showed - their appreciation by awarding him a hearty encore. Miss G. Lewis the sister of the popular captain of the club, sang "By the Fountain," and "The Holy City," and the enthusiastic applause she received was her due, she having to respond to an encore. Mr. R N. Forster gave a violin solo, " The last Rose of Summer," and was heartily encored. A banjo solo by- Mr. Davies, of Widnes, also pleased the audience very much, as also did the trio, Messrs. Davies (hanjo), P. Kenyon (tam-: bourine), and Miss A. Kenyon (piano). The humoroue items were able sustained by Mr. F. Dooley,who amused the audience greatly with" I could do with a bit," and , "The hotel where we stay." He received a well merited encore. Mr. A. Dutton gave" Strolling round the town" and later on, " E dunno where 'e are." His rendition' of these was very good, and he had to respond to au encore. Mr. P. Kenyon was not so good in his rendering of his song "I'm in the chair," although he received am encore. Much credit is due to Mr. H. Galloway for his skilful accompaniment of the various artistes, and alse . to Mr. T. Beams and the indefatigaelesecretaries,Messri Whitfield and Bartlett.</p>
        <p>MARKETS.</p>
